     1	# Civil Transactions Law — نظام المعاملات المدنية
     2	
     3	<!-- Written from the Arabic text on laws.boe.gov.sa read on 2026-09-04. No Bureau of Experts English translation was available in the source set at the time of writing (boe-en/civil-transactions-en.txt absent); every English rendering below is the writer's own from the Arabic and is tagged [BOE — Arabic] only. -->
     4	
     5	## Instrument
     6	
     7	| Field | Value |
     8	|---|---|
     9	| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
    10	| Instrument | Civil Transactions Law / نظام المعاملات المدنية |
    11	| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/191 of 29/11/1444H (18 June 2023), approving Council of Ministers Resolution No. 820 of 24/11/1444H |
    12	| Issuing authority | Council of Ministers (the law is a general private-law code; no single supervising ministry). Disputes go to the general courts, or to the commercial courts where the Commercial Courts Law gives them jurisdiction |
    13	| Effective date | 2023-12-16 (Art. 721: in force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette; portal shows publication 1/12/1444H = 19 June 2023; 19 June 2023 + 180 days = 16 December 2023) |
    14	| Amendment history | none recorded on the portal (`<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 0 -->`; status line "ساري"). The Royal Decree itself repeals Arts. 212-217 of the Law of Civil Procedure and part of Art. 211, Art. 25 of the Code of Law Practice, and three definitions in the Law of Ethics of Research on Living Creatures, and amends Art. 16(3) of the Commercial Courts Law and the definition of "real property by allocation" in the Movable Assets Security Law and Art. 15(8) of the Documentation Law (Decree paras. 2-3) |
    15	| Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/655fdb42-8c96-422b-b8c4-b04f0095c94c/1 |
    16	| Official translation | none available in the source set on 2026-09-04. A Bureau of Experts English translation is understood to exist on the same portal `[model knowledge — verify]`; when obtained, English wording in this file should be conformed to it |
    17	| Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 |
    18	| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: read the portal header of the Arabic file (issue date 29/11/1444H = 18/06/2023, publication 1/12/1444H = 19/06/2023, status "ساري", zero articles flagged as amended, issuing instruments M/191 and CoM Resolution 820). No amending instrument is named anywhere in the text. The check covered only the portal page as fetched; no separate search of the Official Gazette (Umm al-Qura) was made |
    19	
    20	## Scope
    21	
    22	The Civil Transactions Law is Saudi Arabia's first codified general law of obligations and contracts (721 articles). It covers legal personality (natural and juristic), the sources of obligations (contract, unilateral act, harmful act/tort, unjust enrichment, statute), the effects and transfer of obligations, extinction including limitation of actions, the named contracts (sale, exchange, gift, loan, settlement, lease, loan for use, contracting/muqawala, agency, deposit, custodianship, partnership, mudaraba, guarantee), and real rights. It is the default law for every contract, including commercial contracts, "to the extent it does not conflict with the nature of the commercial transaction and where no special provision exists in the commercial laws" (Royal Decree para. 6).
    23	
    24	It does not cover employment contracts (Art. 479 refers to the Labor Law), insurance (Art. 607 refers to the insurance laws), company incorporation (Companies Law), bankruptcy (Art. 196 refers to the Bankruptcy Law), electronic signatures and records (Electronic Transactions Law; this law has no article on electronic contracting), or the formal registration and licensing requirements of other laws (Decree para. 4). It contains no interest-rate provision and no decennial (10-year) builder/engineer liability article; both points are addressed in the rows below. Where the text is silent, Art. 1 sends the reader to the 41 general maxims in Art. 720 and then to "the rulings derived from Islamic Sharia most suited to this Law".
    25	
    26	## Rules
    27	
    28	Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.
    29	
    30	| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
    31	|---|---|---|---|---|
    32	| Art. 1 | Hierarchy of sources: the Law's provisions apply to every matter they cover in wording or substance; failing that, the general maxims in the Final Provisions (Art. 720); failing that, the rulings derived from Islamic Sharia most suited to this Law. Special statutory provisions are not prejudiced. | تُطبق نصوص هذا النظام على جميع المسائل التي تناولتها في لفظها أو في فحواها، فإن لم يوجد نص يمكن تطبيقه طُبقت القواعد الكلية الواردة في الأحكام الختامية، فإن لم توجد قاعدة يمكن تطبيقها طُبقت الأحكام المستمدة من الشريعة الإسلامية الأكثرُ ملاءمة لهذا النظام. لا يخل تطبيق نصوص هذا النظام بالنصوص النظامية الخاصة. | Sharia is the residual source, not a free-standing override of the text: a court should start with the article, not with a fiqh opinion. Special laws (Commercial Courts Law, E-Commerce Law, Labor Law) prevail on their own subject. A reviewer should cite this article whenever a counterparty argues that a Sharia principle displaces a clear article. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    33	| Art. 2 | Periods and time limits in this Law are computed by the Hijri calendar. | تحسب المدد والمواعيد الواردة في هذا النظام بالتقويم الهجري. | All statutory periods in this file (10 years, 5 years, 1 year, 180 days, 90 days, 30 days) are Hijri. A Hijri year is about 354 days, so "10 years" is roughly 9 years 8.5 months Gregorian; the renewal-tracker and any limitation computation must convert. Contractual periods are whatever the contract says (parties may specify Gregorian). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    34	| Royal Decree M/191 para. 5 (transitional) | The Law applies to all facts that occurred before it came into force, except (a) where a statutory provision or a judicial principle relating to the fact conflicts with the Law and a party relies on it, and (b) where the rule concerns a limitation period that began to run before the Law came into force. | خامساً: تسري أحكام نظام المعاملات المدنية على جميع الوقائع التي حدثت قبل العمل به، وذلك باستثناء ما يأتي: إذا وُجد نص نظامي أو مبدأ قضائي يتعلق بالواقعة بما يخالف أحكام هذا النظام وتمسك به أحد الأطراف. إذا كان الحكم يتعلق بمدة لمرور الزمن المانع من سماع الدعوى بدأ سريانها قبل العمل بهذا النظام. | Pre-16 Dec 2023 contracts are governed by this Law unless a party pleads a contrary settled judicial principle, so legacy contracts in a diligence set should be reviewed against these rows, with a flag that the counterparty may invoke pre-Law practice (e.g. the older reluctance to award lost profit) `[model knowledge — verify]`. This is a decree paragraph, not a numbered article: cite it as "Royal Decree M/191, para. Fifthly". | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    35	| Royal Decree M/191 para. 6 | The Law (and para. 5) applies to commercial transactions to the extent this does not conflict with the nature of the commercial transaction, and only where no special provision exists in the commercial laws. | سادساً: تسري أحكام نظام المعاملات المدنية وما ورد في البند (خامساً) من هذا المرسوم على المعاملات التجارية؛ بما لا يخالف طبيعة المعاملة التجارية، وذلك فيما لم يرد به نص خاص في الأنظمة التجارية. | This is why the Law is the jurisdiction baseline for vendor, SaaS and NDA reviews: B2B contracts are "commercial transactions" and the Law fills every gap the commercial laws leave. The "nature of the commercial transaction" carve-out is undefined; expect it to be argued around consumer-protective rules such as adhesion-contract review. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    36	| Art. 720 | Forty-one general maxims apply so far as they do not conflict with statutory provisions, e.g. rule 2 "in contracts, regard is to intentions and meanings, not to words and forms"; rule 3 "custom is authoritative"; rule 10 "the presumption in contracts and conditions is validity and bindingness"; rule 13 "no statement is attributed to one who is silent, but silence where a statement is needed is a statement"; rule 16 "harm is to be removed"; rule 33 "the hand that took is liable until it returns"; rule 41 "ignorance of the rule is no excuse". | العبرة في العقود بالمقاصد والمعاني لا بالألفاظ والمباني … العادة مُحَكَّمة … الأصل في العقود والشروط الصحة واللزوم … لا ينسب إلى ساكت قول، لكن السكوت في معرض الحاجة إلى البيان بيان … الضرر يزال … على اليد ما أخذت حتى تؤديه … الجهل بالحكم ليس عذرًا. | Rule 10 is the answer to "is this unusual clause enforceable in KSA": the default is yes unless an article or public order says otherwise. Rule 2 must be read with Art. 104(1) (clear wording is not departed from) — the maxim governs only where wording is unclear. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    37	| Arts. 17-18, 47-48, 87-93 (persons, capacity, representation; note only) | Juristic persons include the State, public bodies, endowments (awqaf), companies granted personality by law, and civil/cooperative associations. A juristic person has an independent estate, capacity within the limits of its constitutive instrument or the law, standing to sue, a domicile at its principal management centre (a branch may be its domicile for that branch's activity), and must have a representative who expresses its will. Every person has capacity unless deprived or limited by statute. Minors (even discerning), the insane and the feeble-minded are interdicted by law; the prodigal and the heedless by court order. Contracting through a representative is valid unless the law provides otherwise; the representative may not exceed the limits of the instrument creating the authority (contract, judgment or statute); acts within authority in the principal's name bind the principal; an undisclosed representative binds the principal only if the third party ought to have known of the representation or was indifferent; a representative may not contract with himself without authorisation, subject to the principal's ratification. | يكون للشخص ذي الصفة الاعتبارية: ذمةٌ ماليةٌ مستقلة. أهليةٌ في الحدود التي يعينها سند إنشائه أو التي تقررها النصوص النظامية. حق التقاضي. موطنٌ مستقلٌ … يجب أن يكون للشخص ذي الصفة الاعتبارية من يمثله ويعبر عن إرادته. … كل شخصٍ أهلٌ للتصرف؛ ما لم يكن عديم الأهلية أو ناقصها بمقتضى نص نظامي. … إذا تعاقد النائب في حدود نيابته باسم الأصيل فإن ما ينشأ عن العقد من حقوقٍ والتزاماتٍ يُضاف إلى الأصيل … لا يجوز للنائب أن يتعاقد مع نفسه بمقتضى نيابته دون أن يكون مأذونًا له بذلك. | Corporate authority to sign is bounded by the articles of association and the Companies Law; a reviewer checks the signatory's authority (commercial register extract, board resolution) rather than relying on apparent authority. See Arts. 87-93 on representation. Age of majority and guardianship are in other laws (Art. 54). For commercial reviews capacity questions arise only for individual counterparties; corporate capacity is Art. 18. Corporate signatories acting beyond the powers in the articles or a board delegation do not bind the company under this text; the Companies Law's rules on managers' authority and the commercial-register publicity of restrictions determine what third parties can rely on `[model knowledge — verify]`. Self-dealing by a director/agent needs express authorisation or ratification. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    38	| Arts. 30-31, 33 | The contract chapter applies to named and unnamed contracts, without prejudice to laws governing contracts of a special nature. A contract is formed by the linking of offer and acceptance to produce a legal effect, subject to any form the law prescribes. Offer and acceptance may be made by anything indicating intent: words, writing, understood gesture or conduct (mu'ata), express or implied, unless law, agreement or the nature of the transaction requires otherwise. | تطبق الأحكام الواردة في هذا الفصل على العقود المسماة وغير المسماة … ينشأ العقد بارتباط الإيجاب بالقبول لإحداث أثر نظامي، مع مراعاة ما تقرره النصوص النظامية من أوضاعٍ معيّنةٍ لانعقاد العقد. … يكون الإيجاب والقبول بكل ما يدل على الإرادة. يجوز أن يكون التعبير عن الإرادة باللفظ أو بالكتابة أو بالإشارة المفهومة أو بالمعاطاة، وأن يكون صريحًا أو ضمنيًّا. | No general writing requirement; SaaS click-through, order forms and exchanges of email can form a contract. Formal requirements come from other laws (e.g. real-estate registration, notarisation). The Law has no article on electronic contracting; the validity of electronic records and signatures is governed by the Electronic Transactions Law (Royal Decree M/18 of 1428H) `[model knowledge — verify]`. An "agreement must be in writing to be amended" clause is enforceable as an agreed form requirement under this article's "unless the agreement requires otherwise". | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    39	| Arts. 35-38 | An offeror may withdraw the offer before acceptance unless the offer carries a fixed period; withdrawal of an open offer must be communicated or the offeror is liable for loss (not expected profit). An offer lapses on withdrawal, rejection (any modification in the acceptance is a rejection and a new offer), death or incapacity of either party before acceptance even if the offer had a fixed period, or expiry of the period. Acceptance after lapse is a new offer. Silence is not acceptance unless agreed or a presumption indicates it; silence is acceptance where there is a prior course of dealing to which the offer relates, or the offer is purely for the offeree's benefit. Between parties present (in one place or via direct communication means) the contract is concluded at the time and place acceptance is issued; between absent parties, when and where the offeror learns of the acceptance; both subject to contrary agreement. | للموجب أن يعدل عن الإيجاب قبل صدور القبول؛ ما لم تكن للإيجاب مدةٌ معينة … وأي تعديل يتضمنه القبول يعد رفضًا يتضمن إيجابًا جديدًا. … لا يعد سكوت من وجه إليه الإيجاب قبولًا إلا إذا كان هناك اتفاقٌ أو وجدت قرينة تدل على ذلك. يعد السكوت قبولًا إذا كان هناك تعاملٌ سابقٌ بين المتعاقدين واتصل الإيجاب بهذا التعامل. … إذا كان المتعاقدان حاضرين في مكانٍ واحدٍ أو في مكانين مختلفين عبر وسائل الاتصال المباشرة؛ عُدَّ العقد قد تمَّ في الزمان والمكان اللذين صدر فيهما القبول … إذا كان المتعاقدان غائبين؛ عُدَّ العقد قد تمَّ في الزمان والمكان اللذين علم فيهما الموجب بالقبول. | Quotations with a validity period are irrevocable for that period. A counter-marked-up order form is a counter-offer, so "battle of the forms" resolves by last-shot: the last unmodified acceptance forms the contract. Vendor "deemed acceptance unless you object within N days" clauses in change orders or renewal notices are enforceable if the contract so agrees (first sentence) and, absent agreement, may bind repeat customers under the course-of-dealing limb. Email/portal contracting is "absent parties": the contract forms when the offeror learns of acceptance (receipt/knowledge theory), not on dispatch. Fix the place of contracting expressly; it drives the default place of performance (Art. 277). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    40	| Arts. 40, 96 | In an adhesion contract acceptance is mere submission to fixed terms the offeror does not allow to be negotiated. If an adhesion contract contains abusive (ta'assufi) terms the court may amend them or relieve the adhering party of them as justice requires; any contrary agreement is void. | إذا تمَّ العقد بطريق الإذعان وتضمن شروطًا تعسفيةً؛ فللمحكمة أن تعدلها أو تعفي الطرف المـُذْعِن منها وفقًا لما تقتضيه العدالة. ويقع باطلًا كل اتفاق على خلاف ذلك. | Non-negotiable SaaS terms of service and standard vendor terms are adhesion contracts on the Art. 40 definition. A Saudi customer can ask a court to rewrite abusive terms (broad exclusions, unilateral price changes) and the clause cannot opt out. Whether courts will apply this to B2B contracts between sophisticated parties, given the Decree para. 6 "nature of commercial transaction" carve-out, is unsettled `[model knowledge — verify]`. Ambiguities are also read against the drafter (Art. 104(3)). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    41	| Art. 41 | Negotiating a contract creates no duty to conclude it, but a party who negotiates or breaks off negotiations in bad faith is liable for the other party's loss, excluding expected profit from the contract. Bad faith includes lack of seriousness and deliberate failure to disclose a material fact affecting the contract. | يكون من يتفاوض أو يُنهي التفاوض بسوء نية مسؤولًا عن الضرر الذي أصاب الطرف الآخر، ولا يشمل ذلك التَّعويض عمَّا فاته من كسب متوقع … يُعدُّ من سوء النية عدم الجدية في التفاوض، أو تعمد عدم الإدلاء ببيانٍ جوهريٍّ مؤثرٍ في العقد. | Term sheets and LOIs marked non-binding are non-binding, but walking away to use a competing bid, or concealing a material fact during RFP responses, exposes the party to reliance damages. Non-binding LOIs should still state the parties' freedom to withdraw. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    42	| Arts. 42-44 | Agreement on the essential matters with deferral of non-essential ones is a concluded contract; the court fixes unresolved non-essential terms by law, nature of the transaction and custom, unless the parties tied formation to later agreement. A promise to contract binds only if the essential terms and the period for concluding are fixed and the formalities of the intended contract are met; a judgment then stands in place of the contract. Payment of earnest money (arboun) on contracting means the payer alone may withdraw from the contract, forfeiting the arboun; if no withdrawal period is fixed the court sets it by custom; silence or non-performance within the period is withdrawal. All subject to contrary agreement. | إذا اتفق المتعاقدان على المسائل الجوهرية في العقد وعلى إرجاء الاتفاق على المسائل غير الجوهرية؛ كان ذلك كافيًا لاعتبار القبول مطابقًا للإيجاب … لا يكون الوعد … ملزمًا إلا إذا عُيّنت المسائل الجوهرية في العقد المراد إبرامه، والمدة التي يجب إبرامه فيها. … دفع العربون عند إبرام العقد يفيد أن لدافع العربون وحده الحق في العدول عن العقد، وليس له إذا عدل أن يسترد مبلغ العربون، وذلك كله ما لم يتفق على خلافه. | "Subject to contract" language matters: without it, an agreed scope and price with open boilerplate is a binding contract. Options and rights of first refusal need essential terms and a deadline to be enforceable as promises to contract. A "deposit" paid at signing is by default an option fee giving the payer a walk-away right. If a deposit is meant as part-payment with no withdrawal right, say so expressly. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    43	| Arts. 45-46 | A framework agreement fixes the basic terms governing contracts the parties later conclude under it and forms part of those contracts. Express or implied reference to a standard document, rules or any other document makes it part of the contract. | الاتفاق الإطاري عقدٌ يُحدد المتعاقدان بمقتضاه البنود الأساسية التي تخضع لها العقود التي ينشئها المتعاقدان بينهما … إذا أحال المتعاقدان صراحةً أو ضمنًا في العقد إلى أحكام وثيقةٍ نموذجيَّةٍ أو قواعد محددة أو أي وثيقة أخرى عُدَّت جزءًا من العقد. | MSA + order-form structures are expressly recognised. Incorporation by reference (URLs to online terms, SLAs, AUPs, DPAs) works, including implied reference; the reviewer should still capture the referenced version because the referenced document is part of the contract. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    44	| Arts. 57-67 (mistake, fraud, duress) | A party may seek annulment for a fundamental mistake without which it would not have contracted (as to the object's quality, the other party's identity or attributes, or the legal rule), but only if the other party shared, knew of, or could easily have detected the mistake. A mere clerical or arithmetical error does not affect the contract. A mistaken party may not rely on the mistake contrary to good faith and is bound by the contract it intended if the other party offers to perform it. Fraud (taghrir) is deceiving the other party by fraudulent means into a contract it would not otherwise have made; deliberate silence to conceal a matter the other party would not have contracted knowing of it is fraud. The victim may seek annulment if the fraud concerned a fundamental matter. Third-party fraud allows annulment only if the contracting party knew or ought to have known of it. Duress is an unlawful threat, physical or moral, that frightens a person into acting; it requires a threat of serious imminent harm to the person, honour or property of the coerced or another, without which the contract would not have been made; the coerced party may seek annulment if the duress came from the other party, or from a third party the other party knew or should have known of. | للمتعاقد طلب إبطال العقد إذا وقع في غلط جوهري لولاه لم يرض بالعقد … لا يُعتد بغلط المتعاقد إلا إذا كان المتعاقد الآخر قد وقع معه في الغلط نفسه أو علم بوقوعه فيه أو كان من السهل عليه أن يتبينه. لا يؤثر في العقد مجرد الغلط المادي في الحساب أو الكتابة. … التغرير أن يخدع أحد المتعاقدين الآخر بطرق احتياليَّةٍ … يعد تغريرًا تعمد السكوت لإخفاء أمرٍ لم يكن المغرر به ليبرم العقد لو علم به. … الإكراه تهديد شخصٍ دون حقٍّ بوسيلةٍ ماديَّةٍ أو معنويَّةٍ تُخيفه فتحمله على التصرف … للمُكرَه طلب إبطال العقد إذا صدر الإكراه من المتعاقد الآخر. | Pricing typos in an order form are corrected, not a ground to escape the deal. "Mistake as to the legal rule" is expressly a ground, which is unusual and gives a party who misunderstood a regulatory constraint an argument. Non-disclosure is actionable fraud, so "no reliance on representations outside this agreement" clauses do not protect a party that deliberately concealed a material fact. Read with Art. 173 (no exclusion of liability for ghish). Commercial pressure (threat to exercise a lawful termination right) is not "without right" and is not duress. Threats to withhold performance already owed could be. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    45	| Arts. 68-69 | Where a party exploits the other's manifest weakness or pressing need to obtain a contract causing it gross unfairness (ghabn), the court may on the victim's application reduce its obligations, increase the other's, or annul the contract; the action must be brought within 180 days of contracting. Ghabn is an excess or shortfall in the consideration beyond what is customary, determined by custom; ghabn alone does not permit annulment except for incapacitated persons' property or where statute so provides, and never in auctions; the other party may avert annulment by offering what the court considers sufficient to remove the ghabn. | إذا استغل أحد المتعاقدين ضعفًا ظاهرًا أو حاجة ملحة في المتعاقد الآخر، لإبرام عقدٍ لحقه منه غبنٌ، فللمحكمة … أن تنقص من التزاماته أو تزيد من التزامات المتعاقد الآخر أو تبطل العقد، ويجب أن ترفع الدعوى بذلك خلال (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ التعاقد. | An unfavourable price by itself is not a ground to reopen a B2B contract; exploitation of a pressing need (e.g. an emergency supply at an extortionate price) is, but the 180-day (Hijri) window is short. Diligence: check whether any contract signed under distress is within the window. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    46	| Arts. 72, 74-76 | The object must be possible, not contrary to public order, and determined or determinable, or the contract is void. The contract may contain any term the parties agree that meets those conditions; a void term is severed alone, but a party may seek annulment of the whole contract if it would not have contracted without the term. A contract whose motivating cause is unlawful, if expressed or apparent from circumstances, is void; an unstated cause is presumed lawful. | يجوز أن يتضمن العقد أي شرطٍ يرتضيه المتعاقدان إذا توفرت فيه الشروط … إذا تضمن العقد شرطًا باطلًا بطل الشرط وحده، وللمتعاقد طلب إبطال العقد إذا تبيّن أنه ما كان ليرضى بالعقد دون ذلك الشرط. | Freedom of contract is the rule; "public order" (النظام العام) is the limit and is undefined. In practice Sharia prohibitions (riba, gharar/excessive uncertainty, gambling) are treated as public order `[model knowledge — verify]`. Severability clauses restate the statutory default; they cannot remove the party's right to annul if the void term was essential. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    47	| Arts. 79, 81, 84 | An annulment action is not heard after 1 year from knowledge of the ground (for incapacity or duress, 1 year from capacity being complete or duress ceasing) and in any event after 10 years from contracting (except incapacity). Nullity (butlan) may be invoked by any interested person and raised by the court of its own motion, cannot be cured by ratification, and the action is barred after 10 years from contracting but nullity may be pleaded as a defence at any time. Partial nullity or voidability affects only that part unless the party would not have contracted without it. | لا تسمع دعوى إبطال العقد إذا انقضت (سنة) من تاريخ العلم بسبب الإبطال … لا تسمع دعوى البطلان إذا انقضت (عشر) سنوات من تاريخ التعاقد، ولكن لكل ذي مصلحة أن يدفع ببطلان العقد في أي وقت. إذا كان العقد في جزء منه باطلًا أو يجوز إبطاله؛ يبطل ذلك الجزء فقط. | Voidable (mistake, fraud, duress) claims go stale fast: 1 Hijri year from discovery. Void (unlawful object/cause) contracts can be attacked for 10 years and defended against indefinitely. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    48	| Art. 94 | A validly concluded contract may not be revoked or amended except by agreement or by statute. Rights created by the contract vest on conclusion without depending on delivery unless statute provides otherwise, and the parties must perform what the contract imposes. | إذا تم العقد صحيحًا لم يجز نقضه أو تعديله إلا بالاتفاق أو بمقتضى نص نظامي. تثبت الحقوق التي يُنشئها العقد فور انعقاده … ويجب على المتعاقدين الوفاء بما أوجبه العقد عليهما. | Pacta sunt servanda in statutory form. Unilateral vendor rights to change terms, prices or SLAs are enforceable only because the contract itself grants them (the "by agreement" limb); the reviewer should treat a unilateral-modification clause as a negotiated concession, and note the Art. 96 adhesion-contract risk if the clause is abusive. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    49	| Art. 95 | A contract must be performed according to its contents and in a manner consistent with good faith. It binds the party not only to what it states but to what follows from it under statute, custom and the nature of the contract. | يجب تنفيذ العقد طبقًا لما اشتمل عليه وبطريقة تتفق مع ما يوجبه حسن النية. لا يقتصر العقد على إلزام المتعاقد بما ورد فيه، ولكن يشمل ما هو من مستلزماته وفقًا لما تقضي به النصوص النظامية والعرف وطبيعة العقد. | Implied terms exist by statute. An "entire agreement" clause does not exclude terms implied by custom and the nature of the contract; a cooperation duty and a duty not to frustrate the other party's performance will be implied. Good faith is a performance standard, not a free-standing cause of action for pre-contract conduct (that is Art. 41). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    50	| Art. 97 | Hardship: where general exceptional circumstances that could not be foreseen at contracting make performance onerous for the debtor so as to threaten it with a grave loss, the debtor may without undue delay invite the other party to negotiate; the request does not entitle the debtor to withhold performance; if no agreement is reached within a reasonable period the court may, weighing both parties' interests, reduce the onerous obligation to a reasonable level. Any agreement contrary to this article is void. | إذا طرأت ظروف استثنائيةٌ عامَّةٌ لم يكن في الوسع توقعها وقت التعاقد وترتب على حدوثها أن يصير تنفيذ الالتزام التعاقدي مرهقًا للمدين بحيث يهدده بخسارةٍ فادحةٍ؛ فله -دون تأخرٍ غير مسوغ- دعوة الطرف الآخر للتفاوض. طلب التفاوض لا يخول المدين الامتناع عن تنفيذ الالتزام. إذا لم يُتوصل إلى اتِّفاقٍ خلال مدَّةٍ معقولةٍ؛ فللمحكمة تبعًا للظروف وبعد الموازنة بين مصلحة الطرفين أن ترد الالتزام المرهق إلى الحد المعقول. يقع باطلًا كل اتفاق على خلاف أحكام هذه المادة. | Mandatory: a clause saying "no adjustment for change in circumstances", or a fixed-price clause purporting to exclude judicial rebalancing, is void to that extent. The trigger is narrow (general, unforeseeable, threatening a grave loss, not mere loss of profit), the debtor must keep performing, and negotiation is a precondition. A well-drafted change-in-law / price-adjustment clause is the practical way to control the outcome, since the court "reduces" the obligation rather than terminating. For muqawala there is a specific rebalancing article (Art. 471(3)). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    51	| Arts. 98-99 | The contract's effect extends to the parties and their universal successors (subject to inheritance rules) unless the contract, the nature of the transaction or statute shows otherwise; personal rights and obligations attached to a thing pass to a particular successor with the thing if they are among its requisites and the successor knew of them. A contract imposes no obligation on third parties but may confer a right on them. | ينصرف أثر العقد إلى المتعاقدين والخلف العام … لا يرتب العقد التزامًا في ذمة الغير، ولكن يجوز أن يكسبه حقًّا. | A share sale (change of control) does not by itself transfer or terminate contracts: the company remains the party. An asset sale does not carry contracts over without the counterparty's consent (Art. 255), except obligations that are "requisites" of the transferred asset and known to the buyer. Change-of-control clauses are therefore a matter of drafting, not statute. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    52	| Arts. 101-102 | A person may stipulate in its own name for the benefit of a third party if it has a material or moral interest; the beneficiary acquires a direct right against the promisor (unless agreed otherwise), and the promisor may raise defences arising from the contract. The stipulator (not its creditors or heirs) may revoke or substitute the beneficiary unless the beneficiary has notified acceptance or revocation would harm the promisor. | يترتب على الاشتراط لمصلحة الغير أن يَكسِبَ الغير حقًّا مباشرًا تجاه المتعهد يستطيع أن يطالبه بوفائه ما لم يُتفق على خلاف ذلك. | Affiliate beneficiary clauses (customer affiliates may enforce the MSA) and third-party indemnitee provisions are enforceable. A "no third-party beneficiaries" clause is the contrary agreement the article contemplates. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    53	| Art. 104 | If the wording of the contract is clear, it is not departed from on the pretext of interpreting it to find the parties' intention. Where interpretation is needed, the common intention is sought beyond the literal meaning, guided by custom, the circumstances, the nature of the transaction, the parties' course of dealing and the honesty and trust that should prevail; terms are construed together. Doubt is construed in favour of the party bearing the obligation or condition, and in adhesion contracts in favour of the adhering party. | إذا كانت عبارة العقد واضحةً فلا يُعدل عن مدلولها بحجة تفسيرها بحثًا عن إرادة المتعاقدين … يُفسر الشك لمصلحة من يتحمل عبء الالتزام أو الشرط، ويُفسر في عقود الإذعان لمصلحة الطرف المـُذْعِن. | Precision in drafting is rewarded: a clear exclusion, cap or termination trigger will be applied as written. Ambiguity cuts against the obligee (the party seeking to enforce) and against the drafter of standard terms. An Arabic/English "prevailing language" clause is respected as part of the clear wording; without one, the court will read the Arabic version `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    54	| Arts. 105-106 | The parties may mutually rescind (iqala) the contract in whole or part on the contract's terms. A contract may be concluded with an option to withdraw (khiyar al-shart): the option holder may withdraw within the fixed period on notice to the other party, which counts as termination; if no period is fixed the court sets it by custom; the option lapses by express or implied waiver or expiry of the period. | يجوز التعاقد بشرط الخيار في العدول عن العقد، ولمن له الخيار حق العدول خلال المدة المعينة بشرط إعلام المتعاقد الآخر، فإذا عدل من له الخيار عُدَّ ذلك فسخًا للعقد. | Termination-for-convenience rights and trial/cooling-off periods are statutorily recognised options and need no cause; give them a defined period and a notice mechanism or the court will fix the period. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    55	| Art. 107 | In bilateral contracts, if a party does not perform, the other may, after serving notice (i'thar) on the defaulting party, demand performance or termination, with compensation in either case where warranted; the court may refuse termination if the unperformed part is of little importance relative to the obligation. | في العقود الملزمة للجانبين، إذا لم يوف أحد المتعاقدين بالتزامه، فللمتعاقد الآخر بعد إعذاره المتعاقد المخل أن يطلب تنفيذ العقد أو فسخه، مع التعويض في الحالتين إن كان له مقتض، وللمحكمة أن ترفض طلب الفسخ إذا كان الجزء الذي لم يوف به المخل قليل الأهمية بالنسبة إلى الالتزام. | Default termination is judicial: without a termination clause, the innocent party must serve notice and then ask the court. There is no statutory materiality threshold beyond "little importance", so the contract should define material breach and cure periods. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    56	| Art. 108 | The parties may agree that the creditor may terminate the contract on the debtor's breach without a court judgment; such agreement does not dispense with the notice (i'thar) unless the parties expressly agree to dispense with it. | يجوز الاتفاق على أن يكون للدائن حق فسخ العقد عند إخلال المدين بالتزاماته دون حاجة إلى حكم قضائي، ولا يُعفي هذا الاتفاق من الإعذار إلا إذا اتفق المتعاقدان صراحةً على الإعفاء منه. | Contractual termination clauses work, but a termination "immediately on breach without notice" is effective only if the clause expressly waives i'thar. Draft: "without need for a court order or any notice, formal demand or i'thar". A cure-period notice satisfies i'thar (Art. 177). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    57	| Art. 109 | Contracts of exchange are deemed concluded on the basis that the object is free of defects, except those custom tolerates; a defect custom does not tolerate is a breach of obligation. | تُعدُّ عقود المعاوضات منعقدةً على أساس سلامة محل العقد من العيوب إلا ما جرى العرف على التسامح فيه. | A statutory implied quality warranty applies to services and software as well as goods. "AS IS" disclaimers work only within the limits of Arts. 173 and 343 (no exclusion for fraud/gross fault or deliberately concealed defects). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    58	| Arts. 110, 174, 292-294 (impossibility, force majeure risk, release) | In bilateral contracts, if performance becomes impossible for a cause not attributable to the debtor, its obligation and the counter-obligation are extinguished and the contract is dissolved automatically. Partial or temporary impossibility (in continuing contracts) extinguishes only the affected part; the creditor may seek termination, which the court may refuse if the impossible part is of little importance. The parties may agree that the debtor bears the risk of force majeure. Release (ibra') by the creditor extinguishes the obligation without the debtor's acceptance, though the debtor may reject it; release is valid only of an existing debt, even if not yet due; it is subject to the substantive rules on gifts and needs no particular form. An obligation is extinguished if the debtor proves performance became impossible for a cause beyond its control, and the counter-obligation with it. | إذا أصبح تنفيذ الالتزام مستحيلًا بسببٍ لا يد للمدين فيه، انقضى التزامه والالتزام المقابل له، وانفسخ العقد من تلقاء نفسه. … يجوز الاتفاق على أن يتحمل المدين تبعة القوة القاهرة. … ينقضي التزام المدين إذا أبرأه الدائن، ولا يتوقف الإبراء على قبول المدين … ينقضي التزام المدين إذا أثبت استحالة التنفيذ عليه بسبب لا يد له فيه. | Statutory force majeure effect: automatic dissolution on total impossibility, suspension on temporary impossibility. The threshold is impossibility, not impracticability (that is Art. 97 hardship). A force majeure clause is therefore mainly useful to (a) define events, (b) add notice/mitigation duties, (c) fix suspension and long-stop termination mechanics, and (d) allocate the risk (Art. 174). Force majeure protection is a default, not mandatory: a clause excluding force majeure relief for the vendor (or for specified events such as subcontractor failure or pandemics) is enforceable. Compare Art. 97 hardship, which cannot be contracted out of. Waivers and releases in settlement agreements need no form. Releases of future claims are not "existing debts" and are exposed to challenge; settle specific existing claims. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    59	| Arts. 111-112 | On termination or dissolution the parties are restored to their pre-contract position; if impossible, the court may award compensation. For continuing (time) contracts termination has no retroactive effect, and the court may award compensation. Termination is not opposable to a good-faith particular successor who acquired a real right. | إذا كان العقد من العقود الزمنية فلا يكون للفسخ أو الانفساخ أثرٌ رجعيٌّ. | Subscriptions, licences and service agreements are continuing contracts: termination is prospective, so fees for periods already served are not refundable by operation of law; refund of prepaid unused fees is a drafting point. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    60	| Art. 113 | Termination of the contract does not extinguish a dispute-resolution clause or a confidentiality clause, unless agreed otherwise, without prejudice to other laws. | دون إخلال بالنصوص النظامية، لا يزول بفسخ العقد شرط الالتزام بتسوية المنازعة ولا شرط الالتزام بالسرية؛ ما لم يتفق على خلاف ذلك. | Confidentiality and arbitration/jurisdiction clauses survive termination by statute. Other survival items (limitation of liability, indemnities, non-solicit, audit rights) survive only if the survival clause says so; the reviewer should still check the survival clause lists them. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    61	| Arts. 114, 191 | In bilateral contracts where mutual obligations are due, either party may withhold its performance while the other withholds its own. More generally, anyone bound to render something may withhold it while the creditor has not performed a connected obligation arising from the debtor's obligation and has not given adequate security. | إذا كانت الالتزامات المتقابلة مستحقة الوفاء جاز لأي من المتعاقدين أن يمتنع عن تنفيذ التزامه ما دام المتعاقد الآخر ممتنعًا عن تنفيذ ما التزم به. | Statutory suspension right: a vendor may suspend services for non-payment of due fees without a suspension clause, and a customer may withhold payment for undelivered milestones. Contractual "no set-off, pay first, dispute later" clauses contract out of this; note Art. 96 if the contract is one of adhesion. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    62	| Arts. 120, 125, 127-129, 143 | Every fault that causes harm to another obliges its author to compensate. A person is not liable if the harm arose from a cause beyond its control such as force majeure, a third party's fault or the victim's fault, unless agreed otherwise. Multiple tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable; the court apportions and, failing that, shares equally. A victim whose fault contributed to or increased the harm loses its right in proportion. A principal (matbu') is liable to the victim for harm caused by its subordinate's fault in or because of the work, where it had actual authority to supervise and direct, with recourse against the subordinate. A tort compensation action is not heard after 3 years from the victim's knowledge of the harm and of the person responsible, and in any event after 10 years from the harm; where the claim arises from a crime it remains admissible while the criminal action is. | كل خطأٍ سبب ضررًا للغير يُلزم من ارتكبه بالتعويض … لا يكون الشخص مسؤولًا إذا ثبت أن الضرر قد نشأ عن سببٍ لا يد له فيه، كقوةٍ قاهرةٍ أو خطأ الغير أو خطأ المتضرر؛ ما لم يُتفق على خلاف ذلك. … إذا تعدد المسؤولون عن فعل ضار كانوا متضامنين في التزامهم بتعويض الضرر … يكون المتبوع مسؤولًا تجاه المتضرر عن الضرر الذي يحدثه تابعه بخطئه أثناء تأدية عمله أو بسبب هذا العمل. … لا تُسمع دعوى التعويض الناشئة عن الفعل الضار بانقضاء (ثلاث) سنوات من تاريخ علم المتضرر بوقوع الضرر وبالمسؤول عنه. وفي جميع الأحوال لا تسمع الدعوى بانقضاء (عشر) سنوات من تاريخ وقوع الضرر. | General fault-based tort. Contract counterparties can sue in tort where the contract does not cover the loss; Art. 173(2) prevents excluding tort liability by agreement, which is why Saudi limitation-of-liability clauses do not reliably cap tort claims `[model knowledge — verify]`. Vicarious liability for employees and directed contractors. Indemnities for "acts of personnel" restate the statutory position; the important drafting point is the contractual allocation between the parties, since joint liability towards the victim cannot be excluded (Art. 173(2)). Tort claims have a shorter window than contract claims (10 years, Art. 295). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    63	| Arts. 136-138 | Compensation must repair the harm in full, restoring the victim to the position it was or could have been in but for the harm. Recoverable harm is the loss suffered and the profit lost, if a natural result of the harmful act; harm is a natural result if the victim could not have avoided it by the reasonable effort of an ordinary person. Compensation for a harmful act includes moral damage (physical or psychological suffering of a natural person from injury to body, liberty, honour, reputation or social standing); the court assesses it. | يتحدد الضرر الذي يلتزم المسؤول بالتعويض عنه بقدر ما لحق المتضرر من خسارةٍ وما فاته من كسب، إذا كان ذلك نتيجةً طبيعيةً للفعل الضار. ويعد كذلك إذا لم يكن في مقدور المتضرر تفاديه ببذل الجهد المعقول … يشمل التعويضُ عن الفعل الضار التعويضَ عن الضرر المعنوي. | Lost profit is recoverable by statute (a change from prior Saudi practice, which was restrictive `[model knowledge — verify]`). Mitigation is built into causation. Moral damages are defined for natural persons only; a company's reputational loss must be pleaded as material loss. These articles are imported into contract damages by Art. 180. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    64	| Arts. 161, 164, 167-169 | The debtor must perform when due; if it refuses, performance is compelled where the statutory conditions for enforcement are met. After notice, the debtor is compelled to specific performance where possible; if specific performance would be oppressive to the debtor the court may, on its application, confine the creditor to compensation if that causes the creditor no serious harm. For an obligation to do: the creditor may refuse performance by a third party where the contract or the nature of the work requires personal performance; if the debtor fails, the creditor may with court permission (or without it in urgency) have the obligation performed at the debtor's expense. For an obligation to preserve, manage or exercise care, the debtor performs by exercising the care of an ordinary person even if the purpose is not achieved, unless statute provides otherwise; where the obligation is to achieve a result, performance occurs only when the result is achieved. Breach of an obligation not to do entitles the creditor to removal of the breach at the debtor's expense plus compensation. | يُجبَر المدين بعد إعذاره على تنفيذ التزامه تنفيذًا عينيًّا متى كان ذلك ممكنًا. إذا كان في التنفيذ العيني إرهاقٌ للمدين جاز للمحكمة بناءً على طلبه أن تقصر حق الدائن على اقتضاء التعويض. … إذا كان المطلوب من المدين هو المحافظة على الشيء أو القيام بإدارته أو توخي الحيطة في تنفيذ التزامه، فإنه يكون قد وفى بالالتزام إذا بذل في تنفيذه عناية الشخص المعتاد ولو لم يتحقق الغرض المقصود … أما إذا كان المطلوب هو تحقيق غاية فلا يُعدُّ الوفاء حاصلًا إلا بتحقق تلك الغاية. | Specific performance is the primary remedy, not an exceptional one; injunction-style relief to compel a vendor to continue services or deliver is available in principle. Clauses stating that damages are the sole remedy are enforceable as agreements under Art. 94 but sit uneasily with this default `[model knowledge — verify]`. The means/result distinction is statutory: "reasonable efforts" and "shall achieve" language will be given effect literally. Step-in / replacement-vendor rights exist by default (court permission needed unless urgent); a contractual step-in clause removes the need for permission. Non-compete and non-solicit covenants are obligations not to do, enforceable by removal plus damages subject to public order and the Labor Law for employees `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    65	| Arts. 170-172 | The debtor is ordered to pay compensation for non-performance where specific performance is impossible, including where the debtor's delay makes performance useless to the creditor; on delay the creditor may set a reasonable period and then claim compensation for non-performance; no compensation is due if the debtor proves the failure was due to a cause beyond its control. Delay itself obliges the debtor to compensate the creditor's loss from the delay unless caused by a cause beyond its control. The creditor's contributory fault reduces compensation per Art. 128. | إذا تأخر المدين في تنفيذ التزامه وجب عليه تعويض الدائن عما يلحقه من ضرر بسبب التأخير، ما لم يثبت أن تأخير الوفاء بسبب لا يد له فيه. | Delay damages are the statutory vehicle for late payment and late delivery. The Law has no article awarding interest on money debts; compensation for late payment must be proved as actual loss, and any fixed percentage running with time risks characterisation as riba, which is prohibited under Sharia as applied in the Kingdom and treated as public order `[model knowledge — verify]`. See Arts. 178 (no pre-agreed compensation for money obligations) and 385 (loan increase void). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    66	| Art. 173 | The parties may agree to exempt the debtor from compensation for harm arising from non-performance or delay in performing its contractual obligation, except harm arising from the debtor's fraud (ghish) or gross fault. Liability arising from a harmful act (tort) may not be excluded by agreement. | يجوز الاتفاق على إعفاء المدين من التعويض عن الضرر الناشئ عن عدم تنفيذ التزامه التعاقدي أو تأخره فيه، إلا ما يكون عن غش أو خطأ جسيم منه. لا يجوز الاتفاق على الإعفاء من المسؤولية المترتبة على الفعل الضار. | Exclusion and limitation clauses (caps, consequential-loss exclusions) are valid for contractual liability, but never for fraud or gross fault, and never for tort. Every cap should carve out fraud and gross negligence expressly to match the statute; a cap that purports to cover "any liability howsoever arising" is partly void. "Gross fault" (خطأ جسيم) is undefined; expect it to be argued for reckless or wilful default `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    67	| Arts. 175-177 | Compensation is due only after notice (i'thar) to the debtor unless agreed or provided otherwise. Notice is not required where the parties agreed expressly or impliedly that the debtor is in default on the due date, where performance became impossible or useless by the debtor's act, for tort compensation, for restitution of something received knowingly without right, or where the debtor declared in writing that it will not perform. Notice may be given by any agreed means or any statutory means of service, including filing suit. | لا يُستحق التعويض إلا بعد إعذار المدين؛ ما لم يوجد اتفاق أو نص نظامي بخلاف ذلك … يكون الإعذار بأي وسيلة متفق عليها بين المتعاقدين، أو بأي وسيلة مقررة نظامًا للتبليغ، بما في ذلك رفع الدعوى. | Damages and termination both hinge on i'thar. A notices clause fixing the means (email to a named address) is the "agreed means" and should be drafted with i'thar in mind. A "time is of the essence" or "default occurs automatically on the due date without notice" clause is the Art. 176(a) agreement. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    68	| Art. 178 | The parties may fix the amount of compensation in advance in the contract or a later agreement, unless the object of the obligation is a sum of money; notice is not a condition of entitlement to such compensation. | يجوز للمتعاقدين أن يحددا مقدمًا مقدار التعويض بالنص عليه في العقد أو في اتفاقٍ لاحقٍ؛ ما لم يكن محل الالتزام مبلغًا نقديًّا، ولا يشترط لاستحقاق التعويض الإعذار. | Liquidated damages (delay LDs, service credits, penalty clauses) are valid for non-monetary obligations. They are not permitted for obligations to pay money: a late-payment charge fixed in advance (e.g. "2% per month on overdue invoices") is outside this article, so late-payment compensation must be proved as actual loss under Art. 171, and a fixed percentage running with time is exposed to the riba objection `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    69	| Art. 179 | Agreed compensation is not due if the debtor proves the creditor suffered no harm. The court may, on the debtor's application, reduce agreed compensation if the debtor proves it was exaggerated or the principal obligation was partly performed. The court may, on the creditor's application, increase agreed compensation to the amount of the harm if the creditor proves the harm exceeded the agreed amount as a result of the debtor's fraud or gross fault. Any agreement contrary to this article is void. | لا يكون التعويض الاتفاقيُّ مستحَقًّا إذا أثبت المدين أن الدائن لم يلحقه أي ضرر. للمحكمة بناءً على طلب المدين أن تنقص هذا التعويض إذا أثبت أن التعويض المتفق عليه كان مبالغًا فيه أو أن الالتزام الأصلي قد نُفذ جزء منه. للمحكمة بناءً على طلب الدائن أن تزيد هذا التعويض إلى ما يساوي الضرر إذا أثبت أن الضرر جاوز مقدار التعويض الاتفاقي نتيجة غش أو خطأٍ جسيمٍ من المدين. يقع باطلًا كلُّ اتفاقٍ يُخالف أحكام هذه المادة. | The single most important row for contract review. There is no penalty/LD distinction: every agreed sum is reviewable. (1) An LD clause yields nothing if the debtor proves no harm, so "payable whether or not loss is suffered" and "the parties agree the amount is a genuine pre-estimate and not subject to reduction" are void. (2) The court can cut an excessive amount and pro-rate for partial performance; the burden is on the debtor. (3) The creditor can go above the agreed sum only for fraud/gross fault, so an LD clause otherwise operates as a cap on delay damages — this is what makes "sole and exclusive remedy for delay" language work for the payer. Service-credit regimes in SaaS agreements are agreed compensation and fall under this article. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    70	| Art. 180 | If compensation is not fixed in the contract or by statute the court assesses it under Arts. 136-139. However, where the obligation arises from contract, a debtor who committed no fraud or gross fault is liable only for harm that could ordinarily be foreseen at the time of contracting. | إذا كان الالتزام مصدره العقد فلا يلتزم المدين الذي لم يرتكب غشًا أو خطأ جسيمًا إلا بتعويض الضرر الذي كان يمكن توقعه عادة وقت التعاقد. | Contractual damages are capped by statute at foreseeable loss (like the civil-law and Hadley v Baxendale rule), lost profit included if foreseeable. A consequential-loss exclusion narrows this further and is valid under Art. 173 (subject to the fraud/gross fault carve-out). Disclose special circumstances at signing if you want them covered. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    71	| Arts. 181, 183, 188 | All the debtor's assets secure its debts; all creditors rank equally except where statute gives priority; creditors may agree among themselves on priority not conflicting with statute. A creditor whose claim is due may have a debtor's transaction declared unenforceable against it if the transaction made the debtor's debts exceed its assets and was gratuitous, or for value where the debtor and transferee knew of the insolvency; the action is barred 1 year from the creditor's knowledge and in any event 10 years from the transaction. | أموال المدين جميعها ضامنةٌ للوفاء بديونه، وجميع الدائنين متساوون في هذا الضمان، ولا أولوية لأحدهم إلا بنص نظامي. يجوز الاتفاق بين الدائنين على تحديد الأولوية في استيفاء الديون بما لا يتعارض مع النصوص النظامية. … إذا تصرف المدين تصرفًا ترتبت عليه زيادة ديونه على أمواله، فلكل دائن كان حقه مستحق الأداء وتضرر من التصرف طلب منع نفاذ هذا التصرف في حقه. | Contractual subordination and intercreditor agreements are recognised. Security priority is governed by the Movable Assets Security Law and the real-estate mortgage rules, not this Law (Art. 719). Paulian (claw-back) action outside bankruptcy; the Bankruptcy Law has its own avoidance rules inside proceedings `[model knowledge — verify]`. Diligence on distressed counterparties: intra-group transfers at undervalue are vulnerable. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    72	| Arts. 197-203 | An obligation is conditional if its existence or extinction depends on a future uncertain event. A condition on an impossible event is void and voids the obligation; a condition intended to induce something unlawful prevents the obligation arising; an obligation subject to a suspensive condition depending purely on the obligor's will does not exist. Before a suspensive condition is fulfilled the obligation is not enforceable but the creditor may take protective measures; fulfilment of a resolutive condition extinguishes the obligation with restitution; fulfilment relates back to the date the obligation arose unless the parties' intention or the nature of the contract shows otherwise. | لا يكون الالتزام قائمًا إذا علق على شرط واقف يجعل وجود الالتزام متوقفًا على محض إرادة الملتزم. | Conditions precedent (regulatory approvals, board consent, financing) are suspensive conditions and are recognised; a CP that is entirely within the obligor's discretion ("subject to our approval") means there is no obligation at all. Draft CPs by reference to objective events or third-party acts. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    73	| Arts. 204-206 | An obligation is subject to a time limit (ajal) if its enforceability or extinction depends on a future certain event; it is not enforceable until the term falls due, but the creditor may take protective measures and demand security if it reasonably fears the debtor's insolvency. The debtor loses the benefit of the term if declared insolvent, fails to provide agreed security, or reduces the security. Early payment is permitted by the party for whose benefit the term runs unless it harms the other; in doubt the term is presumed for the debtor's benefit. | يسقط حق المدين في الأجل إذا حكم بإعساره، أو لم يقدم ضمانات الدين المتفق عليها، أو نقصت تلك الضمانات بفعله أو بسبب لا يد له فيه. | Statutory acceleration on insolvency and loss of security; contractual acceleration clauses extend this to other events. Prepayment is allowed by default (subject to no harm), relevant to early exit from instalment arrangements. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    74	| Arts. 222, 225, 231, 234 | Joint and several liability (tadamun) between debtors arises only by agreement or by statute. The creditor may claim the whole debt from all or any of them; claiming from one does not bar claiming from the others; each may raise its own and common defences. A joint debtor is liable in performance only for its own act; notice to one does not affect the others. Between themselves the shares are equal unless agreed or provided otherwise, and an insolvent co-debtor's share is borne by the solvent ones pro rata. | لا يكون التضامن بين المدينين إلا باتفاق أو بنص نظامي … للدائن أن يطالب بدينه كل المدينين المتضامنين أو بعضهم. | Without an express "jointly and severally" clause, co-obligors (consortium members, group affiliates signing together) are liable only for their shares; consortium and guarantor drafting must say تضامن expressly. Tortfeasors are joint by statute (Art. 127). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    75	| Arts. 238-240 | A creditor may assign its right to another unless statute, agreement or the nature of the obligation prevents it; the debtor's consent is not required for the assignment to be concluded. Only the attachable part of a right may be assigned. The assignment is not effective against the debtor or third parties until the debtor accepts it or is notified by any statutory means; effectiveness against third parties through the debtor's acceptance requires that acceptance to be in writing and of certain date. | للدائن أن يحيل حقه إلى شخص آخر، وذلك ما لم تقتضِ النصوص النظامية أو الاتفاق أو طبيعة الالتزام خلاف ذلك، ولا يشترط لانعقاد الحوالة رضى المدين بها … لا تكون حوالة الحق نافذة تجاه المدين أو تجاه الغير إلا إذا قبلها المدين أو أُعلِم بها بأي وسيلة مقررة نظامًا. | Receivables and other rights are freely assignable by default; an anti-assignment clause is the "agreement" that blocks it, so vendor anti-assignment clauses are effective against assignment of rights. Receivables financing needs notice to the account debtor to bind it. Assignment of rights is distinct from assignment of the contract as a whole (Art. 255), which always needs counterparty consent. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    76	| Arts. 241-245 | The right passes to the assignee with its attributes, accessories and securities. An assignor for value warrants only that the right exists at the time of assignment unless agreed otherwise; a gratuitous assignor warrants nothing; the assignor does not warrant the debtor's solvency unless specially agreed, and then only solvency at the time of assignment. The debtor may raise against the assignee the defences it had against the assignor at the time the assignment became effective against it, and defences from the assignment contract itself. | ينتقل الحق إلى المحال له بصفاته وتوابعه وضماناته … للمدين أن يتمسك تجاه المحال له بالدفوع التي كان له أن يتمسك بها تجاه المحيل وقت نفاذ الحوالة في حقه. | Guarantees and security travel with the assigned receivable. The account debtor keeps its set-off and defect defences accrued before notice; an assignee (factor, lender) takes subject to them. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    77	| Arts. 248-254 | Assignment of debt (hawalat al-dayn) transfers the obligation from the original debtor to the new debtor. Concluded between the old and new debtor, it is effective against the creditor only if the creditor accepts; it may also be concluded between the old debtor and the creditor, effective against the new debtor only on its acceptance. The debt passes with its attributes and accessories and the original debtor is discharged; securities remain except those given by third parties; the new debtor may raise the old debtor's defences except those personal to it; the old debtor warrants the new debtor's solvency at the relevant time unless agreed otherwise. | تنعقد حوالة الدين باتفاق بين المحيل والمحال عليه، ولا تنفذ في حق المحال إلا إذا قبلها … تبقى للدين المحال به ضماناته، ومع ذلك لا تبقى الضمانات المقدمة من الغير. | Novation of obligations needs creditor consent, so a debtor cannot offload payment obligations to an affiliate or successor unilaterally. Third-party guarantees fall away on transfer of the debt unless the guarantor re-confirms: in a restructuring or asset transfer, obtain fresh guarantor consent. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    78	| Arts. 255-258 | A party may transfer its position as party to a contract to another with the other party's consent; consent may be given in advance if it identifies the contract, in which case the transfer takes effect against the remaining party when the transferor notifies it. If the remaining party consents, the transferor is released for the future; if not, the transferor remains bound jointly and severally with the transferee unless agreed otherwise or the transfer was conditional on that consent. The transferee may raise defences relating to the debt but not those personal to the transferor; the remaining party may raise all defences it had against the transferor. Third-party securities lapse on release of the transferor. | يجوز للمتعاقد أن ينقل صفته كطرف في عقد إلى غيره بموافقة المتعاقد الآخر، ويجوز أن تمنح الموافقة مقدمًا إذا عين فيها العقد الذي يراد التنازل عنه، وينفذ التنازل في هذه الحال في حق المتنازل لديه إذا أعلمه به المتنازل. | Assignment of a whole contract (e.g. to an affiliate, an acquirer in an asset deal, or a successor) requires the counterparty's consent by statute, whether or not the contract has an anti-assignment clause. Standard "may assign to an affiliate or successor without consent" clauses are the advance consent Art. 255 permits, effective on notice; they must identify the contract (they do, being in it). Absent a release, the assignor stays jointly liable. Diligence: an "assignment permitted without consent" clause is what allows a target's contracts to move in an asset sale; a share sale needs no assignment (Art. 98). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    79	| Arts. 262-263 | A person who pays another's debt is subrogated to the creditor's rights (a) if bound with or for the debtor, (b) if a creditor paying a prior secured creditor, (c) where statute so provides, or (d) if the creditor and payer so agree at or before payment, even without the debtor's consent. The subrogated payer takes the creditor's right with its attributes, accessories, securities and defences, to the extent of what it paid; on partial subrogation the creditor ranks first for the balance unless agreed otherwise. | من وفى دين غيره حل محل الدائن الذي استوفى حقه في الحالات الآتية: إذا كان الموفي ملزمًا بالدين مع المدين أو ملزمًا بوفائه عنه … إذا اتفق الدائن والموفي عند الوفاء أو قبله على أن يحل محله، ولو لم يقبل المدين ذلك. | Guarantors, joint debtors and insurers (by statute) who pay step into the creditor's shoes; contractual subrogation is available by agreement with the creditor. Insurer subrogation clauses and waiver-of-subrogation clauses operate within this framework. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    80	| Arts. 273-275, 277-278 | The debtor may not compel the creditor to accept partial payment unless statute or agreement provides otherwise. Payment is due immediately on the obligation arising unless agreed or provided otherwise; where no statute applies, the court may in exceptional cases grant the debtor a reasonable grace period if its condition requires and the creditor suffers no serious harm. Default place of payment for contractual obligations is where the obligation arose (or where a specific thing was when the obligation arose); costs of payment are on the debtor unless agreed otherwise. | يجب أن يتمَّ الوفاء فورًا بمجرد ترتب الالتزام في ذمة المدين؛ ما لم يوجد اتفاق أو نص نظامي بخلاف ذلك. فيما لم يرد فيه نصٌّ نظامي؛ يجوز للمحكمة في حالاتٍ استثنائيَّةٍ أن تُنظر المدين إلى أجل معقولٍ. | Absent payment terms, fees are due on invoice/performance immediately. The court's power to grant a grace period is narrow but exists and cannot be excluded by the text (no "contrary agreement" language). Bank charges and transfer costs default to the payer. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    81	| Arts. 281-286, 288 | Set-off: a debtor may set off what it owes its creditor against what the creditor owes it, even if the causes differ, where both debts are money or fungibles of the same kind and quality, undisputed, judicially claimable and attachable; set-off operates only if invoked by the interested party and cannot be waived before the right arises; it extinguishes both debts to the extent of the lesser from the time they became eligible; if a condition fails, set-off requires both parties' agreement; set-off may not prejudice rights acquired by third parties; a time-barred debt can still be set off if the conditions were met before the bar. | للمدين المقاصة بين ما هو مستحق الأداء عليه لدائنه وما هو مستحق الأداء له تجاه هذا الدائن ولو اختلف سبب الدينين … وكان كل منهما خاليًا من النزاع، وصالحًا للمطالبة به قضاءً … لا تقع المقاصة إلا إذا تمسك بها من له مصلحة فيها، ولا يصح النزول عنها قبل ثبوت الحق فيها. | Statutory set-off is narrow (undisputed, due, liquidated). Contractual set-off clauses widen it by agreement (Art. 285). A "no set-off" clause is a waiver "before the right arises" and is therefore void under Art. 282 as against statutory set-off `[model knowledge — verify]` — reviewers should not treat customer no-set-off clauses as reliably enforceable. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    82	| Art. 295 | A right is not extinguished by lapse of time, but an action on it against a party who denies it is not heard after 10 years, save where statute provides otherwise or under the exceptions in this section. | لا ينقضي الحق بمرور الزمن، ولكن لا تُسمع الدعوى به على المنكر بانقضاء (عشر) سنوات فيما عدا الحالات التي ورد فيها نص نظامي أو الاستثناءات الواردة في هذا الفرع. | General contract limitation: 10 Hijri years from the due date (Art. 299). Saudi law bars the hearing of the action (عدم سماع الدعوى) rather than extinguishing the right, so a time-barred debt paid voluntarily is not recoverable and can still be set off (Art. 288). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    83	| Art. 296 | Without prejudice to other laws, an action against a denying party is not heard after 5 years for (a) claims of liberal professionals (physicians, lawyers, engineers) for work connected with their profession and their expenses, and (b) periodic recurring rights such as rent of real property, wages, and fixed revenues (except yields owed by a bad-faith possessor or a waqf administrator, which are 10 years). | لا تُسمع الدعوى على المنكر بانقضاء (خمس) سنوات في الحقوق الآتية: حقوق أصحاب المهن الحرة، كالأطباء والمحامين والمهندسين عما أدوه من عملٍ متصلٍ بمهنهم وما أنفقوه من نفقة. الحقوق الدورية المتجددة، كأجرة العقارات والأجور والإيرادات المرتبة ونحوها. | Recurring subscription fees and rent are "periodic recurring rights": 5 years. Engineering consultancy fees are 5 years. The Labor Law's own 12-month bar for labour claims is a "statutory provision" that prevails for employees `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    84	| Arts. 297-298 | Without prejudice to other laws, an action against a denying party is not heard after 1 year for (a) traders' claims for goods and services supplied to persons who do not trade in them, (b) claims of hotels, restaurants and the like arising from those activities, and (c) workers' claims for daily and other wages and the price of things they supplied. The 5-year professional and 1-year periods run even if dealings continue; if a written instrument is drawn up for such a right, the action is heard for 10 years from the instrument's date. | لا تُسمع الدعوى على المنكر بانقضاء (سنة) في الحقوق الآتية: حقوق التّجار عن السلع والخدمات المقدمة لأشخاصٍ لا يتّجرونَ فيها … وإذا حُرِّر سند بحقٍّ من هذه الحقوق لم يمتنع سماع الدعوى به إلا بانقضاء (عشر) سنوات من تاريخ تحرير السند. | A vendor supplying goods or services to a customer that does not trade in them (most B2B service supply to an end-user customer) has only 1 year to sue on unpaid invoices unless a written acknowledgment or signed contract for the debt exists, in which case 10 years. Signed contracts and acknowledged statements of account matter for collection. Whether a signed MSA is a "sanad" for each invoice is an open question. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    85	| Arts. 299-304 | Where no statute provides otherwise, the limitation period runs from the day the right becomes due. It is suspended whenever there is an excuse making it impossible to claim; good-faith negotiations between the parties that are ongoing when the period would expire, and a moral impediment to claiming, are such excuses. The period is interrupted by the debtor's express or implied acknowledgment of the right, by a judicial claim (even before an incompetent court), or by any other judicial step by the creditor to assert the right. After interruption a new period equal to the first runs from the end of the interrupting event's effect; where a judgment is given, or a 5-year professional or 1-year claim is interrupted by the debtor's acknowledgment, the new period is 10 years (unless the judgment covers periodic obligations falling due after it). | يبدأ سريان المدة المقررة لعدم سماع الدعوى لمرور الزمن … من اليوم الذي يكون فيه الحق مستحق الأداء. يقف سريان مدة عدم سماع الدعوى كلما وُجد عذرٌ تتعذر معه المطالبة بالحق. يعدُّ من الأعذار … وجود تفاوضٍ عن حسن نيَّةٍ بين الطرفين يكون قائمًا عند اكتمال المدة. … تنقطع مدة عدم سماع الدعوى في الحالات الآتية: إقرار المدين بالحق صراحةً أو ضمنًا. المطالبة القضائية، ولو كانت أمام محكمة غير مختصة … إذا صدر حكم قضائي بحق … فتكون المدة الجديدة (عشر) سنوات. | Ongoing settlement negotiations stop the clock by statute, so escalation/negotiation steps in a dispute clause do not cost the claimant its limitation period; document that negotiations were ongoing. A demand letter alone does not interrupt; a suit or an acknowledgment does. Getting the debtor to acknowledge the debt in writing converts a 1-year or 5-year claim into a 10-year one. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    86	| Arts. 305-306 | Limitation periods may not be shortened or lengthened by agreement; the debtor may not waive the plea before the right to it has arisen; a waiver towards some creditors does not bind others if it harms them. The court does not apply limitation of its own motion; only on the application of the debtor or an interested person. | لا يجوز الاتفاق على تقصير مدة عدم سماع الدعوى، ولا على إطالتها. لا يجوز أن يُسقط المدين حقه في التمسك بعدم سماع الدعوى قبل ثبوت هذا الحق له … لا تقضي المحكمة بعدم سماع الدعوى لمرور الزمن إلا بناءً على طلب المدين أو ذي مصلحة. | Contractual claims-bar clauses ("claims must be brought within 12 months") and tolling agreements are void as attempts to shorten or lengthen the period; this also undercuts warranty-claim deadlines drafted as limitation periods, though a contractual notice deadline as a condition of the warranty (as in Art. 344 for sale) is a different device `[model knowledge — verify]`. Limitation must be pleaded. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    87	| Arts. 307, 317-318, 320, 350 | Sale transfers ownership of the thing sold for a monetary price. The price is due immediately unless deferred or by instalments to a known term, in which case the term runs from the contract date unless agreed otherwise. Ownership passes on conclusion of the sale (subject to the registration rules in Arts. 655-657). Where the price is deferred or by instalments the seller may stipulate that ownership does not pass until full payment even if delivered; on full payment the buyer's ownership relates back to the sale. If a date is fixed for payment and the seller stipulated that non-payment on the date means there is no sale, and the buyer fails to pay, the sale is treated as terminated if the seller so elects, without need for notice, unless agreed otherwise. | تنتقل ملكية المبيع إلى المشتري بانعقاد البيع … للبائع -إذا كان الثمن مؤجلًا أو مقسَّطًا- أن يشترط تعليق نقل الملكية للمشتري حتى يؤدي جميع الثمن ولو سُلِّم المبيع. … إذا حُدد في البيع موعدٌ معيَّنٌ، واشترط البائع أنه إذا لم يؤد المشتري الثمن في الموعد فلا بيع بينهما، ولم يؤده؛ عُدَّ البيع مفسوخًا إذا اختار البائع ذلك، دون حاجةٍ إلى إعذار. | Retention-of-title clauses are expressly valid. Title passes on contract, not delivery, so risk and title must be addressed together (risk stays with the seller until delivery under Art. 329). Registration of the ROT as a security interest under the Movable Assets Security Law may be needed for priority against third parties `[model knowledge — verify]`. Express "time of the essence for payment" clauses in sales are enforced without i'thar; the seller has an election, so the buyer cannot rely on its own default to escape. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    88	| Arts. 321-324, 329 | The seller must deliver the thing in its condition at the time of sale, at the seller's cost, free of any third-party right unknown to the buyer, with its accessories and customary appurtenances; delivery is putting the thing in the buyer's possession or placing it at the buyer's disposal with notice. If the thing perishes before delivery for a cause attributable to no one, the sale is dissolved and the buyer recovers the price; partial loss dissolves pro tanto with a buyer's option to terminate the whole. | يلتزم البائع بتسليم المبيع إلى المشتري بالحال التي كان عليها وقت البيع، وتكون نفقات تسليم المبيع على البائع … إذا هلك المبيع قبل التسليم بسببٍ لا يد لأحد المتبايعين أو الغير فيه انفسخ البيع واسترد المشتري الثمن. | Risk passes on delivery, not on contract. Incoterms and delivery clauses displace these defaults ("unless agreed otherwise" in Art. 321). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    89	| Arts. 330, 337 | The seller warrants against its own interference and against third-party rights existing before the sale or derived from the seller. The parties may exclude, limit or extend the warranty of title, except for eviction arising from the seller's own act or something it deliberately concealed; even with an exclusion the buyer recovers the price unless it knew of the ground of eviction at the sale. | يصحُّ الاتفاق على إعفاء البائع من ضمان الاستحقاق أو الحد من هذا الضمان أو زيادته، عدا ما يكون منه ناشئًا عن فعل البائع أو كان قد تعمد إخفاءه. | Title/IP-non-infringement warranties can be limited but not for the seller's own acts or concealed defects in title; price refund survives any exclusion. Relevant to software licence IP indemnity carve-outs. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    90	| Arts. 338-339 | The seller warrants on delivery that the thing is free of any defect reducing its value or its usefulness for the purpose shown in the contract, apparent from its nature, or for which it was prepared, even if the seller did not know of it. On a defect the buyer may terminate or keep the thing and recover the price difference (proportion of sound to defective value), and the seller may avert this by supplying a conforming replacement; compensation is also available. No warranty where the buyer knew or could have discovered the defect by ordinary inspection (unless the seller guaranteed against that defect or concealed it), where custom tolerates the defect, where it arose after delivery from a cause not pre-existing, or in judicial/administrative auctions. | يضمن البائع عند تسليم المبيع سلامته من أي عيبٍ ينقص من قيمته أو من نفعه بحسب الغاية المقصودة منه … ويضمن البائع هذا العيب ولو لم يكن عالماً بوجوده. | Statutory implied warranty of merchantability/fitness with a replacement right for the seller. Hardware and off-the-shelf software supply fall here; the buyer's remedies are termination, price abatement, or replacement, plus damages. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    91	| Arts. 340-341, 343-344 | The buyer must check the thing's condition as soon as it can per usual practice and notify a discovered defect within a reasonable time, and a hidden defect as soon as it appears, or is deemed to have accepted the thing with the defect. Express or implied acceptance of the defect forfeits termination and abatement. The parties may exclude, limit or extend the defect warranty unless the seller deliberately concealed the defect. The defect warranty action is not heard after 180 days from delivery unless the seller undertook a longer warranty; the seller may not plead this period if the concealment was fraudulent. | يصحُّ الاتفاق على إعفاء البائع من ضمان العيب أو الحد من هذا الضمان أو زيادته ما لم يكن البائع قد تعمد إخفاءه. لا تُسمع دعوى ضمان العيب بانقضاء (مائةٍ وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ تسليم المبيع؛ ما لم يلتزم البائع بالضمان مدة أطول. | Acceptance-testing clauses and defect-notification windows mirror the statute. The statutory defect claim window is 180 Hijri days from delivery, extendable by a longer contractual warranty, so a 12-month warranty clause is the buyer's protection and should be drafted as an undertaking of the seller, not as a limitation period (Art. 305). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    92	| Art. 385 (loan) | Any condition, at the contract or on deferral of repayment, that includes an increase in the repayment of a loan payable by the borrower to the lender is void. | يقع باطلًا كل شرط عند العقد أو عند تأجيل الوفاء يتضمن زيادة في رد القرض يؤديها المقترض إلى المقرض. | The Law's only express riba rule: interest on a loan (qard) is void, including on rescheduling. The Law does not state a general prohibition on interest for other money obligations; that prohibition is Sharia as applied by Saudi courts, which have historically refused to enforce interest clauses while enforcing the principal `[model knowledge — verify]`. Financing structures are governed by SAMA rules and Sharia-compliant forms outside this Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    93	| Arts. 391-392 (settlement, note) | Settlement (sulh) is a contract by which the parties end an existing dispute or avert a potential one by mutual concessions; a party must have capacity to dispose for value of the rights covered; if it gives up rights without consideration it must have full capacity. | الصلح عقدٌ يحسم بمقتضاه المتصالحان نزاعًا قائمًا أو يتوقيان نزاعًا محتملًا، بأن ينزل كلٌّ منهما على وجه التَّقابل عن مُطالبته أو جزءٍ منها. | Settlement agreements are a named contract; mutual concessions are required for characterisation as sulh. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    94	| Arts. 407, 437-440 (lease, note) | A lease grants use of a non-consumable thing for a fixed period for rent. The lessee may not sublet or assign the lease without the lessor's permission or ratification; an assignee steps into all rights and obligations. A lease ends on expiry of its period unless automatic renewal was stipulated; if the lessee continues in use with the lessor's express or implied consent the lease is renewed on its terms (minus third-party securities) for a period fixed under Art. 413; a rent increase demanded by the lessor binds a lessee who stays on without objecting. | ينتهي عقد الإيجار بانقضاء المدة المعينة فيه؛ ما لم يُشتَرط تجدده تلقائيًّا. إذا انتهى عقد الإيجار واستمر المستأجر في الانتفاع بالمأجور برضى المؤجر الصريح أو الضمني عُدَّ العقد مجددًا بشروطه وضماناته. | Auto-renewal clauses are expressly recognised for leases; holding over renews the lease by conduct. Commercial leases also fall under the Ejar platform rules and, for real estate, other statutes `[model knowledge — verify]`. There is no equivalent statutory auto-renewal rule for service or subscription contracts (see Open questions). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    95	| Arts. 461-466, 479 | Muqawala is a contract by which the contractor undertakes to make a thing or perform work for remuneration without being the employer's subordinate or agent; materials may come from either side, and the contractor is responsible for agreed specifications (or fitness for purpose by custom) of materials it supplies. The contractor must complete the work per the contract and within the agreed time, or per recognised standards and within a reasonable time. If the contractor breaches the contract terms during the work, the employer may serve notice to comply and correct within a reasonable period; on expiry it may engage another contractor at the first's expense (Art. 167) or seek termination; immediate termination is available where the defect cannot be remedied or the contractor's delay makes completion on time hopeless. Employment contracts are governed by their own statute. | إذا أخل المقاول بشروط العقد أثناء سير العمل فلصاحب العمل إعذاره بالالتزام بها وتصحيح العمل خلال مدة معقولة يعينها له، فإذا انقضت هذه المدة دون تصحيح فلصاحب العمل أن يعهد إلى مقاول آخر بإنجاز العمل أو تصحيحه على نفقة المقاول الأول … أو أن يطلب فسخ العقد. … تسري على عقد العمل النصوص النظامية الخاصة به. | Muqawala covers construction, engineering, manufacturing and most professional-services and SOW-based contracts (the independent-contractor definition). Notice-and-cure followed by replacement at the contractor's cost is the statutory remedy path; SOW cure periods should match. Employment matters go to the Labor Law file; this Law applies to employment only residually (Art. 1). Contractor-vs-employee characterisation turns on the Art. 461 "not subordinate" test read with the Labor Law definition `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    96	| Arts. 467-469 | If the work perishes before delivery for a cause not attributable to the contractor, the contractor may not claim the price or its expenses unless the employer was in breach of its duty to take delivery. The employer must take delivery promptly once the work is completed and placed at its disposal; unjustified refusal shifts the risk. Remuneration is due on taking delivery unless agreed otherwise; where the work is in parts or priced per unit, the employer pays for completed parts after inspection and acceptance, provided the completed part is distinct or significant. | يلتزم صاحب العمل بالوفاء بالأجر عند تسلم العمل المتفق عليه، ما لم يتفق على خلاف ذلك. إذا كان العمل مكوَّنًا من عدة أجزاء، أو كان الأجر محدَّدًا على أساس الوحدة؛ التزم صاحب العمل بأن يفي للمقاول من الأجر بقدر ما أنجز من العمل بعد معاينته وقبوله. | Payment-on-acceptance is the default; milestone billing needs acceptance criteria. Deemed-acceptance clauses (silence for N days) are the "agreed otherwise" and interact with Art. 37. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    97	| Arts. 470-472 | Under a unit-price bill of quantities, a contractor who sees the design will clearly exceed the estimate must immediately notify the employer with the expected increase or loses the right to the excess; if the overrun is gross the employer may withdraw without delay, paying for work done. Under a lump-sum contract on an agreed design the contractor may claim no increase even if material prices, wages or other costs rise; changes or additions to the design justify an increase only if due to the employer's fault or made with its permission and an agreed price increase. If the contractual balance collapses because of general exceptional unforeseeable circumstances that undermine the financial basis of the contract, the court may, weighing both interests, restore the balance including by extending time, increasing or reducing the price, or terminate the contract. If no price is fixed, the contractor is entitled to the customary price plus the value of materials. | إذا تم عقد المقاولة بموجب تصميمٍ متفقٍ عليه مقابل أجرٍ إجماليٍّ فليس للمقاول أن يطالب بأيّ زيادةٍ في الأجر ولو ارتفعت أسعار المواد المستخدمة في العمل أو ارتفعت أجور العمال … إذا انهار التوازن العقدي … فللمحكمة … أن تقضي بإعادة التوازن العقدي، بما في ذلك تمديد مدة التنفيذ أو زيادة الأجر أو إنقاصه، أو أن تقضي بفسخ العقد. | Fixed-price SOWs are firm by statute against ordinary cost inflation; variations need written authorisation and an agreed price. The muqawala hardship rule is wider than Art. 97 (court may extend time, reprice or terminate) and has no express non-derogation sentence, but Art. 97's own non-derogation clause will likely be applied by analogy `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    98	| Arts. 473-474 | The contractor may subcontract all or part of the work unless statute, agreement or the nature of the work prevents it or the contractor's identity was a material consideration; the contractor remains liable to the employer. A subcontractor may not claim from the employer directly except what the contractor has assigned to it. | للمقاول أن يُسند تنفيذ العمل كله أو بعضه إلى مقاول من الباطن؛ ما لم تقتض النصوص النظامية أو الاتفاق أو طبيعة العمل خلاف ذلك أو كانت شخصية المقاول محل اعتبار. تبقى مسؤولية المقاول قائمة قِبَلَ صاحب العمل. | Subcontracting is free by default; a consent requirement must be written in. No statutory direct-payment right for subcontractors (contrast Egyptian-model codes `[model knowledge — verify]`). Reviewers should treat the prime contractor's liability for subcontractors as statutory. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
    99	| Arts. 475-478 | Muqawala ends on completion. Either party may seek termination on a supervening excuse (udhr tari') relating to performance, compensating the other for resulting harm. A contractor who begins and becomes unable to complete for a cause beyond its control is paid for work done and for expenses on unfinished work to the extent of the employer's benefit. Death of the contractor dissolves the contract only where personal performance was stipulated or the contractor's identity was a consideration. | لكل من المتعاقدين طلب فسخ عقد المقاولة إذا حدث له عذر طارئ يتعلق بتنفيذ العقد، ويلزمه تعويض المتعاقد الآخر عما ينشأ عن هذا الفسخ من ضرر. | A statutory termination-for-excuse right exists on both sides, but with compensation; termination for convenience with a fixed fee is the contractual version and is enforceable (Art. 106). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
   100	| Decennial liability (not in this Law) | The muqawala chapter (Arts. 461-478) contains no article imposing a 10-year joint liability on contractors and architects/engineers for collapse or defects of buildings and fixed structures; the only building-related liability in the Law is the building custodian's tort liability for collapse due to poor maintenance, age or defect (Art. 131). | لا يوجد في نظام المعاملات المدنية نص على ضمان المقاول والمهندس العشري؛ المادة (131): يكون حارس البناء مسؤولًا عن تعويض الضرر الذي يحدثه تهدّم البناء كله أو بعضه؛ ما لم يثبت أن الضرر لا يرجع سببه إلى إهمال في الصيانة أو قدم في البناء أو عيب فيه. | Do not cite a Saudi statutory decennial liability from this Law. Engineering and construction counterparties' latent-defect exposure rests on the contract's defects-liability and warranty provisions, the general 10-year limitation (Art. 295), Art. 173 (no exclusion for fraud/gross fault), the Saudi Building Code and the Engineers' professional regulations `[model knowledge — verify]`. Whether courts will import a decennial rule from Sharia/comparative practice via Art. 1 is unknown; treat as an open question for counsel. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] (absence confirmed by reading Arts. 461-478 and searching the text for المهندس/تهدم) |
   101	| Arts. 480-487, 503-505 (agency, note) | Agency (wakala) is a contract placing the agent in the principal's position for a legal act; it may be absolute, restricted, conditional or for a term, but not in general words without specifying the type of act; acts beyond administration need a special agency. A paid agent owes the care of an ordinary person; money received for the principal is a deposit. The principal may revoke or restrict the agency at will (unless given in the agent's or a third party's interest), on notice; revocation of a paid agency at an inopportune time or without acceptable justification obliges the principal to compensate the agent, and the same applies to the agent's untimely renunciation. | للموكل أن يعزل وكيله أو يقيد وكالته متى أراد إلا إذا كانت صادرة لصالح الوكيل أو لصالح الغير … إذا كانت الوكالة بأجر وعزل الموكل وكيله في وقت غير مناسب أو بغير مسوغ مقبول، لزمه تعويض الوكيل عن الضرر. | Powers of attorney must specify the type of act; a general "do anything" power is invalid. Commercial agency and distribution are separately regulated by the Commercial Agencies Law `[model knowledge — verify]`; terminating a paid agent without cause at a bad time carries statutory compensation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
   102	| Arts. 578-579, 581, 583-584 | Guarantee (kafala) is a contract by which the guarantor undertakes to the creditor to perform the debtor's obligation if the debtor does not; it is concluded between guarantor and creditor (the creditor's silence on receiving the offer is acceptance) and does not depend on the debtor's consent. It is valid only if the guaranteed obligation is valid. A guarantee of a future debt is valid if the amount is fixed in advance, and of a conditional debt; a guarantor of a future debt with no fixed term may withdraw on sufficient advance notice to the creditor before the debt arises. A guarantee for more than the debt or on harsher terms is valid only to the extent of the debt and its terms. | تصح الكفالة في الدين المستقبل إذا حدد مقدمًا مقدار المكفول به … للكفيل في الدين المستقبل إذا لم يعين مدة للكفالة أن يرجع عن كفالته بشرط إعلام الدائن برجوعه قبل ترتب الدين بوقت كافٍ. | Parent-company guarantees of "all sums" under an MSA are guarantees of future debts and need a stated maximum amount to be valid; open-ended uncapped guarantees are exposed. Uncapped, unlimited-duration guarantees are also revocable on notice before new debts arise. Guarantor liability cannot exceed the principal debt. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
