   105	
   106	## Open questions for a local practitioner
   107	
   108	- **Adhesion review in B2B deals (Arts. 40, 96, 104(3)).** The text does not confine adhesion-contract review to consumers. Whether commercial courts apply Art. 96 to standard-form B2B SaaS/vendor terms, and how the Decree para. 6 "nature of the commercial transaction" carve-out is being used, needs confirmation from recent commercial court practice.
   109	- **"Gross fault" (خطأ جسيم) in Arts. 173, 179, 180.** Undefined in the text. Ask counsel how courts have drawn the line (recklessness? serious negligence?) since 2024; the answer determines how much protection a liability cap gives.
   110	- **Late-payment compensation and interest.** The text gives delay damages (Art. 171), bars pre-agreed compensation for money obligations (Art. 178) and voids loan increases (Art. 385), but says nothing about a general prohibition on interest. Ask counsel (a) whether courts award anything beyond proven loss for late payment, (b) whether "late payment charges" framed as administrative costs survive, and (c) how foreign-law interest awards are treated at enforcement.
   111	- **Contractual claims-bar periods (Art. 305 vs Art. 344).** Art. 305 voids agreements shortening limitation; Art. 344 lets the seller extend the defect-warranty period. Whether a "claims must be notified within 12 months" clause in a services contract is a void limitation clause or a valid condition of the warranty is unresolved in the text.
   112	- **No-set-off clauses (Art. 282).** The text voids waiver of set-off "before the right arises"; whether commercial courts nonetheless enforce no-set-off clauses in B2B contracts as an agreed payment mechanism is unknown.
   113	- **Decennial liability.** Absent from the Law. Confirm with counsel what latent-defect regime applies to contractors and engineers (Saudi Building Code, Engineers' Council rules, Government Tenders and Procurement Law for public works) and whether any court has imported a 10-year rule via Art. 1.
   114	- **Hardship in muqawala (Art. 471(3)) vs general hardship (Art. 97).** Art. 471(3) lacks Art. 97's non-derogation sentence. Whether a fixed-price construction/engineering contract can validly exclude Art. 471(3) rebalancing is open.
   115	- **Automatic renewal of service contracts.** The Law regulates auto-renewal only for leases (Art. 440). There is no statutory notice period, cap, or consumer-style cancellation rule for auto-renewing B2B subscriptions in this Law; the E-Commerce Law and consumer-protection rules may apply to B2C. Confirm whether any 2024-2026 regulation (Ministry of Commerce) has addressed subscription auto-renewal.
   116	- **Official English translation.** Not in the source set. When obtained, conform the English column and note any divergences from the Arabic in the Practical effect column.
   117	- **Application to pre-Law contracts (Decree para. 5).** How often are counterparties succeeding in pleading a pre-2023 "judicial principle" (e.g. on lost profit or penalty clauses) to displace the Law for legacy contracts?
   118	- **Governing-law and language clauses.** The text has no conflict-of-laws chapter. Whether Saudi courts give effect to a foreign governing-law clause in a contract performed in the Kingdom, and how a bilingual contract with an English-prevails clause is treated, are outside this Law and must be confirmed.
   119	
   120	## How skills should use this file
   121	
   122	- **Stop rule (all skills).** If a skill needs a rule that is not in this file, or the row it needs carries `[model knowledge — verify]` (in the Tag column or inline in Practical effect), the skill must say so in its output and either stop that finding or flag it as "unverified — Saudi counsel to confirm". It must never supply the missing rule from memory. Rows tagged `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` may be cited by article number; the inline `[model knowledge — verify]` sentences within those rows are practice commentary and must be flagged separately if relied on.
   123	- **Hijri periods.** Every period in this file is Hijri (Art. 2). Any skill that computes a date (limitation expiry, 180-day defect window, 180-day ghabn window, 180-day guarantor notice) must compute in Hijri and state the formula: "Art. [N] period of [X] Hijri [days/years] from [trigger] per Art. 299 → [date] (Umm al-Qura), converted from Gregorian [date]", and tag the result with the row's provenance tag plus "computed".
   124	- **commercial-legal `review` (router).** At the jurisdiction-detection step, when the contract is Saudi-governed or the counterparty is Saudi, load this file in place of the US jurisdiction delta table and pass the following row sets to the routed skill. State the retroactivity position (Decree para. 5) for contracts dated before 16 December 2023.
   125	- **commercial-legal `vendor-agreement-review`.** At the clause-by-clause step, cite: liquidated damages / penalties / service credits → Arts. 178-179 (court reduction, no-harm defence, no contracting out, not available for money obligations); limitation and exclusion of liability → Arts. 173, 180 (fraud/gross fault carve-out mandatory, tort not excludable, foreseeability cap); hardship / change in circumstances → Art. 97 (mandatory) and Art. 471(3) for SOW/muqawala work; force majeure → Arts. 110, 125, 174 (impossibility standard; risk allocation allowed); termination → Arts. 107-108 (judicial default; contractual termination valid; i'thar must be expressly waived), 111 (prospective for continuing contracts), 113 (confidentiality and DR survive); assignment → Arts. 238-240 (rights), 248-254 (debts), 255-258 (contract; consent required; advance consent valid on notice); limitation → Arts. 295-306 (10/5/1 years, suspension by negotiation, no contractual shortening, must be pleaded); warranties → Arts. 109, 338-344; suspension/set-off → Arts. 114, 191, 281-288; interest/late fees → Arts. 171, 178, 385 plus the riba flag; guarantees → Arts. 578-606. Redlines that propose "penalty payable regardless of loss" or "no court adjustment" must be refused as void under Art. 179.
   126	- **commercial-legal `nda-review`.** At triage: confidentiality survives termination by statute (Art. 113) so a missing survival clause is YELLOW, not RED; non-solicit and non-compete covenants are obligations not to do (Art. 169) enforceable subject to public order, with employee non-competes governed by the Labor Law (out of scope here, flag); pre-contract disclosure duties and bad-faith negotiation exposure → Art. 41; liquidated damages for breach of confidence → Arts. 178-179; exclusion of liability for deliberate disclosure is void (Art. 173).
   127	- **commercial-legal `saas-msa-review`.** At the auto-renewal step: state that auto-renewal of B2B subscriptions is not statutorily regulated by this Law (only leases, Art. 440); enforceability rests on Arts. 94 and 37, with Art. 96 adhesion-contract review as the customer's protection against abusive renewal or unilateral price-change terms; deemed acceptance by silence → Art. 37. Price escalation → Arts. 94, 96. Service credits → Arts. 178-179 (agreed compensation; reviewable; cap effect). Uptime SLA remedies as sole remedy → Arts. 164, 179 (`[model knowledge — verify]` on sole-remedy clauses). Data portability and subprocessor rights are not addressed by this Law; route to the PDPL file. Suspension for non-payment → Art. 114.
   128	- **commercial-legal `renewal-tracker` and `renewal-watcher` agent.** No statutory notice window exists for service-contract renewals; use only the contract's own notice period. Where a register entry is a lease, apply Art. 440 (holding over renews; automatic renewal clause valid). Convert Hijri-dated contracts per Art. 2 and show both calendars.
   129	- **commercial-legal `escalation-flagger` and `stakeholder-summary`.** When summarising, express Saudi positions as "statutory default / mandatory / drafting point" using the row's Practical effect; label mandatory rules (Arts. 96, 97, 179, 173 carve-out, 305) as non-negotiable.
   130	- **commercial-legal `amendment-history`.** Amendments require agreement (Art. 94); unilateral notices of change are effective only under a clause permitting them. Flag amendments made by silence/course of dealing (Art. 37).
   131	- **corporate-legal `diligence-issue-extraction`, `tabular-review`, `material-contract-schedule`.** At contract-review extraction: change of control — a share sale does not transfer contracts (Art. 98), so a change-of-control termination right exists only if drafted; asset deals need counterparty consent to move contracts unless the contract contains advance consent (Art. 255); assignor stays jointly liable absent release (Art. 256); third-party guarantees lapse on debt transfer (Art. 252). Guarantees given by the target → Arts. 583 (future-debt cap), 591 (subsidiary unless joint). Distressed transfers → Art. 183 claw-back. Penalty exposure under target contracts → Arts. 178-179. Legacy contracts → Decree para. 5.
   132	- **corporate-legal `closing-checklist`, `integration-management`.** Post-closing contract-transfer steps: obtain counterparty consents (Art. 255), notices to account debtors for assigned receivables (Art. 240), guarantor re-confirmations (Arts. 252, 258).
   133	- **corporate-legal `entity-compliance`, `written-consent`, `board-minutes`.** Juristic personality and representative authority → Arts. 17-18, 87-93 (note only; the Companies Law file governs).
   134	- **employment-legal skills.** This Law does not apply to employment contracts (Art. 479); only `worker-classification` should cite Art. 461 (independent contractor definition) alongside the Labor Law, and it must flag that the Labor Law test controls.

