 succeeded in 0ms:
# Personal Data Protection Law — نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية

<!-- Instrument file for references/jurisdictions/ksa. Written 2026-09-04 from the Arabic text on laws.boe.gov.sa (consolidated post-M/148 text: in the regenerated source file each amended article is headed [معدلة], its body is the original 1443H wording labelled "النص الأصلي (قبل التعديل)", and the "تعديلات المادة" block that follows is the wording in force; every row below cites the in-force block, cross-checked against the live portal pop-ups fetched the same day) and from SDAIA's official English of the Implementing Regulation. Scope of this file is deliberately limited to what a COMMERCIAL CONTRACT REVIEW must check; the privacy plugin's fuller treatment is out of scope. -->

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
| Instrument | Personal Data Protection Law / نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية, with (a) the Implementing Regulation of the PDPL (اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام حماية البيانات الشخصية) issued by SDAIA, and (b) the Regulation on Personal Data Transfer Outside the Kingdom (لائحة نقل البيانات الشخصية إلى خارج المملكة), also SDAIA |
| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/19 of 9/2/1443H (16 September 2021), approving Council of Ministers Resolution No. 98 of 7/2/1443H; amended by Royal Decree No. M/148 of 5/9/1444H (about 27 March 2023, Hijri converted), which replaced or amended 24 articles, repealed Art. 32, and repealed item Second of Royal Decree M/19. The Implementing Regulation and the Transfer Regulation are SDAIA decisions issued under Art. 42 (issuing instrument numbers not in the source files) |
| Issuing authority | Competent Authority (الجهة المختصة) designated by the Council of Ministers: the Saudi Data and Artificial Intelligence Authority (SDAIA) (Council of Ministers Resolution 98 item Second: SDAIA for two years, with consideration of transfer to the National Data Management Office; see the last row). Saudi Central Bank retains its powers for its supervised entities (Art. 30(1)) |
| Effective date | 2023-09-14 (Art. 43 as amended by M/148: "after 720 days from the date of publication in the Official Gazette"; published 17/2/1443H = 24 September 2021; 24 September 2021 + 720 days = 14 September 2023). Council of Ministers Resolution 98 item Fourth (and Royal Decree M/19 item Third): controllers must regularise within one year from the effective date, i.e. by 14 September 2024, extendable by the Competent Authority for some entities; SDAIA treated 14 September 2024 as the start of enforcement `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Amendment history | Royal Decree M/148 of 5/9/1444H: amended Art. 1 (definitions of destruction, disclosure, sensitive data, data subject), 4, 5, 6 (added legitimate-interest ground), 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 24, 29 (transfer regime rewritten), 30, 33, 35, 36, 37, 42, 43; repealed Art. 32 (national register fee). Implementing Regulation: first issued September 2023, amended September 2024 `[model knowledge — verify]`; the SDAIA English file read carries no edition date in its front matter (see Open questions). Transfer Regulation: issued September 2023, amended September 2024 `[model knowledge — verify]`; not among the source files |
| Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/b7cfae89-828e-4994-b167-adaa00e37188/1 |
| Official translation | Law: none on the portal page (no "Translated document" attachment listed; no `boe-en` file on 2026-09-04). Implementing Regulation: SDAIA official English (`authority/sdaia-pdpl-implementing-regs-en.txt`, tag `[authority — SDAIA]`); SDAIA "Guide to the Saudi PDPL for Controllers and Processors", Version 1.0, December 2023 (`authority/sdaia-guide-en.txt`), used only as secondary support |
| Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Law, Arabic: all 24 amended articles read from the in-force "تعديلات المادة" blocks of the regenerated source file, cross-checked against the live portal pop-ups fetched the same day); Implementing Regulation (SDAIA English) 2026-09-04 |
| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page read. Issue date 9/2/1443H (16/09/2021); publication 17/2/1443H (24/09/2021); status "ساري"; `articles marked as amended on the portal: 24`; amending instrument named: Royal Decree M/148 of 5/9/1444H only. For each of the 24 amended articles the source file carries the original text and the in-force M/148 text; the rows below cite the in-force text only (Art. 1, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 24, 29, 30, 33, 35, 36, 37, 42, 43; Art. 32 repealed). No row relies on the pre-amendment wording except where it is expressly contrasted in the Practical effect column. No amendment after M/148 is shown on the portal. The Transfer Regulation was not read; rows that depend on it are tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` |

## Scope

The Law applies to any processing of personal data of individuals carried out in the Kingdom by any means, and to processing by any entity outside the Kingdom of personal data of individuals resident in the Kingdom (Art. 2). It binds controllers (جهة التحكم: whoever determines purpose and means) and, through them, processors (جهة المعالجة: whoever processes on the controller's behalf) (Art. 1). For a commercial contract review it supplies: the mandatory content of a controller-processor agreement (Art. 8, Implementing Regulation Art. 17), the lawful bases including consent and legitimate interest (Art. 5, 6, 10, 15), the cross-border transfer conditions (Art. 29), breach notification (Art. 20, Regulation Art. 24), DPO and records duties (Art. 30, 31, Regulation Art. 32, 33), and the penalty ceilings (Art. 35, 36). It does not cover purely personal or family use (Art. 2(2)); it does not displace more protective rules in other laws (Art. 3), the National Cybersecurity Authority's remit (Royal Decree M/19 item Fourth), or SAMA's supervisory powers (Art. 30(1)). Data-subject rights (Art. 4, Regulation Art. 3-8) are noted in one row only; the privacy plugin holds the fuller treatment.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 1 (definitions; "sensitive data", "disclosure", "destruction", "data subject" as amended by M/148) | "Personal data": any data, whatever its source or form, that identifies an individual specifically or makes him identifiable directly or indirectly (name, ID number, addresses, contact numbers, licence and property numbers, bank and card numbers, images, and other personal data). "Processing": any operation on personal data by manual or automated means (collection, recording, storage, modification, use, disclosure, transfer, publication, erasure, destruction, etc.). "Controller": any public entity or private natural or juristic person that determines the purpose and manner of processing, whether it processes itself or through a processor. "Processor": any public entity or private natural or juristic person that processes personal data for the benefit of and on behalf of the controller. "Sensitive data" (amended): data on racial or ethnic origin, religious, intellectual or political belief, security and criminal data, biometric identification data, genetic data, health data, and data indicating unknown parentage. "Disclosure" (amended): enabling any person other than the controller or the processor, as the case may be, to obtain, use or view personal data. | جهة التحكم: أي جهة عامة، وأي شخصية ذات صفة طبيعية أو اعتبارية خاصة؛ تحدد الغرض من معالجة البيانات الشخصية وكيفية ذلك؛ سواء أباشرت معالجة البيانات بوساطتها أم بوساطة جهة المعالجة. جهة المعالجة: أي جهة عامة، وأي شخصية ذات صفة طبيعية أو اعتبارية خاصة؛ تعالج البيانات الشخصية لمصلحة جهة التحكم ونيابةً عنها. البيانات الحساسة (المعدلة): كل بيان شخصي يتعلق بأصل الفرد العرقي أو أصله الإثني، أو معتقده الديني أو الفكري أو السياسي. وكذلك البيانات الأمنية والجنائية، أو بيانات السمات الحيوية التي تحدد الهوية، أو البيانات الوراثية، أو البيانات الصحية، والبيانات التي تدل على أن الفرد مجهول الأبوين أو أحدهما. | Classification step for any contract: identify whether the vendor is a processor (acts on the customer's instructions) or an independent controller. M/148 narrowed "sensitive data": credit data, location data, tribal origin and association membership were in the original list and are no longer; credit data keep a separate regime (Art. 24). A processor becomes a controller if it departs from the controller's instructions (Regulation Art. 17(4)). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 2 | The Law applies to any processing of personal data relating to individuals that takes place in the Kingdom by any means, including processing of personal data of individuals resident in the Kingdom by any entity outside the Kingdom; it includes data of the deceased if it identifies him or a family member. Excluded: an individual's processing for purposes not exceeding personal or family use, provided he does not publish or disclose it (Regulation Art. 2 defines personal and family use). | يُطبق النظام على أي عملية مُعالجة لبيانات شخصية تتعلق بالأفراد تتم في المملكة بأي وسيلة كانت، بما في ذلك معالجة البيانات الشخصية المتعلقة بالأفراد المقيمين في المملكة بأي وسيلة كانت من أي جهة خارج المملكة. | Extraterritorial: a foreign SaaS vendor processing Saudi residents' data is a controller or processor under the Law regardless of contract governing law. A foreign vendor's "we are not subject to Saudi law" position does not hold for PDPL duties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 5 (as amended: "explicit" replaces "written"), 7; Regulation Art. 11, 12 | Except in the cases the Law provides, personal data may not be processed, nor its purpose changed, without the data subject's consent; the Regulations set the conditions of consent, the cases where consent must be explicit, and guardian consent for persons lacking capacity. Consent may be withdrawn at any time. Consent may not be a condition of a service or benefit unless the service is related to the processing consented to. Regulation Art. 11: consent may be in any appropriate form (written, verbal, electronic) if freely given, for clear and specific purposes explained before or at the time of the request, by a person with full capacity, documented so it can be verified later, and separate for each purpose; consent must be explicit for sensitive data, credit data, and decisions based solely on automated processing. Regulation Art. 12: withdrawal must be as easy as giving consent; processing stops without undue delay; withdrawal does not affect prior processing or processing on another basis; the controller must notify recipients and ask them to destroy the data. | فيما عدا الأحوال المنصوص عليها في النظام، لا تجوز معالجة البيانات الشخصية أو تغيير الغرض من معالجتها إلاَّ بعد موافقة صاحبها. وتُبين اللوائح شروط الموافقة، والأحوال التي يجب فيها أن تكون الموافقة صريحة... يجوز لصاحب البيانات الشخصية الرجوع عن الموافقة... في أي وقت. لا يجوز أن تكون الموافقة... شرطاً لإسداء خدمة أو تقديم منفعة، ما لم تكن الخدمة أو المنفعة ذات علاقة بمعالجة البيانات الشخصية التي صدرت الموافقة عليها. | Consent is the default basis, so a contract that relies on the customer's consents should warrant that they meet Regulation Art. 11 (documented, purpose-specific, separate) and allocate who handles withdrawals (Regulation Art. 12(4) requires notifying downstream recipients, which a processor clause must support). The M/148 change from "written" to "explicit" removes the wet-ink concern for sensitive-data consents. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 6 (as amended: paragraph 4 added), 10 (as amended), 15 (as amended); Regulation Art. 16 | Processing does not require consent where: (1) it serves an actual interest of the data subject and contacting him is impossible or difficult; (2) it is under another law or in performance of a prior agreement to which the data subject is party; (3) the controller is a public entity and processing is required for security or judicial purposes; (4) (added by M/148) processing is necessary for the controller's legitimate interests, provided it does not prejudice the data subject's rights or conflict with his interests and the data are not sensitive; the Regulations set the controls. Art. 10 (amended) allows collection from a source other than the data subject and processing for a new purpose on the same grounds plus public availability, vital interests, public health or safety, anonymised data, and (7) legitimate interest on the same conditions. Art. 15 (amended) allows disclosure on the same grounds including (6) legitimate interest. Regulation Art. 16: legitimate interest is not available to public entities; conditions: lawful purpose, balancing, no sensitive data, within the data subject's reasonable expectations; examples: fraud detection, network and information security; a documented legitimate-interest assessment is required before processing. | لا تخضع معالجة البيانات الشخصية للموافقة... في الأحوال الآتية: ... عندما تكون المعالجة بمقتضى نظام آخر أو تنفيذاً لاتفاق سابق يكون صاحب البيانات الشخصية طرفاً فيه... 4- عندما تكون المعالجة ضرورية لتحقيق مصالح مشروعة لجهة التحكم، ما لم يخل ذلك بحقوق صاحب البيانات الشخصية أو يتعارض مع مصالحه ولم تكن تلك البيانات بيانات حساسة. | The two bases a commercial contract usually relies on: performance of an agreement with the data subject (Art. 6(2)) and legitimate interest (Art. 6(4), non-sensitive data only, assessment documented). Employee and customer data processed for contract performance need no consent; analytics, security and fraud prevention can rest on legitimate interest. A vendor contract should identify which basis the controller relies on so the processor's permitted purposes match it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 8 (as amended); Regulation Art. 17(1) | When choosing a processor, the controller must choose one that provides the guarantees necessary to implement the Law and Regulations, and must verify the processor's compliance with the Law and Regulations, without prejudice to the controller's own responsibility to the data subject or the Competent Authority; the Regulations set the rules, including for any subsequent contracts made by the processor. Regulation Art. 17(1): the controller must ensure the processor gives sufficient guarantees and that the agreement with the processor includes: (a) purpose of processing; (b) categories of personal data; (c) duration of processing; (d) the processor's commitment to notify the controller without undue delay of any personal data breach; (e) whether the processor is subject to regulations of other countries and the effect on its compliance with the Law; (f) that the data subject's prior consent is not required for disclosures mandatory under Saudi law, provided the processor notifies the controller of such disclosure; (g) identification of any subcontractors or other party to whom personal data will be disclosed. | على جهة التحكم عند اختيارها جهة المعالجة أن تلتزم باختيار الجهة التي توفر الضمانات اللازمة لتنفيذ أحكام النظام واللوائح، وعليها التحقق من التزام تلك الجهة بأحكام النظام واللوائح، ولا يخل ذلك بمسؤولياتها تجاه صاحب البيانات الشخصية أو الجهة المختصة بحسب الأحوال. وتحدد اللوائح الأحكام اللازمة لذلك، على أن تشتمل على الأحكام المتعلقة بأي تعاقدات لاحقة تقوم بها جهة المعالجة. | This is the mandatory-terms checklist for every Saudi-facing vendor, SaaS or outsourcing agreement where the vendor processes personal data: seven items in Regulation Art. 17(1). A GDPR-style DPA covers most of them but usually lacks item (e) (disclosure of foreign-law exposure, e.g., US CLOUD Act) and item (f) (mandatory Saudi disclosures without consent, with notice to the controller); both must be added. The controller's own liability is not reduced by outsourcing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Regulation Art. 17(2)-(5) | The controller must issue clear instructions to the processor; the processor must notify the controller in writing without undue delay of any breach of those instructions or of Saudi law. The controller must periodically assess the processor's compliance (it may appoint an independent third party) covering processing by the processor or third parties acting for it. A processor that breaches the controller's instructions or the agreement is treated as a controller and is directly accountable. Before engaging a sub-processor the processor must take sufficient guarantees that protection is not reduced, choose only sub-processors giving sufficient guarantees, and obtain the controller's prior acceptance, notifying the controller before contracting and allowing it to object within a period agreed between controller and processor. | — (SDAIA English text; Arabic original of the Regulation not in the source files) | Contract terms to check: instruction-only processing; written notice of deviation; audit or assessment right (or independent assessor); sub-processor prior acceptance with an agreed objection window (general pre-authorisation with a list and a notice period fits the text, silent auto-approval does not `[model knowledge — verify]`); liability allocation reflecting that a deviating processor becomes a controller. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 11 (paragraph 4 as amended), 14; Regulation Art. 19 | The purpose of collection must relate directly to the controller's purposes and not conflict with any legal provision; collection methods must be appropriate, direct, clear, secure and free of deception; content must be adequate and limited to the minimum necessary for the purpose; once data are no longer necessary, collection must stop and what was collected must be destroyed without delay. The controller may not process data without adequate steps to verify accuracy, completeness, currency and relevance. | يجب أن يكون مُحتوى البيانات الشخصية ملائماً ومقصوراً على الحد الأدنى اللازم لتحقيق الغرض من جمعها... إذا اتضح أن البيانات الشخصية التي تجمع لم تعد ضرورية لتحقيق الغرض من حجمها، فعلى جهة التحكم التوقف عن جمعها، وإتلاف ما سبق أن جمعته منها دون تأخير. | Data-minimisation and accuracy are statutory; a vendor's right to collect "any data" for product improvement should be narrowed to the contract purpose. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 12 (as amended), 13 (as amended) | The controller must adopt a privacy policy and make it available to data subjects when collecting their data, covering purpose, content of data collected, method of collection, means of storage, manner of processing and destruction, the data subject's rights and how to exercise them. When collecting directly from the data subject the controller must inform him, at collection, of: the legal basis for collection; the purpose, whether collection is mandatory or optional, and that the data will not be processed inconsistently with the purpose except under Art. 10; the identity and address of the collector; the entities to which the data will be disclosed and whether the data will be transferred, disclosed or processed outside the Kingdom; the consequences of not completing collection; the Art. 4 rights; other elements set by the Regulations. | على جهة التحكم أن تعتمد سياسة للخصوصية، وأن تجعلها متاحة لأصحاب البيانات الشخصية... اتخاذ الوسائل الكافية لإحاطته علماً بالعناصر الآتية عند جمع بياناته: المسوغ النظامي لجمع بياناته الشخصية... الجهة أو الجهات التي سَيُجرى إفصاح البيانات الشخصية إليها... وما إذا كانت البيانات الشخصية ستنقل أو سيفصح عنها أو ستعالج خارج المملكة. | Contract relevance: the controller's notice must name the categories of recipients and say whether data leave the Kingdom, so the vendor agreement must disclose processing locations and sub-processors accurately enough for the controller to comply with Art. 13(4). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 18 (paragraph 1 as amended) | The controller must destroy personal data without delay once the purpose of collection ends; it may retain the data if everything identifying the data subject is removed per the Regulations. It must retain the data after the purpose ends where a legal requirement mandates retention for a set period (destroy at the later of that period or the purpose's end) or where the data are closely connected to a case before a judicial body (destroy after proceedings end). | على جهة التحكم إتلاف البيانات الشخصية بعد انتهاء الغرض من جمعها دون تأخير... على جهة التحكم الاحتفاظ بالبيانات الشخصية حتى بعد انتهاء الغرض من جمعها في الحالتين الآتيتين: إذا توافر مسوغ نظامي يوجب الاحتفاظ بها مدة مُحددة... إذا كانت البيانات الشخصية متصلة اتصالاً وثيقاً بقضية منظورة أمام جهة قضائية. | Termination and exit clauses: require deletion or anonymisation on termination, with a carve-out only for legal-retention and litigation-hold data; open-ended vendor retention "for backup" is inconsistent with "without delay". | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 19; Regulation Art. 23 | The controller must take the organisational, administrative and technical measures necessary to protect personal data, including during transfer, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 23: implement security measures against breach; adopt the National Cybersecurity Authority's controls, standards and rules, or recognised best practice and cybersecurity standards if not bound by the NCA's. | على جهة التحكم اتخاذ ما يلزم من إجراءات ووسائل تنظيمية وإدارية وتقنية تضمن المحافظة على البيانات الشخصية، بما في ذلك عند نقلها. | Security schedule check: reference the NCA Essential Cybersecurity Controls where the customer is NCA-regulated, otherwise a recognised standard (ISO 27001 or equivalent). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 20 (as amended); Regulation Art. 24 | The controller must notify the Competent Authority on becoming aware of a leak, damage or unlawful access to personal data, per the Regulations; and must notify the data subject if the incident may cause harm to his data or conflict with his rights or interests, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 24: notify the Competent Authority within no more than 72 hours of becoming aware, if the incident potentially harms the data or the data subject or conflicts with his rights or interests; the notification must describe the incident (time, date, circumstances, time of awareness), data categories and numbers of affected data subjects, risks and mitigation measures, whether data subjects were notified, and contact details of the controller or DPO; missing information is supplied as soon as possible with reasons; the controller keeps a copy of reports and corrective measures; NCA reporting duties are unaffected; data subjects are notified without undue delay in clear language with a description, risks, contact details and recommendations. | تشعر جهة التحكم الجهة المختصة عند علمها بحدوث تسرب لبيانات شخصية أو تلفها أو وصول غير مشروع إليها؛ وفقاً لما تحدده اللوائح. تشعر جهة التحكم صاحب البيانات الشخصية إذا كان من شأن حدوث تسرب... أن يرتب ضرراً على بياناته أو يتعارض مع حقوقه أو مصالحه. | The 72-hour clock runs from the controller's awareness; the processor's statutory duty is only "without undue delay" (Regulation Art. 17(1)(d)). A vendor agreement should fix a processor-to-controller notice period short enough for the controller to file within 72 hours (24 to 48 hours is the usual ask `[model knowledge — verify]`) and require the Regulation Art. 24(1) content. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 22; Regulation Art. 25 | The controller must assess the effects of processing personal data for any product or service offered to the public, according to its activity, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 25: a written impact assessment is required for processing sensitive data; combining or linking two or more datasets from different sources; large-scale processing of persons lacking capacity, constant monitoring, new technologies, or automated decision-making; and products or services likely to cause serious harm to privacy; minimum content listed; a copy must be given to any processor acting for the controller; re-assessment if harm is indicated. | على جهة التحكم إجراء تقويم للآثار المترتبة على معالجة البيانات الشخصية لأي منتج أو خدمة تقدم للعموم بحسب طبيعة النشاط الذي تمارسه جهة التحكم. | Vendor contracts for AI, analytics, monitoring or profiling tools should include a duty on the vendor to supply the information the controller needs for its impact assessment and to receive a copy of it (Regulation Art. 25(3)). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 24 (paragraph 1 as amended), 25, 26 | Credit data: the Regulations must require verification of the data subject's explicit consent (M/148 replaced "written") for collection, change of purpose, disclosure or publication, and notice to him when a disclosure request is received. Direct marketing: personal contact means may be used to send advertising or awareness material only with the recipient's consent and with a clear opt-out mechanism. Marketing processing is allowed for non-sensitive data collected directly from the data subject with his consent under the Law, per the Regulations. | لا يجوز لجهة التحكم استخدام وسائل الاتصال الشخصية... لأجل إرسال مواد دعائية أو توعوية، إلاَّ وفقاً لما يأتي: أن تؤخذ موافقة المتلقي المستهدف... أن يوفر مرسل المواد آلية واضحة... تُمكن المتلقي المستهدف من إبداء رغبته في التوقف. | Marketing-technology and CRM vendor terms: consent-based sending with opt-out; a vendor's right to use customer contact data for its own marketing requires the data subject's consent, not merely the customer's. Fintech and BNPL contracts: explicit consent flow for credit data. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 29 (as amended by M/148) | (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a controller may transfer personal data outside the Kingdom or disclose it to a party outside the Kingdom for any of these purposes: (a) performance of an obligation under an agreement to which the Kingdom is party; (b) serving the Kingdom's interests; (c) performance of an obligation to which the data subject is party; (d) other purposes set by the Regulations. (2) Conditions: (a) the transfer or disclosure must not prejudice national security or the Kingdom's vital interests; (b) an appropriate level of protection for personal data must exist outside the Kingdom, not lower than that of the Law and Regulations, according to an assessment by the Competent Authority in coordination with the relevant bodies; (c) the transfer or disclosure is limited to the minimum data needed. (3) Paragraph (2) does not apply in cases of extreme necessity to protect the data subject's life or vital interests, or to prevent, examine or treat infection. (4) The Regulations set the rules, standards and procedures, including the cases in which controllers are exempted from conditions (2)(b) and (2)(c), and the controls and procedures for exemption and its withdrawal. | يجوز لجهة التحكم نقل البيانات الشخصية إلى خارج المملكة أو الإفصاح عنها لجهة خارج المملكة، وذلك لتحقيق أيٍّ من الأغراض الآتية: ... ج- إذا كان ذلك تنفيذاً لالتزام يكون صاحب البيانات الشخصية طرفاً فيه. د- إذا كان ذلك تنفيذاً لأغراض أخرى وفق ما تحدده اللوائح. تكون الشروط الواجب توافرها... أ- ألا يترتب على النقل أو الإفصاح مساس بالأمن الوطني أو بمصالح المملكة الحيوية. ب- أن يتوافر مستوى مناسب لحماية البيانات الشخصية في خارج المملكة؛ بما لا يقل عن مستوى الحماية المقرر في النظام واللوائح، وفقاً لنتائج تقويم تجريه الجهة المختصة. ج- أن يقتصر النقل أو الإفصاح على الحد الأدنى من البيانات الشخصية الذي تدعو الحاجة إليه. | The statutory structure since M/148: a permitted purpose (the "other purposes" list is in the Transfer Regulation) plus three conditions, of which (b) is an adequacy test run by SDAIA and can be waived under the Transfer Regulation. The original 2021 requirement for case-by-case Competent Authority approval of each transfer is gone. For contract review: identify where the vendor and its sub-processors process data, and require the transfer mechanism to be stated in the agreement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Transfer Regulation (not read) — mechanisms under Art. 29(4) | Under the Regulation on Personal Data Transfer Outside the Kingdom (as amended September 2024): "other purposes" include central processing within a group, provision of services or products to the data subject, scientific research, and other listed purposes; where the destination lacks a SDAIA adequacy decision, a controller may transfer under appropriate safeguards, namely SDAIA's Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Common Rules for intra-group transfers, or a certification of compliance, with a documented transfer risk assessment in prescribed cases; narrow exceptions exist for specific situations (e.g., performance of a contract with the data subject, vital interests). | — | Everything in this row is model knowledge because the Transfer Regulation was not among the source files. A contract reviewer must: (1) confirm whether the destination is on SDAIA's adequacy list; (2) if not, require SDAIA SCCs (not EU SCCs) or BCRs; (3) confirm a transfer risk assessment was done where required; (4) not rely on the EU adequacy concept. Which of these applies, and the current SCC template, must be checked against the Regulation. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Localisation (no PDPL article) | The PDPL contains no general data-localisation or in-Kingdom hosting requirement; localisation obligations, where they exist, come from sector regulators and the NCA (e.g., the NCA's cloud controls and government-data rules, SAMA's outsourcing rules, CST's cloud framework and their data-classification levels). | — | For a SaaS or cloud contract, the localisation question is answered by the customer's sector regulator and the data classification, not by the PDPL; a reviewer should ask which sector rules apply before demanding Saudi hosting. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Art. 30 (as amended); Regulation Art. 32 | The Competent Authority supervises the Law and Regulations, without prejudice to the Saudi Central Bank's powers. The Regulations set the cases in which a controller must appoint or designate one or more persons as personal data protection officer and their responsibilities. Controllers must cooperate with the Competent Authority, which may request documents and information, use other bodies for support, set monitoring tools including a national register of controllers, provide services through that register for a fee, and delegate some tasks. Regulation Art. 32: a DPO is mandatory where the controller is a public entity providing services involving large-scale processing; where the controller's primary activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects; or where its core activities involve processing sensitive data. The DPO may be an executive, an employee or an external contractor; responsibilities include being the contact point for the Competent Authority, supervising impact assessments and audits, enabling data-subject rights, notifying breaches, maintaining records and handling violations. The Competent Authority issues DPO appointment rules. | تحدد اللوائح الأحوال التي يجب فيها على جهة التحكم تعيين أو تحديد شخص (أو أكثر) يتولى دور مسؤول حماية البيانات الشخصية، وتبيّن اللوائح كذلك مسؤولياته. | Diligence and vendor onboarding: ask whether the counterparty falls in a mandatory-DPO case and who the DPO is; the contract's notice clause for data matters should route to that person. The DPO may be outsourced. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 31; Regulation Art. 33, 34 | The controller must keep records of processing activities for a period set by the Regulations, available to the Competent Authority on request, containing at least: controller contact details; purpose; categories of data subjects; recipients of disclosure; whether data are transferred or disclosed outside the Kingdom; expected retention period. Regulation Art. 33: written records kept throughout processing and for five years after processing ends, accurate and current, produced to the Competent Authority on request; minimum content adds DPO information, data categories, retention periods per category, categories of recipients, description of transfers outside the Kingdom with legal basis and recipients, and security measures; SDAIA provides templates. Regulation Art. 34: SDAIA issues rules for registration in the National Register of Controllers, specifying which controllers must register. | تحتفظ جهة التحكم بسجلات لمدة تحددها اللوائح لأنشطة معالجة البيانات الشخصية... ما إذا جرى (أو سَيُجرى) نقل البيانات الشخصية إلى خارج المملكة أو الإفصاح عنها لجهة خارج المملكة. | Vendor contracts should oblige the processor to supply the information the controller needs for its record (locations, sub-processors, retention) and to keep its own records. Art. 32 of the Law (mandatory registration with an annual fee up to SAR 100,000) was repealed by M/148; registration now depends on SDAIA's rules under Regulation Art. 34, which were not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 33 (as amended) | The Competent Authority sets the requirements for practising commercial, professional or non-profit activities related to personal data protection in the Kingdom; may license bodies to issue accreditation certificates to controllers and processors; may license bodies to audit or examine processing activities; and sets the tools and mechanisms for monitoring compliance by entities outside the Kingdom that process data of residents, and the procedures for enforcing the Law outside the Kingdom. | تتولى الجهة المختصة تحديد الأدوات والآليات المناسبة لمتابعة التزام الجهات التي في خارج المملكة بالتزاماتها المقررة بموجب أحكام النظام واللوائح عند معالجتها لبيانات شخصية متعلقة بالأفراد المقيمين في المملكة. | M/148 removed the original Art. 33(2) obligation on every foreign controller to appoint a SDAIA-licensed representative in the Kingdom; the amended text leaves foreign-entity oversight to SDAIA's tools. A foreign vendor's contract need not therefore name a Saudi representative under the Law as it stands, although SDAIA may set such a mechanism by rule `[model knowledge — verify]`. SDAIA-licensed audit and certification bodies exist under paragraphs (2)-(3); a certification can be asked for as evidence of Art. 8 "guarantees". | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 35 (as amended) | Criminal offence: whoever discloses or publishes sensitive data in breach of the Law with intent to harm the data subject or to obtain a personal benefit is punished with imprisonment up to two years and/or a fine up to SAR 3 million. The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes before the competent court; the court may double the fine on recidivism, up to twice the maximum. | يُعاقب بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (سنتين) وبغرامة لا تزيد على (ثلاثة ملايين) ريال، أو بإحدى هاتين العقوبتين؛ كل من أفصح عن بيانات حساسة أو نشرها مخالفاً أحكام النظام إذا كان ذلك بقصد الإضرار بصاحب البيانات أو بقصد تحقيق منفعة شخصية. | The original 2021 text also criminalised unlawful cross-border transfer (up to one year and SAR 1 million); M/148 removed that offence, so unlawful transfer is now an administrative matter under Art. 36. The remaining offence is intent-based and aimed at individuals (e.g., a rogue employee). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 36 (paragraph 2 as amended), 37, 38 | Administrative penalties for any other breach of the Law or Regulations by a private natural or juristic person: warning or a fine up to SAR 5 million; the fine may be doubled for repeat breaches up to twice the maximum (SAR 10 million). A committee formed by the head of the Competent Authority (at least three members including a technical specialist and a legal adviser) imposes them according to type, gravity and impact, subject to approval by the head of the Authority; decisions may be challenged before the competent court. Authority staff have inspection and seizure powers and may call on criminal-enforcement bodies. The court may confiscate proceeds; the court or committee may order publication of the judgment or decision summary at the offender's expense once final. | تُعاقَب بالإنذار أو بغرامة لا تزيد على (خمسة ملايين) ريال، كلُّ شخصية ذات صفة طبيعية أو اعتبارية خاصة -مشمولة بأحكام النظام- خالفت أيًّا من أحكام النظام أو اللوائح. وتجوز مضاعفة عقوبة الغرامة في حالة تكرار المخالفة حتى لو ترتب عليها تجاوز الحد الأقصى لها على ألا تتجاوز ضعف هذا الحد. | Exposure ceiling for contract liability caps and indemnities: SAR 5 million per breach, SAR 10 million on repeat, plus publication. Both controller and processor are "persons covered by the Law" and can be fined directly. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 40, 41 | Without prejudice to the penalties, anyone harmed by a breach of the Law or Regulations may claim compensation before the competent court for material or moral damage proportionate to the harm. Everyone who has carried out processing must keep data secrets confidential even after the end of his employment or contractual relationship. | لمن لحقه ضرر -نتيجةَ ارتكاب أي من المخالفات المنصوص عليها في النظام أو اللوائح- حق المطالبة أمام المحكمة المختصة بالتعويض عن الضرر المادي أو المعنوي. يلتزم كل من باشر عملاً من أعمال معالجة البيانات الشخصية بالمحافظة على الأسرار المتعلقة بالبيانات حتى بعد انتهاء علاقته الوظيفية أو التعاقدية. | Data-subject compensation claims are a third-party liability that indemnity and cap clauses must address (moral damage is expressly recoverable). Confidentiality survives termination by statute, supporting perpetual confidentiality for personal data in NDAs and DPAs. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 4 (as amended), 9, 21; Regulation Art. 3 (note only) | Data subjects have the rights to be informed of the legal basis and purpose of collection; to access their data; to obtain a copy in a readable, clear format; to correction, completion or updating; and to destruction of data no longer needed, all per the Regulations. Controllers may set periods and limits for access in stated cases. Requests must be answered within the period and by the means set by the Regulations: Regulation Art. 3 gives 30 days, extendable by 30 days with notice where effort is disproportionate or requests are multiple, with identity verification and a record of all requests. | يكون لصاحب البيانات الشخصية... الحقوق الآتية: الحق في العلم... الحق في وصوله إلى بياناته الشخصية... الحق في طلب الحصول على بياناته الشخصية... بصيغة مقروءة وواضحة... الحق في طلب تصحيح... الحق في طلب إتلاف. | Note only for this file: the processor clause must oblige the vendor to assist the controller in meeting the 30-day deadline. The privacy plugin holds the full rights treatment. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
| Art. 42, 43 (as amended); Council of Ministers Resolution 98 items Second and Fourth | The head of the Competent Authority issues the Regulations within 720 days of the Law's issue, after coordination with the ministries and authorities named. The Law is in force 720 days after publication. Council of Ministers Resolution 98: SDAIA is the Competent Authority for two years, during which transfer of supervision to the National Data Management Office is to be considered in light of implementation results and sector maturity; controllers must regularise within one year of the effective date, extendable by the Competent Authority. | تكون الجهة المختصة هي الهيئة السعودية للبيانات والذكاء الاصطناعي، وذلك لمدة سنتين، ينظر خلالها... في نقل اختصاص الإشراف على تطبيق أحكام النظام ولوائحه التنفيذية إلى مكتب إدارة البيانات الوطنية. يُعمل بالنظام بعد (سبعمائة وعشرين) يوماً من التاريخ الذي نشر به في الجريدة الرسمية. | Effective 14 September 2023; grace period to 14 September 2024. SDAIA has continued to act as Competent Authority after the two-year window; whether supervision has formally moved to the National Data Management Office (an SDAIA unit) is not shown in the sources and must be confirmed `[model knowledge — verify]`. Contract notices and filings should be addressed to SDAIA unless counsel confirms otherwise. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (status of the NDMO transfer: `[model knowledge — verify]`) |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- Edition of the Implementing Regulation: the SDAIA English file has no edition date or decision number in its front matter; its Art. 1 cites M/148 (as both the 2023 and 2024 editions do). The content matches the post-M/148 regulation (legitimate-interest article, 72-hour breach rule, three DPO cases, five-year record retention). Confirm whether the file is the September 2024 amended edition and whether any provision relied on above (Art. 11, 16, 17, 24, 25, 32, 33) changed in that amendment.
- The Regulation on Personal Data Transfer Outside the Kingdom was not read: confirm the current list of permitted "other purposes" (Art. 29(1)(d)), the adequacy list, the SDAIA Standard Contractual Clauses (whether mandatory in form), Binding Common Rules, certification, the transfer-risk-assessment trigger, and the exemption and withdrawal procedure under Art. 29(4).
- Whether SDAIA has issued rules under Regulation Art. 34 requiring specific controllers to register in the National Register of Controllers, and under Regulation Art. 32(4) on DPO appointment, and whether either applies to foreign processors.
- Whether the Competent Authority has issued rules or guidance treating processor-to-controller breach notice as subject to a fixed deadline (the Regulation says "without undue delay").
- Whether SDAIA has issued a mechanism under amended Art. 33(4) requiring foreign entities to appoint a local representative or register, replacing the repealed Art. 33(2).
- Formal status of the Competent Authority after the two-year period in Council of Ministers Resolution 98 item Second (SDAIA versus the National Data Management Office), and the identity of the enforcement committee under Art. 36(2).
- Interaction with the E-Commerce Law Art. 5 (consent for disclosure of consumer data) and with SAMA and CST sector rules for regulated controllers (Resolution 98 items Sixth and Seventh contemplate memoranda of understanding, not read).
- Arabic original of the Implementing Regulation was not read; the SDAIA English was used and is an authority document, not the Bureau of Experts translation.

## How skills should use this file

- `review` (router), `vendor-agreement-review`, `saas-msa-review` (commercial-legal): load at the data-protection clause step whenever the counterparty will process personal data of Saudi residents or the customer is a Saudi entity. Check the agreement against the Art. 8 / Regulation Art. 17(1) seven-item list and Regulation Art. 17(2)-(5) (instructions, deviation notice, assessment right, sub-processor acceptance), Art. 20 / Regulation Art. 24 (breach notice content and the 72-hour controller deadline), Art. 18 (deletion on termination), Art. 29 (transfer purpose and conditions), Art. 31 / Regulation Art. 33 (information for records), and Art. 36 and 40 (exposure for caps and indemnities). Cite the row for each finding.
- `nda-review` (commercial-legal): cite Art. 41 (statutory survival of confidentiality) and Art. 1 (personal data within "confidential information") when the NDA covers personal data; cite Art. 8 if the NDA in substance allows processing on the discloser's behalf.
- `escalation-flagger`, `stakeholder-summary` (commercial-legal): cite Art. 36 (SAR 5 million, SAR 10 million on repeat) and Art. 35 (criminal exposure for sensitive-data disclosure) when quantifying risk.
- `diligence-issue-extraction`, `tabular-review`, `entity-compliance` (corporate-legal): for a Saudi target, check for a privacy policy (Art. 12), records of processing (Art. 31), a DPO where mandatory (Regulation Art. 32), impact assessments where triggered (Regulation Art. 25), breach reports kept (Regulation Art. 24(3)), and transfer mechanisms for offshore processing (Art. 29). Cite the row for each finding.
- `material-contract-schedule`, `integration-management` (corporate-legal): cite Art. 29 and the transfer-regulation row (model knowledge) when post-closing integration moves Saudi personal data to a foreign group system; cite Art. 5 and Regulation Art. 12 if the integration changes the purpose of processing.
- `hiring-review`, `termination-review`, `policy-drafting`, `handbook-updates` (employment-legal): cite Art. 6(2) (contract performance basis for employee data), Art. 13 (notice at collection), Art. 18 (destruction after purpose), and Art. 29 (transfer of employee data to a foreign parent) — noting that the transfer mechanism row is model knowledge.
- Computed dates: effective date = publication (2021-09-24) + 720 days = 2023-09-14 (Art. 43 as amended, settled); grace period end = effective date + 1 year = 2024-09-14 (Resolution 98 item Fourth, settled); the statement that SDAIA began enforcement on that date is `[model knowledge — verify]`. Skills must show the formula and attach the tag of the weakest input.
- Stop rule: if a skill needs a rule that is missing from this table, depends on the Transfer Regulation or on SDAIA rules not read (adequacy list, SCC form, registration and DPO rules), or is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, it must say so and stop or flag for Saudi counsel. It must not state a transfer mechanism as compliant, name the current Competent Authority as anything other than SDAIA without confirmation, or fill any gap from memory.

