FILE electronic-transactions-law.md
1: # Electronic Transactions Law — نظام التعاملات الإلكترونية
2: 
3: <!-- Instrument file for references/jurisdictions/ksa. Written 2026-09-04 from the Arabic text on laws.boe.gov.sa. This is the file that answers "is an electronic signature valid to execute a contract in Saudi Arabia". Companion: e-commerce-law.md (consumer online contracts). -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Electronic Transactions Law / نظام التعاملات الإلكترونية |
11: | Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/18 of 8/3/1428H (27 March 2007), approving Council of Ministers Resolution No. 80 of 7/3/1428H |
12: | Issuing authority | Ministry of Communications and Information Technology (policy, Art. 15(1)); Communications and Information Technology Commission (now the Communications, Space and Technology Commission, CST `[model knowledge — verify]`) for licensing certification service providers and enforcement (Art. 15(2), 25); National Center for Digital Certification (NCDC, المركز الوطني للتصديق الرقمي) established by Art. 16. Implementing Regulation issued by the Minister on the Commission's proposal (Art. 30) — not read |
13: | Effective date | 2007-08-15 (Art. 31: "after 120 days from publication in the Official Gazette"; published 29/3/1428H = 17 April 2007; day 120 is 15 August 2007) |
14: | Amendment history | Royal Decree M/57 of 1/8/1436H (about 19 May 2015, Hijri converted) replaced Art. 24 (penalties): same maximum penalty (SAR 5 million and/or 5 years) with an added power for the court to order publication of the judgment summary at the convicted person's expense. Read on 2026-09-04 from the in-force "تعديلات المادة" block of the regenerated source file (the article is headed [معدلة]; its body is the pre-2015 wording, the amendment block is the wording in force), cross-checked against the live portal pop-up |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/6f509360-2c39-4358-ae2a-a9a700f2ed16/1 |
16: | Official translation | Portal lists an English page (`/2`) and a "Translated document" attachment; not among the source files and not read. English renderings below are the writer's from the Arabic |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page read in full. Issue date 8/3/1428H (27/03/2007); publication 29/3/1428H (17/04/2007); status "ساري"; `articles marked as amended on the portal: 1` (Art. 24, by Royal Decree M/57 of 1/8/1436H). No other amending instrument named. The Implementing Regulation (CITC, 2008 `[model knowledge — verify]`) was not read |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law gives legal effect to electronic transactions, electronic records and electronic signatures in the public and private sectors (Art. 2), on a technology-neutral definition of "electronic signature" (Art. 1(14)), and layers on top of it a licensed certification regime (certification service providers licensed by the Commission, digital certificates supervised by the NCDC, Art. 15-22). It applies to all electronic transactions and signatures except (Art. 3) transactions relating to personal status (الأحوال الشخصية) and the issuance of deeds (صكوك) for dispositions of real property, unless the responsible authority allows those to be done electronically. The statute does not exclude powers of attorney, negotiable instruments or wills in terms; any such exclusion would come from the Regulation or from the sector regulator (Ministry of Justice for notarised instruments), which were not read. Nobody is compelled to transact electronically (Art. 4). The Law does not itself regulate consumer online sales (E-Commerce Law), nor evidence generally (the Evidence Law of 1443H, not read, contains its own chapter on digital evidence `[model knowledge — verify]`).
23: 
24: ## Rules
25: 
26: Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.
27: 
28:  |  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Practical effect  |  Tag  | 
29:  | --- | --- | --- | --- | 
30:  |  Art. 1(10), (13), (14), (17), (21)  |  "Electronic transaction": any exchange, communication, contracting or other procedure concluded or performed, wholly or partly, by electronic means. "Electronic record": data created, sent, received, transmitted or stored by electronic means and retrievable in an intelligible form. "Electronic signature": electronic data included in, attached to or logically associated with an electronic transaction, used to establish the identity of the signatory, his approval of the transaction, and to detect any alteration after signing. "Digital certificate": an electronic document issued by a certification service provider confirming the identity of the holder of a signature system and containing signature-verification data. "Certification service provider": a person licensed to issue digital certificates or related services.  |  The signature definition is functional and technology-neutral: a typed name, a drawn signature on a platform, or a PKI certificate can all be an "electronic signature" if they identify the signatory, show assent and allow alteration to be detected. The certified tier (digital certificate from a licensed provider) is a subset with stronger evidentiary presumptions (Art. 14(3)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
31:  |  Art. 3  |  The Law applies to electronic transactions and signatures, excluding: (1) transactions relating to personal status; (2) issuance of deeds for dispositions of real property — unless the authority responsible for those transactions permits them to be done electronically under controls agreed with the Ministry.  |  Commercial contracts (MSAs, NDAs, SaaS, vendor agreements, employment contracts) are within scope. Real-estate title deeds and personal-status instruments are outside it unless the Ministry of Justice permits electronic issuance; in practice the Ministry of Justice's Najiz platform now issues real-estate deeds and notarised instruments electronically under its own rules, which are not this Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. Powers of attorney are not excluded by the statute, but notarised powers of attorney are issued through Najiz notaries under Ministry of Justice rules `[model knowledge — verify]`.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
32:  |  Art. 4  |  No person is obliged by the Law to deal electronically without his consent, which may be express or implied. A government body's consent to electronic dealing must be express, subject to its own requirements. A person may set additional conditions of his own for accepting electronic transactions and signatures, provided they do not conflict with the Law.  |  Two drafting consequences: (1) include an electronic-signature and counterparts clause so consent is express; (2) a party may validly stipulate a higher standard (e.g., certified signatures only) in the contract. With a government counterparty, electronic execution needs its express consent and compliance with its platform requirements (e.g., Etimad for procurement `[model knowledge — verify]`).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
33:  |  Art. 5  |  Electronic transactions, records and signatures have binding effect; their validity or enforceability may not be denied, nor their enforcement prevented, because they were done wholly or partly electronically, provided they were done in accordance with the conditions of the Law. Information produced by an electronic transaction does not lose effect merely because its details are accessible within the originator's data system and reference is made to how to access it.  |  This is the core non-discrimination rule: an electronically signed commercial contract is valid and enforceable in Saudi Arabia. The proviso ("in accordance with the conditions of the Law") is what separates a simple electronic signature (valid, but proved on the facts) from a certified one (valid with statutory presumptions). Incorporation by reference to terms held in the originator's system (e.g., online terms) is recognised by paragraph (2).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
34:  |  Art. 6, 7  |  Where a law requires a document or information to be retained, an electronic record satisfies it if (a) it is kept in the form in which it was created, sent or received, or in a form that can be shown to reproduce that content; (b) it remains retrievable for later reference; (c) information identifying originator, addressee, and date and time of sending and receipt is kept with it. A person may meet these requirements through a third party's services, at his own responsibility. Where a law requires a document to be in writing, providing it electronically satisfies that requirement if Art. 6(1) is met.  |  "In writing" requirements in Saudi statutes (e.g., the Labor Law's written employment contract, non-compete clauses; arbitration agreements) are met by an electronic record that satisfies Art. 6(1). Keep the platform's audit trail (originator, addressee, timestamps) with the signed PDF; it is part of the statutory retention standard, not an optional extra. Whether the Arbitration Law's writing requirement is satisfied by electronic means is settled by that Law's own Art. 9, not here `[model knowledge — verify]`.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
35:  |  Art. 8, 9  |  An electronic record counts as an original when technical means and conditions confirm the integrity of its information from the time it was first created in final form and allow it to be displayed on demand (means set by the Regulation). An electronic transaction or signature is accepted as evidence (دليل) if its record meets Art. 8; otherwise it may be accepted as a presumption (قرينة) in evidence. Each of the electronic transaction, signature and record is deemed reliable and unaltered since creation unless the contrary appears. In assessing weight, the court considers the reliability of the method of creation, storage or communication and the possibility of alteration; the method of preserving integrity; and the method by which the originator was identified.  |  Two-tier evidentiary treatment. A platform-signed contract with tamper-evident sealing and an audit trail is likely to meet Art. 8 and is admissible as evidence, with a rebuttable presumption of integrity; a bare scanned signature or e-mail assent is still admissible but only as a presumption weighed under Art. 9(4). Reviewers should ask what the platform's completion certificate shows (identity verification method, IP, timestamps, hash) because those are the Art. 9(4) factors.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
36:  |  Art. 10, 11  |  Offer and acceptance may be expressed by electronic transaction, and the contract is valid and enforceable when made in accordance with the Law; it does not lose validity or enforceability merely because it was made by one or more electronic records. Contracts may be formed between automated data systems, or between an automated system and a natural person who knows or should know he is dealing with an automated system, and are valid despite no direct human intervention.  |  Click-wrap and API-driven contracting (SaaS sign-ups, automated ordering) are valid. For B2B SaaS terms accepted by clicking, the contract exists; the evidentiary question is who clicked and with what authority (Art. 9(4)(c), Art. 12).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
37:  |  Art. 12, 13  |  An electronic record is attributed to the originator if sent by him, by someone on his behalf, or by an automated system he programmed to act for him; an intermediary is not the originator. A record is sent when it enters a data system outside the originator's control; the Regulation sets the technical criteria and the time and place of dispatch and receipt. Acknowledgement of receipt may be in any form set by the Regulation unless the parties agree a form.  |  Notice clauses can rely on e-mail or platform delivery; state expressly when an electronic notice is deemed received (Art. 13(2) lets the parties agree the acknowledgement form). Attribution through an employee's or agent's account binds the originator only if the sender acted "on his behalf"; authority remains a fact question.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
38:  |  Art. 14(1), (2)  |  Where a handwritten signature is required on a document or contract, an electronic signature made in accordance with the Law satisfies the requirement; the electronic signature is treated as a handwritten signature and has the same legal effects. A person making an electronic signature must comply with the Law and the controls, conditions and specifications set by the Regulation, take the precautions the Regulation prescribes against unlawful use of his signature-creation data, and report unlawful use to the certification service provider.  |  Statutory equivalence of electronic and wet-ink signatures for contracts. The condition "made in accordance with the Law" and the Regulation's "controls, conditions and specifications" are where the certified-signature regime bites; the Regulation was not read, so the exact technical conditions for full equivalence cannot be stated from this file.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
39:  |  Art. 14(3), (4), (5)  |  Where an electronic signature is produced in any judicial or legal proceeding, it is presumed, unless the contrary is proved or the parties agree otherwise, that (a) the signature is that of the person identified in the digital certificate; (b) it was applied by that person for the purpose stated in the certificate; (c) the transaction has not been altered since signature. If the signature does not meet the controls and conditions of the Law and Regulation, these presumptions do not apply to the signature or the associated transaction. A person relying on another's electronic signature must exercise due care to verify it using the signature-verification data per the Regulation.  |  The presumptions are framed around a digital certificate, i.e. a signature backed by a certificate from a licensed provider. A signature without such a certificate is still valid (Art. 5, 14(1)) but the party relying on it bears the burden of proving identity, assent and integrity under Art. 9. For high-value or contested-risk documents, a Saudi-certified signature shifts that burden to the signatory. Note the parties can vary the presumptions by agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
40:  |  Art. 15, 18, 19, 20, 21  |  The Commission licenses, renews, suspends and revokes certification service provider licences and supervises compliance (Art. 15(2)). A provider must be licensed before operating; issue, deliver and preserve certificates per its licence and the Regulation; use reliable means and protect against forgery; keep a continuously accessible database of issued, suspended and revoked certificates; keep information confidential; collect personal data from the applicant directly or with his written consent; and hand over records to the Commission on cessation (Art. 18). It may not cease, assign or merge without the Commission's prior written approval (Art. 19). It guarantees the accuracy of certified information at delivery and the link between holder and data, and is liable to anyone who relied in good faith (Art. 20). It must revoke or suspend on the holder's request or in Regulation cases, inform the holder immediately, and is liable for damage from failure to suspend or revoke (Art. 21).  |  "Saudi-certified" means a certificate from a provider licensed by the Commission (CST) and operating under the NCDC's root. Reliance on such a certificate is backed by statutory provider liability. A foreign platform that issues its own certificates is not a licensed provider unless licensed here or accredited under Art. 17.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
41:  |  Art. 16, 17  |  A National Center for Digital Certification is established in the Ministry to supervise the issuance and management of digital certificates; the Minister may assign its tasks to the Commission or another body. The Center accredits digital certificates issued by foreign bodies outside the Kingdom, and such certificates are then treated like domestic ones, under controls and procedures set by the Regulation.  |  A foreign-certified signature (e.g., an EU qualified certificate) gets Saudi presumptions only if the NCDC has accredited the issuer. Absent accreditation it is a simple electronic signature under Art. 5 and 9. The NCDC's functions were later moved under the Digital Government Authority `[model knowledge — verify]`; the list of accredited foreign issuers was not read.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
42:  |  Art. 22  |  The certificate holder is responsible for the integrity and confidentiality of his signature system; every use of it is deemed to issue from him; he must comply with the certificate's terms of use, give correct information to the provider and relying parties, notify the provider of any change in certificate information or loss of confidentiality, and may not reuse signature elements of a suspended or revoked certificate with another provider.  |  Deemed-use rule: a company whose authorised signatory's certified signature is misused by an insider is bound unless it can rebut under Art. 14(3). Internal controls over signing credentials are a legal, not only an IT, concern.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
43:  |  Art. 23, 24 (as amended by RD M/57 of 1436H), 25-27  |  Offences include operating as a certification service provider without licence; misuse or disclosure of certificate-applicant information; creating, publishing or using a certificate or signature for a fraudulent or unlawful purpose; forging an electronic record, signature or certificate or knowingly using one; knowingly giving false information to a provider or relying party; accessing, copying or seizing another's signature system without authority; impersonation in certificate applications; publishing forged, revoked or suspended certificates. Penalty: fine up to SAR 5 million and/or imprisonment up to 5 years, with possible confiscation of equipment; the court may order publication of the judgment summary at the offender's expense after the judgment becomes final. The Commission investigates and seizes; cases go to the Public Prosecution; an injured person keeps a civil claim for damages.  |  Forging or misusing an electronic signature is a criminal offence with a five-year ceiling, which supports the practical reliability of platform signatures: a counterparty that later disowns its signature is exposed to Art. 23(6)-(7) if it lies.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
44:  |  Art. 28, 29  |  The Law does not prejudice other laws, in particular intellectual property rights and international agreements to which the Kingdom is party. Ministry, Commission and Center staff must keep confidential the information of providers and their customers.  |  Sector rules (banking, capital markets, government procurement) may add their own execution requirements on top of this Law.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
45:  |  Practice note (no article)  |  Position of DocuSign, Adobe Sign and similar foreign platforms versus Saudi-certified signatures.  |  (1) A contract signed on DocuSign or a similar platform is an electronic transaction bearing an electronic signature within Art. 1(14) and is valid and enforceable between the parties under Art. 5, 10 and 14(1); Saudi commercial courts have accepted such contracts, weighing the platform's audit trail under Art. 9 `[model knowledge — verify]`. (2) Such a signature does not carry the Art. 14(3) presumptions unless the signing certificate was issued by a Commission-licensed provider or an NCDC-accredited foreign issuer (Art. 17); the relying party must prove identity and integrity on the facts `[model knowledge — verify]`. (3) For government counterparties, notarised instruments (powers of attorney, real-estate deeds, corporate resolutions requiring notarisation), and filings with the Ministry of Commerce, execution runs through the relevant portal (Najiz, Absher/Nafath identity, Qiwa for employment contracts, Etimad for procurement), which use Saudi digital identity rather than a foreign platform `[model knowledge — verify]`. (4) Practical checklist for a commercial contract: include an electronic-execution and counterparts clause (Art. 4(3)); keep the completion certificate and audit trail (Art. 6(1)(c), 9(4)); for high-value or litigation-prone documents, or where the counterparty is a Saudi state entity, prefer a Saudi-certified signature or wet ink `[model knowledge — verify]`.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]`  | 
46: 
47: ## Open questions for a local practitioner
48: 
49: - The Implementing Regulation (Art. 30) was not read. It sets the technical conditions under which a signature is "made in accordance with the Law" (Art. 14(2)), the retention and original-record conditions (Art. 6(3), 8), the dispatch and receipt rules (Art. 13), and the licensing and foreign-accreditation procedures (Art. 15, 17). Whether any additional instruments (powers of attorney, negotiable instruments, wills) are excluded by the Regulation must be checked there; the statute excludes only personal-status transactions and real-property deeds.
50: - Which foreign certificate issuers, if any, the NCDC has accredited under Art. 17, and whether a widely used foreign platform's certificates are among them.
51: - The current allocation of the NCDC's functions (Ministry, CST, or Digital Government Authority) and the current name of the Commission.
52: - How the Commercial Courts treat platform-signed contracts in practice: whether an audit-trail certificate is routinely accepted as meeting Art. 8, and whether courts require the platform provider to authenticate it.
53: - Interaction with the Evidence Law (1443H) chapter on digital evidence: which of the two instruments governs admissibility and weight when they differ.
54: - Whether the Ministry of Justice has issued permissions under Art. 3 for electronic real-property deeds (the Najiz practice suggests so) and the scope of those permissions.
55: 
56: ## How skills should use this file
57: 
58: - `written-consent` (corporate-legal): load at the execution-formalities step when a Saudi company's resolution, consent or contract is to be signed electronically. Cite Art. 5 and 14(1) for validity, Art. 14(3)-(4) for the presumption gap between certified and simple signatures, Art. 4(2) for state-entity counterparties, and Art. 3 for the excluded instruments. Where the resolution must be notarised or filed with the Ministry of Commerce, the practice-note row applies and is `[model knowledge — verify]`: the skill must flag, not assert.
59: - `review` (router), `vendor-agreement-review`, `saas-msa-review`, `nda-review` (commercial-legal): load at the execution and counterparts clause check. Cite Art. 4(3) (parties may set their own e-signature conditions), Art. 6-7 (writing and retention requirements satisfied electronically), Art. 10-11 (click-wrap and automated contracting valid), Art. 12-13 (electronic notices and deemed dispatch).
60: - `hiring-review`, `policy-drafting`, `handbook-updates` (employment-legal): cite Art. 7 and 14(1) when an employment contract or policy acknowledgement is to be signed electronically; the Qiwa contract-authentication practice is model knowledge and must be flagged.
61: - `closing-checklist` (corporate-legal): cite Art. 3 for documents that cannot be executed under this Law (real-property deeds) and the practice-note row for notarised instruments.
62: - `diligence-issue-extraction`, `tabular-review` (corporate-legal): when a data-room contract is platform-signed, record whether the completion certificate is present (Art. 6(1)(c), 8, 9(4)) as a finding.
63: - Stop rule: if a skill needs a rule that is missing from this table, depends on the Regulation, or is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (all platform-specific and portal-specific statements, the NCDC accreditation list, court practice), it must say so and stop or flag for Saudi counsel. It must not state that a particular platform's signatures carry the Art. 14(3) presumptions.

FILE arbitration-law.md
1: # Law of Arbitration — نظام التحكيم
2: 
3: <!-- Copied from references/jurisdictions/_TEMPLATE-instrument.md. Covers the Law, its Implementing Regulations, and the SCCA Arbitration Rules 2023 as the institutional layer. -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Law of Arbitration / نظام التحكيم; Implementing Regulations of the Law of Arbitration / اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام التحكيم; SCCA Arbitration Rules (second edition, effective 1 May 2023) with Appendix I (costs and fees), Appendix II (Expedited Procedure Rules), Appendix III (Emergency Arbitrator Procedure Rules) and Appendix IV (Online Dispute Resolution Procedure Rules) / قواعد التحكيم للمركز السعودي للتحكيم التجاري |
11: | Issuing instrument | Law: Royal Decree No. M/34 of 24/5/1433H (2012-04-16), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 156 of 17/5/1433H. Regulations: Council of Ministers Decision No. 541 of 26/8/1438H (2017-05-22). SCCA Rules: adopted by the Saudi Center for Commercial Arbitration (المركز السعودي للتحكيم التجاري), a licensed arbitral institution, not a statutory instrument. |
12: | Issuing authority | Law and Regulations: Council of Ministers (the Regulations were prepared by the Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of Commerce and Industry in coordination with the Supreme Judicial Council, Decision 156 ¶2). SCCA Rules: SCCA (sadr.org). |
13: | Effective date | Law: 2012-07-08 (Art. 58: 30 days after publication; portal shows publication 18/7/1433H = 2012-06-08). Regulations: 2018-11-29 (Regs Art. 19: effective on publication; portal shows publication 21/3/1440H). SCCA Rules: 2023-05-01, applying to arbitrations commenced on or after that date (Rules Art. 2(2)). |
14: | Amendment history | Law: (i) Royal Decree No. M/8 of 18/1/1443H deleted Art. 24(1) (the separate arbitrator-fee contract and its deposit); (ii) Royal Decree No. M/21 of 26/1/1447H amended Art. 10(1) (capacity to agree to arbitrate) and Art. 50(1)(b) (annulment ground of incapacity, wording change). Regulations: Council of Ministers Decision No. 249 of 7/4/1444H deleted Regs Art. 7 (deposit of the arbitrator contract with the SCCA). SCCA Rules: the 2023 edition replaced the first edition of 1 May 2016 (Rules introduction). |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | Law: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/5535039e-13da-43f6-8f53-a9a700f26485/1 ; Regulations: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/12752a60-2a4a-4cab-a05d-a9a700f274a5/1 ; SCCA Rules (English official text): https://www.sadr.org/public/upload/pdf-files/2023-Arbitration-Rules-En.pdf |
16: | Official translation | Law and Regulations: the portal lists an English language page ("الإنجليزية (English)") and a "Translated document" attachment; neither was read today (no `boe-en` file was provided). SCCA Rules: the English text is an official SCCA text and was read; the Arabic SCCA text was not. |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Law, Arabic, Arts. 1–58 including the portal's in-force amendment blocks for Arts. 10, 24 and 50; Regulations, Arabic, Arts. 1–19 including the deletion note for Art. 7; SCCA Rules, English, Arts. 1–46 and Appendices I–IV, read for the articles cited) |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: Law page read; status "ساري"; issue 1433/05/24H; publication 1433/07/18H; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 3 -->` (Arts. 10, 24, 50); the amendment blocks name Royal Decree M/8 of 18/1/1443H and Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H; the in-force wording of each amended article was read and is what the rows cite. Regulations page read; status "ساري"; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 1 -->` (Art. 7, deleted by Council of Ministers Decision 249 of 7/4/1444H). SCCA Rules: the 2023 PDF (printed 27 February 2024) was read; whether the SCCA has since amended its Rules or fee schedule was not checked. A draft new Arbitration Law was reported to have been published for public consultation in September 2025; it was not in the source set and nothing from it is used: `[model knowledge — verify]`. |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law applies to every arbitration conducted in the Kingdom, whatever the nature of the legal relationship, and to international commercial arbitration conducted abroad where the parties agree to subject it to the Law, without prejudice to Islamic Sharia and the international conventions the Kingdom is party to (Art. 2). It does not apply to personal-status disputes or to matters not open to compromise. It covers the arbitration agreement (form, capacity, effect on court proceedings, separability), the tribunal (composition, qualifications, appointment, challenge, removal), interim measures by the court and the tribunal, the procedure, the applicable law, the award (time limit, form, deposit, interpretation and correction), the single remedy of annulment, and the enforcement order. The Regulations add the identity of the competent court, notification mechanics, appointment and challenge details, and the Supreme Court's role in appeals. The SCCA Rules are the institutional rules most often chosen for Saudi-seated arbitration; they bind only by agreement and yield to any mandatory provision of the applicable arbitration law (Rules Art. 2(4)). Enforcement of awards after the enforcement order, and enforcement of foreign awards, is governed by the Enforcement Law: see `enforcement-law.md` (Arts. 7(1)(b), 7(1)(g), 9(2)), cross-referenced here but not restated.
23: 
24: ## Rules
25: 
26: Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.
27: 
28:  |  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Practical effect  |  Tag  | 
29:  | --- | --- | --- | --- | 
30:  |  Law Art. 2  |  Without prejudice to Islamic Sharia and international conventions to which the Kingdom is party, the Law applies to every arbitration, whatever the legal relationship, conducted in the Kingdom, or to international commercial arbitration conducted abroad whose parties agree to subject it to the Law. It does not apply to personal-status disputes or to matters in which compromise (صلح) is not permitted.  |  Arbitrability is defined negatively: commercial and civil claims are arbitrable; family matters and non-compromisable matters (criminal liability, public-order matters, and in practice certain mandatory-law disputes such as employment and commercial-agency registration matters) are not. Which commercial disputes are treated as non-compromisable is a court-practice question. `[model knowledge — verify]` A foreign-seated arbitration is subject to the Law only by express agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
31:  |  Law Art. 3  |  Arbitration is international if the dispute concerns international trade and: the parties' principal places of business were in different states when the agreement was made (a party with several places: the one most connected; none: habitual residence); or both were in the same state but the seat, the place of performance of a substantial part of the obligations, or the place most connected with the dispute is outside it; or the parties agreed to a permanent arbitral organisation or centre seated outside the Kingdom; or the subject-matter is connected with more than one state.  |  Choosing ICC, LCIA or DIAC rules makes a Saudi-seated arbitration "international" for the Law, which fixes the competent court as the Riyadh Court of Appeal (Art. 8(2)) unless the parties choose another Saudi appeal court.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
32:  |  Law Arts. 4–5  |  Where the Law lets the parties choose a procedure, they may delegate the choice to a third party, including any individual, body, organisation or arbitration centre inside or outside the Kingdom. Where the parties subject their relationship to a document (model contract, international convention or other), its arbitration provisions apply insofar as not contrary to Sharia.  |  Institutional rules and FIDIC-type arbitration provisions are given effect, subject always to Sharia.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
33:  |  Law Art. 7  |  A party that continues with the arbitration knowing of a breach of a derogable provision of the Law or of the arbitration agreement, without objecting within the agreed period or, absent agreement, within 30 days of learning of it, is deemed to have waived its right to object.  |  Procedural objections must be raised within 30 days or are lost for annulment purposes; SCCA Rules Art. 34 contains a parallel waiver.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
34:  |  Law Art. 8; Regs Arts. 2, 17  |  The competent court for annulment actions and the matters the Law refers to it is the Court of Appeal originally competent to hear the dispute; for international commercial arbitration, in or outside the Kingdom, the Court of Appeal originally competent in Riyadh unless the parties agree another Court of Appeal in the Kingdom. The Regulations confirm "competent court" means that Court of Appeal except in Art. 9(1), Art. 12 and the end of Art. 40(3) of the Law (court proceedings on the merits), and designate the Supreme Court as the appeal body against an annulment judgment (Art. 51(2)) and as the body hearing the grievance against refusal of enforcement (Art. 55(3)).  |  For a commercial dispute the supervisory court is the appeal circuit of the commercial court (Riyadh for international cases); the Commercial Courts Law's draft Regulations foresee specialised circuits for Arbitration Law applications (`commercial-courts-law.md`, Regs Art. 23(b)). Supporting applications (appointment, challenge, interim measures, extension of time, enforcement order) all go there.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
35:  |  Law Art. 9  |  An arbitration agreement may precede the dispute, as a clause or a separate agreement, or follow it even after a court case is filed, in which case it must define the matters submitted or is void. It must be in writing or is void. It is written if contained in a document issued by the parties, or in exchanged authenticated correspondence, telegrams or other electronic or written means of communication; a reference in a contract to a document containing an arbitration clause is an arbitration agreement, and a reference to a model contract, international convention or other document containing an arbitration clause counts as a written agreement if the reference clearly makes the clause part of the contract.  |  E-mail and e-signed agreements satisfy the writing requirement; incorporation of an arbitration clause from general terms or a master agreement works only if the incorporation language clearly makes the clause part of the contract. Draft order forms and SOWs with an express "the dispute-resolution clause of the MSA applies" sentence.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
36:  |  Law Art. 10 (para. 1 as amended by Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H)  |  An arbitration agreement is valid only if made by a person with capacity to dispose (أهلاً للتصرف), whether a natural or a legal person (in-force text). Government bodies may not agree to arbitrate except with the approval of the Prime Minister, unless a specific statutory text permits it (para. 2, unamended).  |  Confirmed from the in-force text: an arbitration clause with a Saudi government body needs Prime Minister approval unless a specific law authorises it; the Government Tenders and Procurement Law and certain sector laws are the usual "specific texts" `[model knowledge — verify]`. For companies, check the signatory's authority (articles, board resolution, commercial-register powers) because a clause signed without authority is void for incapacity (Art. 50(1)(b)). The 1447H rewording of para. 1 changed the capacity formula, not the rule.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text)  | 
37:  |  Law Arts. 11–12  |  A court seised of a dispute covered by an arbitration agreement must rule the case inadmissible if the defendant so pleads before any request or defence; filing the court case does not prevent commencing or continuing the arbitration or issuing the award. If the parties agree to arbitrate while the case is pending, the court must refer the dispute to arbitration.  |  The arbitration plea is lost if any defence on the merits is filed first; the Commercial Courts Law allows the ruling on this plea to be appealed before the merits (`commercial-courts-law.md`, Art. 74(4)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
38:  |  Law Arts. 13–14  |  The tribunal consists of one or more arbitrators in an odd number, or the arbitration is void. An arbitrator must have full capacity, be of good conduct, and hold at least a university degree in Sharia or law (العلوم الشرعية أو النظامية); in a multi-member tribunal the degree requirement need be met only by its president.  |  Confirmed: a sole arbitrator or a presiding arbitrator must hold a Sharia or law degree; co-arbitrators (engineers, accountants) need not. Nationality and religion are not conditions in the text. A tribunal constituted in breach is an annulment ground (Art. 50(1)(e)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
39:  |  Law Art. 15; Regs Arts. 4, 10  |  Parties may agree how to choose arbitrators; failing agreement, the competent court appoints a sole arbitrator (Regs: within 15 days of the request); for three, each party appoints one within 15 days of the other's request and the two agree the third within 15 days of the last appointment, failing which the court appoints on application within 15 days, the third arbitrator presiding (same rule for larger tribunals); the court also steps in where the agreed procedure breaks down or a third party fails to act; it applies the parties' and the Law's conditions and decides within 30 days; its appointment decision is not separately appealable. The applicant attaches the request for arbitration and the arbitration agreement.  |  Ad hoc clauses should name an appointing authority (e.g. the SCCA) to avoid a court appointment cycle of 15–30 days per step.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
40:  |  Law Arts. 16–19; Regs Arts. 5–6  |  An arbitrator must have no interest in the dispute and must disclose in writing, on appointment and throughout, any circumstances raising justifiable doubts about impartiality or independence; is barred in the same cases as a judge; may be challenged only for serious doubts about impartiality/independence or lack of agreed qualifications (without prejudice to Art. 14); a party may not challenge its own appointee except for later-discovered reasons. Absent agreed procedure: written challenge to the tribunal within five days of learning of the constitution or the ground; if the arbitrator does not withdraw and the other party does not agree within five days, the tribunal decides within 15 days; the challenger may go to the competent court within 30 days, whose judgment is final; no repeat challenge on the same grounds; a challenge before the tribunal suspends the arbitration (a court application does not); a successful challenge voids the proceedings including any award. Regs: an arbitrator may withdraw without giving reasons; no challenge after closing of pleadings. Removal by the court for inability or unjustified delay on either party's request (final); otherwise only by joint agreement, with compensation for a removed arbitrator not at fault; a replacement is appointed by the original method and proceedings are suspended meanwhile (except after a successful challenge).  |  Very short challenge windows (5 days) and a drastic consequence (nullity of everything done) make early conflict checks essential; institutional rules (SCCA Art. 18: 14 days, SCCA Court decides) modify the mechanics but not the statutory grounds.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
41:  |  Law Arts. 20–21  |  The tribunal rules on pleas to its jurisdiction, including that no arbitration agreement exists, has lapsed, is void or does not cover the dispute; such pleas must be raised by the time of the statement of defence (Art. 30(2)) (appointing an arbitrator is no waiver), and pleas that a matter raised during the proceedings falls outside the agreement must be raised at once; late pleas may be admitted for acceptable reasons; the tribunal decides jurisdiction before or with the merits, and rejection of the plea is reviewable only through an annulment action against the final award. An arbitration clause is an agreement independent of the contract's other terms; nullity, rescission or termination of the contract does not affect a clause valid in itself.  |  Competence-competence and separability as in the UNCITRAL Model Law; a termination-for-breach dispute still goes to arbitration.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
42:  |  Law Arts. 22–23  |  The competent court may order interim or precautionary measures on a party's request before the arbitration begins, or on the tribunal's request during it, and may reverse them likewise, unless otherwise agreed; the court may order letters rogatory on the tribunal's request; the tribunal may ask any body for assistance (summoning witnesses or experts, producing documents) without prejudice to its own powers. If the parties so agree, the tribunal may on a party's request order either party to take interim or precautionary measures required by the nature of the dispute, may require security from the applicant, and, on non-compliance, may authorise the other party to take the steps needed to enforce it, without prejudice to asking the competent authority to compel compliance.  |  The tribunal's power to grant interim relief exists only by agreement (institutional rules such as SCCA Art. 28 supply it); court relief is available pre-arbitration on a party's request but during the arbitration only via the tribunal. Draft the clause to confer interim powers expressly and to preserve the right to seek court measures.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
43:  |  Law Art. 24 (para. 1 deleted by Royal Decree M/8 of 18/1/1443H); Regs Art. 7 (deleted by Council of Ministers Decision 249 of 7/4/1444H)  |  The former obligation to sign a separate fee contract with each arbitrator and deposit a copy (Law Art. 24(1); Regs Art. 7 naming the SCCA or the institution as depository) no longer applies. In force: if the parties and the arbitrators do not agree the arbitrators' fees, the competent court fixes them by a decision not subject to any appeal, and must fix them whenever it appointed the arbitrators.  |  Institutional fee schedules (SCCA Appendix I) govern fees in administered cases; in ad hoc cases without agreement the court sets them. The award must still state fees and costs and their allocation (Art. 42(2)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text)  | 
44:  |  Law Arts. 25–27; Regs Arts. 8–9, 11  |  The parties may agree the procedure, including adopting the rules of any arbitration organisation or centre in or outside the Kingdom, provided they do not conflict with Sharia; absent agreement the tribunal chooses, observing Sharia and the Law (Regs: notifying the parties at least ten days before applying its chosen procedure). Proceedings begin on the day a party receives the request for arbitration from the other (with multiple parties, the last to receive it), unless agreed otherwise; the Regulations list the request's contents (parties, representatives, contract summary, arbitration agreement, subject, facts, claims, proposal or notice of arbitrator appointment) and provide that disputes over its adequacy do not stop constitution. Parties are treated equally with a full and equal opportunity to present their case.  |  The 12-month award clock (Art. 40) runs from receipt of the request for arbitration, not from constitution of the tribunal; under SCCA Rules the arbitration commences on the Administrator's determination that filing requirements and the registration fee are met (SCCA Art. 5(4)), which parties should treat as the later of the two dates for safety. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
45:  |  Law Art. 28  |  The parties may agree the place of arbitration in or outside the Kingdom; absent agreement the tribunal fixes it considering the circumstances and convenience; the tribunal may meet anywhere to deliberate, hear witnesses, experts or parties, inspect or examine documents.  |  The Law has no default seat; SCCA Rules likewise leave the seat to the SCCA Court/tribunal (see SCCA row), with Riyadh as the default only in the ODR track. Name the seat in the clause.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
46:  |  Law Art. 29  |  Arbitration is conducted in Arabic unless the tribunal decides or the parties agree on another language or languages; the choice governs written statements, oral pleadings, and every decision, communication and award unless otherwise provided; the tribunal may require translation of some or all documents.  |  Arabic is the statutory default; an English-language arbitration requires a clause or tribunal decision, and the award must still be filed with a certified Arabic translation (Art. 44).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
47:  |  Law Arts. 30–37; Regs Arts. 12–14  |  Written statement of claim and defence within the agreed or tribunal-set periods (counterclaims and set-off allowed, even later if justified), with copies of every submission and expert report to the other party; amendments allowed unless refused to avoid delay; hearings are held unless the tribunal decides documents-only (subject to contrary agreement), with sufficient notice and signed minutes; a claimant's failure to file its statement ends the proceedings, a respondent's failure does not; absence from a hearing or failure to produce documents lets the tribunal proceed on the evidence before it; the tribunal may appoint experts (Regs: the appointment decision fixes mission, deadline, fees and advance, and non-payment of the advance may forfeit the party's reliance on the expert), parties must cooperate, the report is circulated for comment and the expert may be examined at a hearing; joinder or intervention of a non-party requires the consent of all parties and the joined party (Regs Art. 13); matters outside the tribunal's authority, forgery pleas or criminal proceedings suspend the arbitration and the award deadline only if their determination is necessary; pleadings are closed and may be reopened before the award (Regs Art. 14).  |  No unilateral joinder of group companies or guarantors without their consent under the Law (SCCA Art. 12 adds a rules-based joinder mechanism, which still needs a binding arbitration agreement, SCCA Art. 24(3)). Default awards are available against a non-participating respondent.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
48:  |  Law Art. 38  |  Subject to Sharia and public order in the Kingdom, the tribunal applies the rules the parties chose to the merits (a chosen national law means its substantive rules, not its conflict rules, unless agreed otherwise); absent choice, the substantive rules of the law it considers most connected; it must observe the contract terms and take account of trade usages, customs and the parties' course of dealing; it may decide ex aequo et bono only if the parties expressly authorised amiable composition (تفويض بالصلح).  |  A foreign governing law is honoured in a Saudi-seated arbitration, but Sharia and public order sit above it: awards of conventional interest, or of damages the court views as speculative, are at annulment/enforcement risk (Arts. 50(2), 55(2)(b)). `[model knowledge — verify]` Draft interest and liquidated-damages clauses with this in mind.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
49:  |  Law Arts. 39–40  |  Awards of a multi-member tribunal are by majority after secret deliberation; if no majority can form, the tribunal chooses an umpire (محكم مرجح) within 15 days or the court appoints one; procedural decisions may be taken by the president if the parties so agree in writing or all members authorise it; an amiable-composition award must be unanimous; interim and partial awards are permitted unless agreed otherwise. The final award must be issued within the agreed period or, absent agreement, within 12 months from commencement; the tribunal may extend by up to six months unless the parties agree a longer extension; if the award is not issued in time either party may ask the competent court to fix a further period or to terminate the arbitration, after which either party may sue in court; replacement of an arbitrator extends the period by 30 days.  |  Confirmed: 12 months + 6 months by the tribunal, then only by the court or party agreement; an award issued out of time is exposed to annulment for lapse of the agreement (Art. 50(1)(a)). Institutional rules (SCCA Art. 33: 75 days from closing, extendable by the Administrator) operate inside this statutory ceiling unless the parties' adoption of the rules is treated as agreement to a longer period; take an express extension. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
50:  |  Law Art. 41; Regs Art. 15  |  Proceedings end with the final award or with a tribunal decision terminating them: on the parties' agreement; on the claimant's withdrawal unless the respondent shows a serious interest in continuing; where the tribunal finds continuation pointless or impossible; or under Art. 34(1). Death or incapacity of a party does not end the proceedings but extends the period by 30 days. The arbitration agreement survives a termination decision unless the parties agree otherwise.  |  Withdrawal of a claim does not free the parties from the clause; the dispute must be re-arbitrated, not litigated.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
51:  |  Law Arts. 42–44  |  The award is in writing and reasoned, signed by the arbitrators (a majority suffices if the minority's reasons for not signing are recorded); it must state the date and place of issue, the parties' and arbitrators' names, addresses, nationalities and capacities, a summary of the arbitration agreement, of the parties' statements, claims, pleadings and documents, and of any expert report, the operative part, and the arbitrators' fees and costs and their allocation. A true copy is delivered to each party within 15 days of issue; no publication without both parties' written consent. The tribunal deposits the original or a signed copy, in its language, with the competent court within the same 15 days, with a certified Arabic translation if in a foreign language.  |  Proof of deposit with the competent court is a prerequisite to the enforcement order (Art. 53(4)); an English award needs a certified Arabic translation for both deposit and enforcement. Missing formal contents are an annulment ground where they affected the award (Art. 50(1)(g)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
52:  |  Law Arts. 45–48; Regs Art. 16  |  A settlement reached during the arbitration may be recorded as a consent award with the force of an award for enforcement. Within 30 days of receipt a party may request interpretation of ambiguity in the operative part (issued in writing within 30 days, forming part of the award); the tribunal corrects clerical or arithmetical errors within 15 days of the award or the request (a correction exceeding its power is challengeable by annulment); within 30 days of receipt a party may request an additional award on claims omitted, issued within 60 days extendable by 30, the award-period rule not applying to it.  |  A consent award is the cleanest enforceable form for a settlement of an arbitrated dispute (Enforcement Law Art. 7(1)(b)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
53:  |  Law Arts. 49–50 (para. 1(b) as amended by Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H)  |  Awards under the Law are not subject to any appeal other than an annulment action. Annulment lies only where: (a) there is no arbitration agreement, or it is void, voidable or lapsed by expiry of its period; (b) a party to the agreement lacked capacity or had limited capacity when it was made, under the law governing its capacity (in-force wording: عديم الأهلية); (c) a party could not present its defence for want of proper notice of an arbitrator's appointment or of the proceedings, or for another reason beyond its control; (d) the award set aside the legal rules the parties agreed to apply to the merits; (e) the tribunal was constituted or the arbitrators appointed contrary to the Law or the parties' agreement; (f) the award decided matters outside the agreement (severable parts survive); (g) the award failed to meet the required conditions in a way affecting its content, or rests on void procedures that affected it. The court annuls of its own motion an award contrary to Sharia or public order in the Kingdom, or to the parties' agreement, or on a non-arbitrable subject. The arbitration agreement survives annulment unless agreed otherwise or the agreement itself is annulled; the court may not review the facts or merits.  |  Closed list of grounds plus ex officio Sharia/public-order review; ground (d) makes a tribunal's failure to apply the chosen law an annulment ground, which is broader than the Model Law. The 1447H amendment to (b) is a wording change only.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text)  | 
54:  |  Law Art. 51; Regs Arts. 17(1), 18  |  The annulment action is brought by either party within 60 days following notification of the award; a waiver of the right to bring it made before the award does not bar the action (Regs: a waiver after the award does). If the court upholds the award it must order its enforcement, by a judgment not subject to any appeal; if it annuls the award, its judgment may be appealed within 30 days from the day after notification (Regs: to the Supreme Court). The applicant files the original or certified copy of the award, a true copy of the arbitration agreement and a certified Arabic translation if needed.  |  Confirmed 60-day window from notification; "exclusion of recourse" clauses in the contract are ineffective. Asymmetric finality: an upheld award is final at once, an annulment can go to the Supreme Court.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
55:  |  Law Arts. 52–55; Regs Art. 17(2)  |  An award under the Law has res judicata effect and is enforceable. The competent court (or its delegate) issues the enforcement order on an application accompanied by the original or certified copy of the award, a true copy of the arbitration agreement, a certified Arabic translation if in another language, and proof of deposit under Art. 44. The application is not admissible until the annulment period has expired; the order is issued only after verifying that the award does not conflict with a judgment or decision of a Saudi court, committee or body with jurisdiction over the subject, contains nothing contrary to Sharia or public order (severable parts may be enforced), and was properly notified to the losing party. An annulment action does not stay enforcement, but the court may order a stay if requested in the annulment petition on serious grounds, deciding the request within 15 days, possibly against security, and must then decide the annulment within 180 days of the stay. The enforcement order is not subject to grievance; refusal may be challenged within 30 days before the Supreme Court.  |  Two-step enforcement for a Saudi-seated award: (1) enforcement order from the competent Court of Appeal after the 60-day annulment window; (2) execution by the enforcement court, the award being an enforcement instrument under `enforcement-law.md` Art. 7(1)(b) (and, for foreign awards, Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9(2) with the treaty route). Earliest realistic enforcement start is therefore about three months after notification of the award. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
56:  |  Law Arts. 56–58; Regs Arts. 3, 19  |  The Council of Ministers issues the Regulations; the Law replaces the Arbitration Law of 1403H (Royal Decree M/46 of 12/7/1403H) and takes effect 30 days after publication. Regulations: notifications may be made electronically; periods run from the day after notification unless agreed otherwise, with an official-holiday last day extended to the next working day; the Regulations took effect on publication.  |  Day-counting for the 60-day annulment period and the 30-day appeal starts the day after notification; a last day on Friday/Saturday or a public holiday rolls forward.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
57:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 2–3  |  The Rules apply where the parties agreed to SCCA arbitration or to arbitration "by the SCCA" without naming rules, and thereby authorise the SCCA to administer; they apply to arbitrations commenced on or after 1 May 2023 with the appendices as integral parts; they yield to non-derogable provisions of the applicable arbitration law; unless agreed otherwise, the Expedited Procedure Rules (Appendix II) apply automatically within their scope and, alternatively, the ODR Rules (Appendix IV) within theirs, prevailing over the main Rules in case of conflict. The SCCA Court is an independent body whose decisions under the Rules (appointment and removal of arbitrators, challenges, consolidation, costs, award scrutiny) are final and binding; the parties waive any appeal or review of them.  |  A bare "SCCA arbitration" clause imports the 2023 Rules and, for claims up to SAR 4m, the expedited track with a sole arbitrator; opt out expressly if a three-member tribunal is wanted for mid-value disputes.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
58:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 5–6, 15–16  |  The arbitration commences when the Administrator determines that the Request for Arbitration (statement referring to SCCA arbitration, party details, contract and arbitration agreement, claim description, relief and amount, proposals on number of arbitrators, language and seat) is complete and the registration fee paid; the Answer is due within 30 days of commencement, with any jurisdictional plea and counterclaims, and its absence does not stop the arbitration. One arbitrator is appointed unless the parties agreed otherwise or the SCCA Court, after consulting them, decides three are appropriate for size or complexity; the parties may agree any nomination procedure, failing which the SCCA Court appoints on request after 30 days; joint nomination of a sole arbitrator within 30 days of commencement, otherwise SCCA Court appointment; for three-member tribunals each side nominates within 30 days (multiple claimants or respondents jointly; a joined party jointly within 30 days of joinder).  |  Sole arbitrator is the institutional default; the Law's Art. 14 degree requirement then bites on that single appointee.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
59:  |  SCCA Rules Art. 7; Appendix III  |  Before the tribunal is constituted a party may apply for emergency measures by e-mail to the Administrator and the other parties, with the SCCA administrative fee and emergency-arbitrator fee (Appendix III Art. 9: advance deposit SAR 75,000, being SAR 15,000 administrative fee and SAR 60,000 emergency-arbitrator fees and expenses, increasable by the SCCA Court); the SCCA Court appoints a single emergency arbitrator within one business day (challenge within one business day of notification); a schedule is set within two business days; the emergency arbitrator may order or award any interim, provisional or precautionary measure, with reasons (summary form allowed), possibly against security, and must issue the interim award or order within 14 days of transmission of the file (extendable by the Administrator); the emergency arbitration is terminated if no Request for Arbitration is filed within 10 days of the application; the measure binds once issued, ceases to bind if claims are withdrawn or the arbitration is not registered, and the constituted tribunal may reconsider, modify or vacate it; the emergency arbitrator cannot sit on the tribunal unless the parties agree; court applications for interim measures remain compatible with the agreement; the procedure does not apply after constitution, where the parties opted out, or where they agreed another pre-arbitral emergency mechanism.  |  Fast pre-tribunal relief exists under the Rules, but the Law's Art. 23 makes tribunal-ordered measures depend on party agreement and offers no direct court enforcement mechanism for an emergency order; the practical enforcement route for a non-complying party remains an application to the competent court under Art. 22 or Art. 23(2). `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
60:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 17–18  |  Arbitrators must be impartial and independent, sign a statement of acceptance, availability, independence and impartiality, disclose circumstances giving rise to justifiable doubts at acceptance and throughout, and follow the SCCA Code of Ethics; a party's failure to disclose known circumstances within a reasonable period waives the challenge; each party must promptly disclose any non-party with an economic interest in the outcome, including third-party funders; unilateral communications with arbitrators are prohibited save as the Code allows. Challenge grounds: justifiable doubts as to impartiality or independence, failure to perform duties, or manifest lack of the agreed qualifications; notice of challenge within 14 days of notification of the appointment or of learning the circumstances; the challenge does not suspend the arbitration unless the tribunal decides; the SCCA Court decides admissibility and merits if the parties do not agree and the arbitrator does not withdraw (reasons on request, possibly for a fee); the Administrator may itself refer a non-performing arbitrator for removal.  |  Litigation-funding arrangements must be disclosed in SCCA cases. The 14-day institutional window sits alongside the Law's 5-day / 15-day / 30-day statutory sequence; a party relying on the Rules' procedure should still watch the Art. 17 statutory route to the court. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
61:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 22–23; Appendix III Art. 5; Appendix IV Arts. 7–8  |  Place of arbitration: if the parties do not agree by the date the Administrator sets, the SCCA Court initially determines the seat, subject to the tribunal's final determination within 30 days of its constitution, having regard to the circumstances and convenience; hearings, meetings and deliberations may be held anywhere (and remotely); the award is deemed issued at the seat wherever signed, including electronically. Language: if not agreed, the Administrator initially determines it, the tribunal finally within 30 days, guided by the language of the arbitration agreement and contract, the circumstances and convenience; translations may be ordered. The emergency arbitrator's seat follows Art. 22. **Only in the ODR track** (Appendix IV) are Riyadh and Arabic the defaults absent agreement.  |  Contrary to a common assumption, the 2023 main Rules contain no Riyadh seat default and no English/Arabic language default; the seat and language are institutional decisions unless the clause fixes them. Fix both in the clause; the Law's own default language is Arabic (Art. 29).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
62:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 24, 28  |  The tribunal rules on its own jurisdiction (existence, scope, validity of the arbitration agreement) and on admissibility and arbitrability; invalidity of the contract does not by itself invalidate the clause; with multiple parties the arbitration proceeds among those bound; objections must be raised no later than the Answer (extendable); they may be decided as a preliminary matter or in the award; objections before constitution do not stop the Administrator from constituting the tribunal. Interim measures: on application the tribunal may order measures to maintain or restore the status quo, prevent imminent harm or prejudice to the process, preserve assets for satisfaction of an award, or preserve evidence; the applicant must show likely harm not adequately reparable by damages that substantially outweighs the harm to the respondent, and a reasonable possibility of success (relaxed for evidence preservation); the tribunal may modify, suspend or terminate measures, require security, require disclosure of changed circumstances, and award costs and damages for measures later found unjustified; court applications are compatible with the agreement.  |  Adoption of the SCCA Rules supplies the party agreement that Law Art. 23(1) requires for tribunal-ordered interim measures.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
63:  |  SCCA Rules Arts. 33, 36–37, 44  |  The tribunal declares the proceedings closed after the last hearing or authorised submission (reopening in exceptional circumstances); unless agreed otherwise it issues the final award within 75 days of closing, extendable by the Administrator. Awards are reasoned, in writing, signed (electronic signature permitted unless the parties or applicable law require otherwise), dated, and state the seat; they are final and binding; the SCCA may publish awards, orders and decisions unless a party objects before the arbitration concludes (anonymised as needed), and the parties must otherwise keep them confidential; before signing, the draft award goes to the Administrator for SCCA Court review as to form and no award issues until approved; filing or registration required by applicable law is the tribunal's responsibility on the parties' notice. Applicable law: the rules of law designated by the parties, else the law the tribunal finds appropriate; equity only on express authorisation; contract terms and trade usages always; **the law of the arbitration agreement is the law of the seat unless the parties agreed otherwise in writing.** Confidentiality binds arbitrators, the SCCA, its Court, Board, committees and staff, and the tribunal may make confidentiality orders and protect trade secrets.  |  The SCCA Court's form scrutiny helps satisfy Law Art. 42's content requirements; parties should still ensure the deposit under Art. 44 (the Rules leave statutory filing to the tribunal on the parties' prompting). Specify the law of the arbitration agreement where the seat and governing law differ.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
64:  |  SCCA Appendix II (Expedited Procedure Rules)  |  Apply automatically, as part of agreeing to the Rules, where the aggregate amount in dispute does not exceed SAR 4,000,000 (or equivalent) exclusive of costs, or where the parties opt in; the Administrator may decide they no longer apply (the tribunal then stays unless the SCCA Court reconstitutes it); full evidence goes in with the Request and Answer, the Answer period may be shortened, objections to applicability are decided finally by the Administrator, and a later increase above SAR 4m does not by itself remove the case from the track; a sole arbitrator is nominated within the Administrator's time limit or appointed; the award is made within 30 days of closing and in any event within 180 days of constitution unless the Administrator extends in exceptional circumstances; fees follow the expedited fee schedule.  |  Vendor and SaaS disputes below SAR 4m default to a sole arbitrator and a six-month award timetable; clauses that insist on three arbitrators must opt out of Appendix II expressly.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
65:  |  SCCA Appendix IV (Online Dispute Resolution Procedure Rules)  |  Apply where the parties agree in writing and the aggregate sum in dispute does not exceed SAR 200,000 exclusive of costs (later increases do not remove the case unless decided otherwise); prevail over the Rules in conflict; the Administrator may move the case to the ordinary Rules; the seat is Riyadh and the language Arabic unless agreed otherwise; the final award is issued within 30 days of the tribunal's constitution (or re-affirmation after a challenge), reasoned and allocating costs, after SCCA Court form review, and served through the ODR platform.  |  Low-value recurring-revenue disputes (subscription fees, small service invoices) can be routed to a 30-day online award if the clause opts in.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]`  | 
66:  |  Cross-reference (Enforcement Law)  |  Enforcement of a domestic award after the Art. 53 enforcement order, and of a foreign award, is governed by the Enforcement Law (in force 2026-10-28; the 1433H law until then): domestic awards are enforcement instruments under Art. 7(1)(b) "in accordance with the relevant statutory texts" (i.e. after the Arbitration Law's order); foreign awards under Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9(2) are enforced per the Kingdom's treaty obligations (New York Convention `[model knowledge — verify]`) after verifying the Art. 9(1) conditions, including public order and the absence of a prior Saudi case; enforcement disputes and stays follow Enforcement Law Arts. 45–47.  |  Route enforcement-stage questions to `enforcement-law.md`; this file stops at the enforcement order.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the cross-referenced text in `enforcement-law.md`; routing statement `[model knowledge — verify]` as to practice  | 
67: 
68: ## Open questions for a local practitioner
69: 
70: - **Draft new Arbitration Law.** A draft replacement Arbitration Law was reported to have been released for public consultation in September 2025 (reported themes: relaxing arbitrator qualifications, institutional emergency measures, clearer treatment of government-entity arbitration). It was not in the source set; nothing above relies on it. Confirm its status and whether any provision has been enacted since the M/21 of 1447H amendment. `[model knowledge — verify]`
71: - **Government-entity arbitration (Art. 10(2)).** The in-force text still requires Prime Minister approval absent a specific statutory text. Which laws count as the "specific text" (e.g. Government Tenders and Procurement Law provisions, sector laws for MISA-licensed projects) and the current approval practice must be confirmed. `[model knowledge — verify]`
72: - **Arbitrator qualifications (Art. 14).** Whether a foreign law degree satisfies "شهادة جامعية في العلوم الشرعية أو النظامية", and how the courts treat awards by sole arbitrators without such a degree in international cases, is practice-dependent. `[model knowledge — verify]`
73: - **Interaction of institutional time limits with Art. 40.** Whether adopting institutional rules that permit extensions by the institution counts as the parties "agreeing a longer period" under Art. 40(2), or whether a court extension is still needed after 18 months, is not settled in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`
74: - **Non-arbitrable commercial matters.** The Law excludes "matters not open to compromise"; which commercial or regulatory disputes (e.g. commercial-agency registration, employment, insolvency-related claims, competition penalties) the courts treat as non-arbitrable is not in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`
75: - **Interest and public order.** The treatment of interest components in awards (severance under Art. 55(2)(b) versus full annulment under Art. 50(2)) is practice-dependent. `[model knowledge — verify]`
76: - **Regs Art. 7 deletion.** With the deposit requirement gone, whether any registration of arbitrator engagements or awards with the SCCA is required for ad hoc arbitrations should be confirmed; only the Art. 44 court deposit remains in the text.
77: - **Emergency-arbitrator orders.** No provision of the Law addresses court enforcement of an emergency arbitrator's order; whether the competent court will support it under Art. 22–23 or treat it as an interim award for Art. 39(5) purposes is untested in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`
78: - **SCCA Rules currency.** The 2023 edition and fee schedule were read; confirm no later amendment (the SCCA reserves the right to revise fees).
79: 
80: ## How skills should use this file
81: 
82: - **Skills that load this file.** `commercial-legal:vendor-agreement-review`, `commercial-legal:saas-msa-review` and `commercial-legal:nda-review` load it at the dispute-resolution clause step and must check, in order: form of agreement (Law Art. 9), capacity and government-entity approval (Art. 10), arbitrability (Art. 2), seat and language (Arts. 28–29; SCCA Arts. 22–23), tribunal qualifications (Art. 14), interim-measure powers (Arts. 22–23; SCCA Arts. 7, 28), award time limit (Art. 40; SCCA Art. 33), annulment window (Art. 51), and the expedited-track threshold (SCCA Appendix II) when the clause names the SCCA. `commercial-legal:escalation-flagger` loads Arts. 10(2), 14, 40 and 51 when a live arbitration or a government counterparty is involved. `commercial-legal:stakeholder-summary` cites only the Practical-effect column and carries the row tag. `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads Arts. 9–10, 49–51 and 55 when reviewing pending arbitrations and awards on a target. `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` loads Arts. 51–55 when a closing condition depends on an award becoming enforceable (annulment period expiry + enforcement order).
83: - **Cited rows.** Quote the Article cell and reproduce the Tag cell verbatim; SCCA rows carry `[authority — SCCA]` and must be described as institutional rules that apply only by agreement, never as statute.
84: - **Computed dates.** Award due = commencement (receipt of the request for arbitration, Art. 26; or the later SCCA commencement date) + 12 months, + up to 6 months by tribunal extension (Art. 40). Annulment deadline = notification of the award + 60 days, counted from the day after notification with holiday roll-forward (Art. 51(1); Regs Art. 3(2)). Earliest enforcement-order application = day after the annulment period expires (Art. 55(1)). Appeal against an annulment judgment = day after notification + 30 days (Art. 51(2)). SCCA: Answer = commencement + 30 days (Art. 6); final award = closing + 75 days (Art. 33); expedited award = constitution + 180 days (Appendix II Art. 10); emergency order = file transmission + 14 days (Art. 7(8)). Each computed date carries `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the formula; any statement about how long enforcement then takes is `[model knowledge — verify]`.
85: - **Enforcement routing.** Questions about executing an award, or enforcing a foreign award, go to `enforcement-law.md` (Arts. 7, 9, 45–47) with the effective-date gate stated there; this file answers only up to the Art. 53 enforcement order.
86: - **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule not in this table, or the row it needs is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, or the answer turns on the reported draft Arbitration Law, on court practice regarding Sharia/public-order review, or on an SCCA fee figure, it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory or upgrade a tag.

