1: # Law of Commercial Courts — نظام المحاكم التجارية
2: 
3: <!-- Copied from references/jurisdictions/_TEMPLATE-instrument.md. Covers the Law and, as a secondary source, the Ministry of Justice implementing-regulations text. -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Law of Commercial Courts / نظام المحاكم التجارية, with its Implementing Regulations / اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام المحاكم التجارية |
11: | Issuing instrument | Law: Royal Decree No. M/93 of 15/8/1441H (2020-04-08), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 511 of 14/8/1441H. Regulations: issued by the Minister of Justice in coordination with the Supreme Judicial Council under Art. 95 of the Law; the file read today is the Ministry of Justice PDF labelled **مشروع اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام المحاكم التجارية** (draft, dated 1441/9H – 2020/5) and is treated as the published project text. The approved regulation (reported as Minister of Justice Decision No. 8344 of 21/10/1441H `[model knowledge — verify]`) must be confirmed article by article before any regulation row below is relied on. |
12: | Issuing authority | Ministry of Justice (وزارة العدل); court organisation by the Supreme Judicial Council (المجلس الأعلى للقضاء) |
13: | Effective date | 2020-06-16 (Art. 96: 60 days after publication in the Official Gazette; portal shows publication 24/8/1441H = 2020-04-17). Regulations: "published in the Official Gazette and effective after publication" (Art. 95); date of the approved regulation not confirmed from the file. |
14: | Amendment history | Royal Decree No. M/191 of 29/11/1444H amended Art. 16(3): "disputes between partners in a mudaraba" (منازعات الشركاء في شركة المضاربة) became "disputes arising from the partnership contracts provided for in the Civil Transactions Law" (المنازعات التي تنشأ عن عقود المشاركة المنصوص عليها في نظام المعاملات المدنية). The portal marks only Art. 16 as amended; the in-force wording (portal amendment block) was read today and the Art. 16 row reflects it. Art. 16(2) and its SAR 100,000 threshold were not amended. Transitional rule (Decree ¶4): the Law applies to commercial cases not yet decided and procedures not completed at entry into force, except jurisdiction changes for cases already filed, time limits already running, and objection routes for final judgments already issued. |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/38334008-3b70-4c6c-b3af-aba3016a8061/1 ; Regulations (MoJ, file labelled draft): https://www.moj.gov.sa/Documents/ProjectExecution.pdf |
16: | Official translation | The portal offers a "Translated document" attachment; it was not read today (no `boe-en` file for this instrument). Regulations: none read. |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Law, Arabic, 96 articles plus Decree and Decision text; Regulations draft, Arabic, Arts. 1–351 read for Arts. 1–340) |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page read; status line "ساري" (in force); issue date 1441/08/15H (2020-04-08); publication date 1441/08/24H (2020-04-17); issuing instruments listed: Royal Decree M/93 of 15/8/1441H and Council of Ministers Decision 511 of 14/8/1441H; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 1 -->` (Art. 16; the portal's amendment block names Royal Decree M/191 of 29/11/1444H as amending paragraph (3) only). No other amending instrument appears in the text. The regulations file carries the heading "مشروع" and the date 1441/9H; whether the approved regulation differs from this text was not checked and cannot be confirmed from the files given. |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law governs the commercial courts (المحاكم التجارية) and the commercial circuits established in general courts where no commercial court exists (Decree ¶2), and applies, together with its Regulations, to the court and the cases within its jurisdiction, without prejudice to the commercial laws and the international conventions the Kingdom is party to (Art. 2). It covers subject-matter and territorial jurisdiction, pre-filing notice and conciliation, electronic filing and service, written pleadings and hearing management, urgent (interim) requests, evidence in commercial cases, judgments and immediate enforcement, payment orders, appeal, reconsideration and cassation, and (in the Regulations) small claims and class actions. It does not itself govern the compulsory enforcement of judgments or the recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments and arbitral awards: those are matters for the Enforcement Law (`enforcement-law.md`) and the Arbitration Law (`arbitration-law.md`); this file only cross-references them. Where the Law is silent, the Law of Civil Procedure (نظام المرافعات الشرعية) applies insofar as compatible with the commercial nature of the case (Art. 93). Labor, administrative and criminal matters are outside the commercial courts.
23: 
24: ## Rules
25: 
26: Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.
27: 
28:  |  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Practical effect  |  Tag  | 
29:  | --- | --- | --- | --- | 
30:  |  Art. 2; Regs Art. 2  |  The Law and Regulations apply to the commercial court and the cases within its jurisdiction, without prejudice to the commercial laws and international conventions to which the Kingdom is party. The Regulations set the order of application: (a) the international convention, (b) the special procedural text in a commercial law, (c) the Law and Regulations.  |  A special procedure in the Companies Law, Bankruptcy Law or an IP law displaces the general commercial-court procedure; a treaty (e.g. a bilateral judicial cooperation agreement) ranks above both. Skills should check the sector law before assuming these timelines.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
31:  |  Art. 6; Regs Arts. 10–13  |  By exception to the Law and the Law of Civil Procedure, and without prejudice to public order and the rules of justice, parties to a commercial dealing who are all merchants may agree on specific pleading procedures. The Regulations require any agreement the Law or Regulations refer to be in writing; the agreement may cover the number of memoranda, notice procedures, document deadlines, a named expert or expert procedure (including agreeing to accept an expert's opinion), and shortening (never lengthening) procedural periods other than the court's own; it must be invoked at the first hearing or the right lapses; parties may also agree a case plan for the court's approval.  |  A B2B contract can validly pre-agree litigation mechanics (e.g. a single joint expert, capped memoranda) for a Saudi commercial-court dispute, but only between merchants and only to accelerate, not to slow down. A dispute-resolution clause that attempts to extend statutory periods is ineffective on this point.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
32:  |  Art. 7; Regs Arts. 38–47  |  Any procedure under the Law may be electronic, including filing and registering claims and requests, hearing them, remote pleading, exchange of memoranda, judgment and objection. The Regulations give an electronic act the same effects as a physical one, permit AI tools, bar a party from denying an opponent's submission merely because it was electronic, route e-litigation through the Ministry's approved systems, allow electronic verification in place of signature, and permit remote hearings in all cases subject to the Ministry's approved means and publicity (a non-merchant party may ask for an in-court hearing within one day of being notified).  |  Commercial litigation runs on the Ministry of Justice's Najiz platform in practice; time stamps follow the court's time zone (Regs Art. 43) and electronic deadlines expire at 23:59 (Regs Art. 5). The platform name "Najiz" is not in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
33:  |  Art. 8(1); Regs Arts. 100–107, 327  |  The Regulations determine conciliation and mediation procedures, including the cases in which conciliation/mediation must precede the hearing, not to exceed 30 days from commencement unless the parties agree a longer period. The draft Regulations require prior conciliation/mediation before registering: (a) single-judge cases (Regs Art. 17), (b) Art. 16(3) claims (drafted as mudaraba partner disputes; since Royal Decree M/191 of 1444H the paragraph covers partnership-contract disputes under the Civil Transactions Law), (c) cases between spouses or relatives to the fourth degree, (d) cases on contracts containing a written agreement to attempt conciliation, mediation or amicable settlement before litigation, (e) other categories the court president designates. Compliance is shown by a certificate that conciliation ended without (or with partial) settlement, or by proof of applying and the period having elapsed. Small claims: prior conciliation not exceeding 15 days. A settlement reached after registration is recorded and takes the force of an enforcement instrument.  |  A contractual multi-tier clause (negotiation/mediation before court) is given procedural teeth: the commercial court will not register the claim until the attempt is evidenced. Draft a fixed, short attempt period; the court-side cap is 30 days unless the parties agreed longer. Whether the approved regulation kept this list must be confirmed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 8) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
34:  |  Arts. 9–12; Regs Arts. 71–83  |  Valid service addresses include: the verified or chosen electronic address; residence; the address registered with the postal authority; the commercial-register address for that activity; the address stated in the case papers; **the address stated in the contract, for disputes arising from that contract**; and an address chosen by a foreigner in the Kingdom. A party may designate a lawyer's address. Change of the case-papers address must be notified within 48 hours or service there remains effective. Electronic service is by SMS to the verified mobile, verified/chosen e-mail, or notification through a government e-account; other addresses are served through a licensed postal provider. A person resident abroad may be served under these rules where the parties have so agreed, without prejudice to international conventions; the Regulations require that address to be recorded in a document between the parties, provide diplomatic-channel service where that fails, and treat the contract address as valid for five years from the contract unless agreed otherwise. Legal persons: service on the legal representative, a general partner, the manager, the legal department, or a branch manager for branch disputes; foreign companies with a branch or commercial representative: on that branch manager or representative.  |  The notices clause of a contract is also the service clause for Saudi litigation: a stale address in the contract can carry valid service for five years. Foreign counterparties should be advised that agreeing to service at a contract address (including e-mail) enables service abroad without letters rogatory.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
35:  |  Art. 13; Regs Arts. 89–93  |  The court may fine a party up to SAR 10,000 for breach of case procedure or hearing order (final). Case data, papers and documents of commercial cases are open to the public for a fee equal to the cost of the service; on application by an interested person the court may declare certain papers or documents confidential vis-à-vis persons other than the court, without prejudice to the publicity of hearings. The Regulations treat a document as confidential where a law or competent-authority decision so provides, where the parties' agreement makes it confidential, or where access would infringe a trade secret; confidentiality cannot be claimed if the parties previously agreed there is none; the court's decision on confidentiality is not subject to any objection.  |  Commercial-court filings are public by default. A contractual confidentiality clause is a recognised ground for a sealing order under the draft Regulations, so NDA and MSA confidentiality clauses should expressly cover litigation filings and require the party filing to seek confidentiality.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
36:  |  Art. 15  |  Absent a special text in the commercial laws or in treaties and conventions to which the Kingdom is party, the international-jurisdiction rules of the Law of Civil Procedure apply to cases within the commercial court's jurisdiction.  |  Whether a Saudi commercial court will take jurisdiction over a foreign defendant is answered by the Law of Civil Procedure (generally: defendant domiciled or resident in the Kingdom, obligation to be performed in the Kingdom, etc.). Those rules are not in this file. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
37:  |  Art. 16 (marked amended on the portal); Regs Arts. 48–50  |  The commercial court has jurisdiction over: (1) disputes between merchants arising from their original or ancillary commercial activities; (2) claims against a merchant in commercial-contract disputes where the principal claim exceeds SAR 100,000 in the text read today (the Supreme Judicial Council may raise this figure); (3) as amended by Royal Decree M/191 of 29/11/1444H, disputes arising from the partnership contracts (عقود المشاركة) provided for in the Civil Transactions Law (original text: disputes between partners in a mudaraba); (4) claims and violations under the Companies Law; (5) under the Bankruptcy Law; (6) under the intellectual-property laws; (7) under the other commercial laws; (8) claims and requests concerning judicial custodians, trustees, liquidators and appointed experts where linked to a case within its jurisdiction; (9) compensation claims for harm arising from a case previously heard by the court. The draft Regulations: item (2) requires a contractual relationship (Regs Art. 48) and, in Regs Art. 49, states the threshold as SAR 500,000; item (3) requires a written contract (Regs Art. 50, drafted against the original mudaraba wording).  |  Merchant-to-merchant contract claims go to the commercial court regardless of value. A non-merchant's claim against a merchant on a commercial contract goes to the commercial court only above the threshold; the Law text in force says SAR 100,000 (the M/191 amendment touched only paragraph (3)) while the draft Regulations say SAR 500,000, so whether the Council has raised the figure must be confirmed before any routing advice. Partnership-contract disputes under the Civil Transactions Law (including mudaraba as one form) are now expressly commercial-court matters. Company-law claims (shareholder disputes, director liability) and bankruptcy matters are commercial-court matters; Regs Art. 8 measures the claim by the sums in the statement of claim excluding costs and fees, or by the value of the underlying obligation for non-monetary relief.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text including the M/191 amendment to paragraph (3)); threshold conflict flagged; `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
38:  |  Art. 17; Regs Arts. 51–55  |  Unless the parties agree otherwise in writing, territorial jurisdiction lies with the court of the defendant's place of residence; if none in the Kingdom, the claimant's; the claim may also be brought where the contract was concluded, performed or was to be performed. Company cases: the court of the company's head office (claims against the company, by the company against a partner, between partners, or against managers/board members); branch-contract disputes may be brought where the branch is. The plea of lack of territorial jurisdiction is lost once the party makes any request or defence, including at the case-preparation stage.  |  A written venue clause choosing a particular Saudi commercial court (e.g. Riyadh) is effective; venue is optional, not exclusive, absent agreement. Raise a venue objection in the very first submission.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
39:  |  Art. 18; Regs Arts. 54, 56–57  |  The court may rule separately on a plea of lack of subject-matter jurisdiction within 20 days of the plea. The Regulations add an administrative jurisdiction report within two working days of registration and a circuit decision within three working days, referral of a no-jurisdiction judgment to appeal the day after filing, and extend Art. 18 to pleas of lack of judicial (ولائي) and territorial jurisdiction.  |  Jurisdiction fights are front-loaded and fast; see Art. 79(2)–(4) for the 10-day appeal and finality of the resulting judgment.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
40:  |  Art. 19; Regs Arts. 116–121  |  In the cases the Regulations specify, the claimant must notify the defendant in writing to perform the claimed right at least 15 days before filing. Before registration, and under court supervision, the parties may undertake conciliation/mediation, exchange of documents and memoranda, or a meeting to define claims, defences and evidence, documented as the Regulations provide. Draft Regulations: the notice is required in all cases within the court's jurisdiction except penalty cases under commercial laws, cases with an administrative body as party, small claims and urgent requests; it must state the parties, subject, claims and supporting document, with proof attached to the statement of claim (failing which the cure procedure of Art. 21 applies); proof of a prior attempt at amicable settlement, conciliation or mediation counts as the notice once the 15-day period has elapsed.  |  Send a formal demand (through a traceable channel) at least 15 days before filing; a contractual notice-of-breach step, if evidenced, does double duty. Pre-filing exchange under Art. 19(2) also interrupts the five-year non-hearing period (Regs Art. 63) and shortens small-claims timelines (Regs Art. 324).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
41:  |  Arts. 20–23; Regs Arts. 94–99, 131–136  |  Claims are brought by a statement of claim filed with the court; the Regulations determine which claims must be filed by a lawyer. The statement must give the parties' details and addresses, enumerate the claims and all grounds; unrelated claims may not be joined. Registration is by the competent administration; deficiencies must be cured within 15 days (registration then dates back to the filing), with a 15-day grievance to the court president (final). The defendant must lodge a defence memorandum with the answer, all pleas, claims and grounds at least one day before the hearing (urgent requests excepted). Draft Regulations: all claims must be filed by a lawyer except Art. 16(2), (8), (9) claims and small claims; pleading by a lawyer is mandatory above SAR 10m (items 1, 2, 8, 9) or SAR 2m (items 3, 4, 6, 7) and in financial-restructuring bankruptcy applications; licensed in-house legal departments of private legal persons and representatives of administrative bodies may act; the first hearing is set within 20 days of registration and at least four days after service (24 hours in urgent cases).  |  Front-loaded pleading: the defence must be complete before the first hearing, and a late-produced defence can be refused (Art. 26). In-house counsel can appear only if licensed by the Ministry.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
42:  |  Art. 24; Regs Arts. 58–67  |  Absent a special text, claims within the court's jurisdiction are not heard after five years from the date the claimed right arose, unless the defendant acknowledges the right or the claimant shows an excuse the court accepts. Draft Regulations: rights arising before the Law run from its entry into force; the period cannot be varied by agreement; waiver of the plea is valid only after the period has run; the period starts when the debt falls due; "excuse" includes a material or moral impediment for its duration; a judicial claim (even before an incompetent court) or an Art. 19(2) pre-filing step interrupts the period; the plea belongs to the debtor and is not raised by the court of its own motion; it may be raised at any stage including on appeal; abandoned or annulled claims count towards the period; non-hearing of the principal claim extends to dependent claims such as against a guarantor.  |  Five years is the working limitation period for commercial claims in the commercial court; a contractual limitation clause (longer or shorter) is not effective under the draft Regulations. Diligence on receivables and claims older than five years should treat them as at risk. Cross-check the Civil Transactions Law's own non-hearing periods where that law applies. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
43:  |  Arts. 25–28; Regs Arts. 68–70, 145–151  |  Proceedings and pleading are in writing, though the circuit may hear the parties orally and must record an oral summary on request; the court may fine or refuse counter/incidental claims from a party who fails to file on time without excuse; adjournment only for a recorded reason and never twice for the same reason; a circuit may delegate preparation of the case to one of its judges. Draft Regulations: statutory periods may be exceeded only for necessity by reasoned decision; maximum duration 180 days at first instance (small claims excepted), 20 days for appeal without hearing, 90 days for appeal with hearing; a pre-trial meeting checks jurisdiction, offers settlement, fixes the issues, evidence, witness list and timetable; at most two pleading hearings after service of the defendant; adjournment after the first hearing not more than 30 days.  |  Plan on roughly six months to a first-instance judgment and three further months on appeal with a hearing, if the draft timetable is the approved one; expert referrals add up to 90 days (Regs Art. 278). Timelines are court-side maxima, not guarantees.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
44:  |  Art. 29(2); Regs Arts. 104–107  |  If the parties settle before the circuit, the settlement is recorded in the hearing minutes, signed by the parties and approved by the presiding judge; the approved minutes are an enforcement instrument (سند تنفيذي), a copy is delivered as for judgments and the case ends. The Regulations extend this to settlements at any stage, even after closing of pleadings or before the appeal court, and to a settlement recorded before the competent administration, and require a copy where the settlement is embodied in an enforcement instrument under the Enforcement Law.  |  A court-recorded settlement is directly enforceable through the enforcement court without a fresh action (see `enforcement-law.md`, Art. 7(1)(c)). Settlement agreements reached in litigation should be recorded in the minutes rather than left as private contracts.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
45:  |  Arts. 30–32; Regs Arts. 154–160  |  A defendant served personally (or through a representative), or who attends any hearing or files a defence, is treated as present even if absent later; if served other than personally and absent, the case is adjourned once and re-served, after which judgment is treated as adversarial; service on a legal person under the Law is always personal service. A claimant absent without accepted excuse: judgment on the defendant's request if the case is ripe, otherwise striking out, with 30 days to apply to resume, failing which the case is deemed never brought. Draft Regulations: a party is absent if not present within 30 minutes of the scheduled time; a defendant served who fails to appear loses the right to raise temporary (procedural) pleas.  |  Companies cannot rely on defective-service defences once served at any Art. 9/11 address; default judgments against corporate defendants are adversarial and appealable only within the ordinary period.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
46:  |  Arts. 33–37; Regs Arts. 161–177  |  Urgent requests (الطلبات المستعجلة) are heard by a single-judge circuit established by the Council, referred immediately and decided within three working days of referral, if necessary in the applicant's presence without the other party. They include: inspection to establish a state of affairs, travel ban, stopping new works, judicial custody, precautionary attachment, obtaining a product sample, preserving specified documents, prohibiting or permitting a disposal, and requests given urgent character by the commercial laws. The order lapses if the main claim is not filed within seven days, if the claim is abandoned or deemed not brought, or if the claimant fails on the merits; any interested person may seek cancellation where urgency has ceased, the case is stayed, interrupted or struck out. The court may require security to compensate the respondent if the applicant proves not entitled, with compensation decided in the merits judgment. Draft Regulations: an urgent request is a matter where delay is feared, without touching the merits; it may be filed before or after the main action; ex parte determination where surprise is needed, with immediate service of the order; a travel-ban request must be accompanied by security of 5% of the principal claim and not less than SAR 50,000; the person banned may lodge security for the claimed amount and is then allowed to travel; urgent decisions are always appealable.  |  Interim relief is available fast and, where surprise matters, ex parte, but it is parasitic on a main claim filed within seven days. Precautionary attachment substance is still governed by the 1433H Enforcement Law's attachment chapter until it is moved into the Law of Civil Procedure (see `enforcement-law.md`, Decree ¶6). Travel bans against foreign executives require real security.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
47:  |  Art. 38  |  Absent a special text in the commercial laws, the evidence chapter applies to cases within the court's jurisdiction; no particular form is required to prove an obligation unless the parties agree otherwise; the court may rely on evidentiary procedures conducted in a foreign state unless contrary to public order; where the parties agree specific evidence rules the court applies them, subject to public order; absent a special text, the burden of proof may be shifted by agreement.  |  Contractual evidence clauses ("entire agreement", "records conclusive absent manifest error", burden-shifting warranties) are given effect in commercial-court litigation subject to public order. Foreign depositions or discovery outputs can be relied on.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
48:  |  Arts. 42–47; Regs Arts. 232–238  |  Statements in official documents bind their maker unless disproved; a private document is deemed to emanate from the person it is attributed to unless that person expressly denies the handwriting, signature, seal or fingerprint (the denial must specify its basis; a party who discussed the document's content cannot later deny it); a copy is deemed to match the original unless disputed, and disputed copies may still serve as presumptions in listed cases; signed or attributable correspondence has the force of a private document. A party may request specific or categorised documents held by the other party that relate to the commercial dealing and are not confidential; refusal to produce what the court orders is a presumption against the refusing party, who may not rely on the withheld document; the court may join a third party or order a government body to produce relevant documents; a document-recovery request is available to an interested person.  |  Targeted document production exists but is narrower than common-law disclosure: requests must identify documents or classes, and contractual confidentiality (Regs Art. 89) is a ground for refusal. Disclosure carve-outs in NDAs for "legally compelled" production remain necessary.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
49:  |  Art. 55; Regs Arts. 260–264  |  Electronic evidence may be treated as proof; it includes electronic records/documents, electronic media, means of communication, e-mail, electronic registers and any other electronic evidence the Regulations specify. Draft Regulations: electronic evidence embodying an official or private document has the same legal effect; evidence from electronic media is valid only if created with the other party's consent or knowledge; it is probative where issued under the Electronic Transactions Law, through a means verified with a government or licensed body or licensed notary, where the contract specified the electronic means, where the opponent discussed its content before denying it, or where drawn from the opponent's public communication channel; the court may appoint an expert to verify it.  |  Name the contractual communication channels (e-mail addresses, platforms, e-signature tool) in the contract; under the draft Regulations that designation makes the resulting electronic records probative without further authentication.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
50:  |  Art. 56; Regs Arts. 265–284  |  The Minister, in coordination with the Council, issues the rules on expert evidence before the court (engagement and selection, fees and expenses, duties and powers, supervision, court expert departments). Draft Regulations: a party may seek leave to use an expert (stating the field, question, cost estimate and proposed name); where parties agree, a single joint expert may be ordered, the court considering value, complexity and speed; a five-day fast opinion is available; the competent administration manages appointment and deposits, and non-deposit can forfeit the right to expert evidence; the expert must disclose relationships and interests, act impartially, keep confidentiality and flag questions outside competence; the report is due within 60 days of the start date, extendable once by 30 days, otherwise replacement with refund; parties may put written questions once within 15 days; the expert's opinion does not bind the court, which must give reasons for departing from it; a party who relies on an expert report allows the other party to rely on it too.  |  Technical and quantum disputes (SaaS SLA credits, construction accounts, valuation) almost always go to a court-appointed expert; budget 60–90 days and the deposit. A contractual agreement to a named expert or to accept an expert determination is honoured between merchants (Regs Art. 11(d)). Separate ministerial expert rules exist and were not read. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
51:  |  Art. 57; Regs Art. 285  |  Absent a special text or contrary agreement, commercial custom or the course of dealing between the parties may be relied on; the party invoking custom or usage must prove it. The court relies on settled commercial custom and may seek assistance to establish it when disputed.  |  Express contract terms displace custom; a party relying on "industry practice" (e.g. renewal or notice customs) bears the burden of proving it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
52:  |  Arts. 58–66; Regs Arts. 178–209  |  Pleadings close when the parties have presented their case; reopening only by reasoned decision; supplementary memoranda may be allowed (draft Regulations: within 10 days, one per party, no new claims, evidence or attachments). Deliberation is secret; judgments issue at least by majority with dissent reasons recorded; the reasoned draft is deposited at pronouncement; pronouncement in public session, adjournable once; a date for delivery of the judgment copy is set within 20 days of pronouncement (draft Regulations: 10 days absent special text). The judgment must state the court, date, circuit and judges, the parties, a summary of facts, claims and material pleas, and the reasons and operative part (insufficient factual reasoning voids the judgment, Regs Art. 193); the enforceable copy bears the enforcement formula and is delivered only to the party with an interest in enforcement. Draft Regulations require the merits judgment to decide the claim for material and moral damages including litigation costs, weighing the harm, the amount awarded, the debtor's procrastination, custom and any expert opinion. The issuing court handles correction and interpretation.  |  The appeal clock runs from the date fixed for delivery of the judgment copy, not from pronouncement (Art. 79). Litigation costs and lawyers' fees are recoverable only as a damages claim that must be pleaded; there is no automatic costs-follow-the-event rule in the Law. Court filing fees under the Judicial Costs Law (نظام التكاليف القضائية) are a separate regime not read for this file. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
53:  |  Art. 65; Regs Arts. 202–205  |  Judgments and orders are immediately enforceable (التنفيذ المعجل) when issued in an urgent case or as payment orders; on the judgment creditor's request the court may order immediate enforcement where delay would cause serious harm, or where the judgment rests on an acknowledgment or on a private document the debtor did not deny. Draft Regulations: statutory immediate enforcement applies even if the judgment is silent; the court may require security; the appeal court may stay immediate enforcement where the grounds of appeal may lead to reversal and serious harm is feared.  |  Ordinary money judgments are not enforceable until final (the 30-day appeal period has run or appeal is exhausted); the Enforcement Law's list of instruments covers "final judgments and judgments covered by immediate enforcement" (see `enforcement-law.md`, Art. 7(1)(a)). Ask for immediate enforcement where the claim rests on an undenied invoice or acknowledgment.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
54:  |  Arts. 67–71; Regs Arts. 210–228  |  By exception to ordinary filing, a creditor may apply for a payment order (أمر أداء) where the right is established in writing, the debt is due, and it is a determinate sum of money or fungible/specific movable; the creditor must first demand payment in writing at least five days before applying, stating the intended application date; the circuit decides within 10 days of registration, refusal being final without prejudice to an ordinary action; the debtor may lodge a grievance (تظلم) before the competent court within 15 days of service, by ordinary claim procedure and stating reasons; the court may stay immediate enforcement on reasoned decision where serious harm is feared. Draft Regulations: the sum is "determinate" if fixed on established bases leaving no discretion to the court; not available where the debtor resides outside the Kingdom, nor for reciprocal obligations unless the creditor attaches written proof of its own performance; venue is the debtor's residence; the application does not bar precautionary measures; the demand is proved through a postal or licensed electronic service provider; the debtor may also appeal for formal defects; the grievance reopens the merits within its scope.  |  For undisputed invoices, signed acknowledgments or written debt instruments against a Saudi-resident debtor, the payment order is the fastest route to an immediately enforceable title (Art. 65(1)(b)). Not usable against a foreign debtor under the draft Regulations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
55:  |  Art. 74; Regs Arts. 288–290  |  Only the losing party, or a party not awarded all its claims, may object; the parties may agree, even before the claim is filed, that the first-instance judgment is final; objections to interlocutory judgments go with the objection to the merits judgment, except that judgments on staying the case, jurisdiction, hearing of the claim, **the plea of an arbitration clause**, and temporary and urgent judgments may be objected to before the merits judgment; objection to an immediately enforceable judgment does not stay it; the objector is not prejudiced by its own objection. Draft Regulations: a finality agreement cannot bind one party only; it must be invoked before any request or defence on appeal; an objector may waive the objection.  |  A mutual "no appeal" clause in a commercial contract is valid and removes the appeal tier; use it deliberately. A refusal to dismiss in favour of arbitration can be appealed immediately rather than after a full trial.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
56:  |  Art. 77; Regs Art. 206  |  Final judgments that have acquired res judicata are conclusive as to what they decided, but only in a dispute between the same parties in the same capacities concerning the same right, subject and cause; the court applies this of its own motion. Reasons acquire res judicata only where inseparably linked to the operative part.  |  Findings in a prior Saudi commercial judgment between the same parties bind a later case on the same right; findings against different parties or on different causes do not.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
57:  |  Arts. 78–79; Regs Arts. 294, 303–312  |  All first-instance judgments and decisions are appealable except small claims not exceeding SAR 50,000 as determined by the Council (draft Regulations: unless the judgment was on jurisdiction, inadmissibility, non-hearing for lapse of time, formal non-acceptance, or a preliminary plea barring the case); the Council determines which appeals are decided without a hearing (draft Regulations: judgments in Art. 16(1)–(3) claims not exceeding SAR 2m, Art. 16(8)–(9) claims, and matters within the single-judge appeal circuit). **The appeal period is 30 days from the date fixed for delivery of the judgment copy; 10 days for judgments on jurisdiction and in urgent cases**, the latter being referred the next day and decided within five working days; a final judgment on subject-matter or territorial jurisdiction is not open to any objection.  |  Diary the copy-delivery date, not the hearing date. Most mid-value contract appeals (≤ SAR 2m) are decided on the papers if the draft Regulations stand. Small claims under SAR 50,000 are single-tier.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
58:  |  Arts. 80–85; Regs Arts. 296–302  |  A single-judge appeal circuit hears objections to single-judge urgent decisions, payment orders, judgments ending or abandoning the case, and other matters the Council designates. Appeal is by petition stating the judgment, grounds and requests (missing elements mean non-acceptance under the draft Regulations); it transfers the case as it stood, limited to what is appealed, and the court may admit new evidence, pleas and defences but **not new claims** (except increased compensation, profits or rent accruing after the final submissions); the respondent may cross-appeal until closing of pleadings; an absent appellant risks the appeal being deemed not brought after one adjournment of up to 30 days; on reversal the case is remitted for alternative claims or, where dismissed on a preliminary ground, for the merits.  |  Frame every head of claim at first instance; new claims are inadmissible on appeal though new evidence may be admitted.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
59:  |  Arts. 86–92; Regs Arts. 313–321  |  Reconsideration (التماس إعادة النظر) of final judgments is available in the cases set by the Law of Civil Procedure, by petition to the court that issued the final judgment (draft Regulations: the appeal circuit that affirmed it; no stay unless ordered for irreparable harm, possibly against security). Cassation before the Commercial Chamber of the Supreme Court lies against appeal-circuit judgments for: violation or misapplication of Sharia or the laws, or of a Supreme Court judicial principle; lack of jurisdiction or improper constitution; error in characterising the facts; or conflict with a prior judgment between the same parties (draft Regulations: only if that prior judgment is final). Cassation does not stay enforcement unless the court so orders on request for irreparable effects (security may be required); a reporting judge screens the petition; on cassation the case is remitted to a differently constituted panel bound by the Supreme Court's principle, or decided by the Supreme Court if ripe, and must be decided by it on a second cassation.  |  Cassation is a legal-error review, not a third instance; appeal-circuit judgments are enforceable immediately absent a stay. Budget for enforcement risk while cassation is pending.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
60:  |  Art. 8(3); Regs Arts. 322–340  |  The Regulations define small claims (الدعاوى اليسيرة), their procedure and judgment. Draft Regulations: small claims are the single-judge cases under Regs Arts. 17–18 (Art. 16(1) claims up to SAR 500,000, Art. 16(2) claims up to SAR 1m, related Art. 16(8)–(9) claims) except construction-contracting claims; decided within 90 days of registration (30 days where pre-filing exchange took place), entirely electronically and remotely unless the circuit decides otherwise, after conciliation not exceeding 15 days; the defendant is served the day of registration with a pre-trial meeting within 15 days; the defence is due one day before that meeting; no claims, evidence or pleas after the pre-trial meeting; expert reports within 20 days (+10); a single hearing (adjournment ≤ 15 days); judgment pronounced within five days of closing and delivered at once or within five days; appeal decided within 15 days of referral unless heard. Draft Regs Art. 17 also sets the single-judge first-instance circuit: Art. 16(1) claims up to SAR 500,000, Art. 16(2) claims up to SAR 1m, and a "requests and orders" circuit for urgent requests and payment orders; Regs Art. 18 lists the single-judge appeal matters.  |  Two different "small claims" concepts coexist: the Law's SAR 50,000 non-appealability line (Art. 78) and the draft Regulations' broader single-judge fast track (up to SAR 500,000 / 1m). Do not conflate them; confirm which the approved regulation adopted.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 8) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text)  | 
61:  |  Art. 8(2); Regs Arts. 341–351  |  The Regulations set the class-action (الدعاوى الجماعية) procedure. Draft Regulations: unity of subject and cause, same defendant, a common objective criterion, at least ten consenting claimants, and a lawyer as representative; leave from the court president within ten days (final), with up to 60 days to reach the headcount; one statement of claim within ten days of leave with a schedule of claims; a public register; the court may appoint an adviser, classify the claimants and allow joinder until closing of pleadings.  |  Consumer- or investor-type group claims against a company can be aggregated; diligence on a target with many similar customer complaints should flag class-action exposure.  |  `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for Art. 8(2) `[BOE — Arabic]`)  | 
62:  |  Art. 93; Art. 96; Decree ¶2–¶4  |  Where the Law is silent, the Law of Civil Procedure applies to cases within the court's jurisdiction insofar as compatible with the commercial nature of the case. The Law entered into force 60 days after publication and repeals conflicting provisions; commercial circuits in general courts serve regions without a commercial court, with appeal to the nearest commercial court; Art. 35 of the Law of Civil Procedure (the former commercial-jurisdiction rule) is repealed.  |  Gaps (joinder, intervention, recusal, letters rogatory) are filled from the Law of Civil Procedure, which is not in this folder.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
63:  |  Cross-reference (no article of this Law)  |  Recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments and of arbitral awards is not governed by this Law. Foreign judgments, orders, arbitral awards, settlement agreements and notarised documents are enforcement instruments only under the Enforcement Law's Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9 (reciprocity, exclusive Saudi jurisdiction, no prior Saudi case, due process, finality, no conflict with a Saudi judgment, public order; foreign awards and settlements per treaty obligations), before the enforcement court. Domestic arbitral awards are enforced through the enforcement order under Arbitration Law Arts. 52–55 and then the Enforcement Law. The commercial court's role is limited to the arbitration-clause plea (Art. 74(4); Arbitration Law Art. 11), to the interlocutory support the Arbitration Law assigns to the "competent court" (the Court of Appeal originally competent, i.e. the commercial appeal circuits for commercial disputes) and, under draft Regs Art. 23(b), to specialised circuits for applications arising under the Arbitration Law.  |  Route any foreign-judgment or award-enforcement question to `enforcement-law.md` (Arts. 7, 9, 11) and `arbitration-law.md` (Arts. 8, 49–55); do not cite this file for those rules.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the cross-referenced texts in the two named files; the routing statement itself is a reading of the texts, `[model knowledge — verify]` as to court practice  | 
64: 
65: ## Open questions for a local practitioner
66: 
67: - **Art. 16(2) threshold.** The Law says SAR 100,000 (unchanged by Royal Decree M/191, which amended only paragraph (3)); the draft Regulations (Art. 49) say SAR 500,000; the article lets the Supreme Judicial Council raise the figure. Confirm whether the Council has raised it and which figure the approved Regulations carry before advising a non-merchant claimant on forum.
68: - **Art. 16(3) after M/191.** The draft Regulations (Arts. 50, 101(b)) still speak of mudaraba; confirm how the approved Regulations treat partnership-contract disputes under the Civil Transactions Law (written-contract requirement, mandatory conciliation, single-judge allocation).
69: - **Status of the Regulations text.** The Ministry of Justice file read is headed "مشروع" and dated 1441/9H. Confirm the approved Implementing Regulations (instrument number and date), and check each regulation row cited above against the approved text, especially: mandatory-conciliation list (Regs Art. 101), lawyer-filing and lawyer-pleading thresholds (Regs Arts. 94–96), single-judge thresholds (Regs Art. 17), small-claims definition (Regs Art. 322), the 5-year contract-address rule (Regs Art. 76), the travel-ban security (Regs Art. 174), the 180/20/90-day maxima (Regs Art. 69), and the 10-day judgment-copy delivery (Regs Art. 191) versus the Law's 20 days.
70: - **Supreme Judicial Council decisions** referred to in Arts. 33, 78, 80 (single-judge circuits, non-appealable small claims, appeals without hearing) were not read; the SAR 50,000 figure in Art. 78(1) is a ceiling "as determined by the Council".
71: - **Expert rules** under Art. 56 (Minister's rules on expert evidence) and the separate **Judicial Costs Law** (court fees) were not read; costs recovery in practice (lawyers' fees as damages, Regs Arts. 182–183) is reported to be conservative. `[model knowledge — verify]`
72: - **International jurisdiction** rules (Art. 15 → Law of Civil Procedure) and the treatment of foreign forum-selection clauses by the commercial court are not covered here; practice on dismissing in favour of a foreign court is unsettled. `[model knowledge — verify]`
73: - **Interaction with the 2026 Enforcement Law**: precautionary attachment under Art. 36 still draws its substantive rules from the 1433H Enforcement Law chapter kept alive by Decree ¶6 to the new law until moved into the Law of Civil Procedure; which text applies to a commercial-court attachment after 2026-10-28 needs confirmation.
74: - **Arbitration-clause plea**: Art. 74(4) makes the ruling on the plea appealable before the merits; whether the commercial court treats a defective or pathological clause as a ground to retain the case, and how the 30-day objection waiver in Arbitration Law Art. 7 interacts, is practice-dependent. `[model knowledge — verify]`
75: 
76: ## How skills should use this file
77: 
78: - **Skills that load this file.** `commercial-legal:vendor-agreement-review`, `commercial-legal:saas-msa-review` and `commercial-legal:nda-review` load it at the dispute-resolution / governing-law / notices clause step (Arts. 6, 9–12, 13, 17, 19, 24, 38, 74; Regs Art. 101 for multi-tier clauses). `commercial-legal:escalation-flagger` loads it when a matter involves an existing or threatened Saudi commercial claim (Arts. 16, 33–37, 65, 67–71, 78–79). `commercial-legal:stakeholder-summary` cites only the Practical-effect column and must carry the row's tag. `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads it when reviewing litigation schedules and receivables (Arts. 16, 24, 65, 77–79; Regs Arts. 341–351 for group-claim exposure). `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` loads Arts. 29(2), 64–65 and 78–79 when a condition precedent depends on a judgment becoming final or a settlement being recorded.
79: - **Cited rows.** A skill citing a rule must quote the Article cell and reproduce the Tag cell verbatim. Where a row draws on the Regulations, the citation must say "draft Regulations text (MoJ file labelled مشروع)" and carry the `[authority — MoJ]` tag; it must not present a regulation rule as settled law.
80: - **Computed dates.** Appeal deadline = date fixed for delivery of the judgment copy + 30 days (Art. 79(1)); + 10 days for jurisdiction and urgent judgments (Art. 79(2)). Payment-order grievance = service + 15 days (Art. 71). Urgent-order lapse = order date + 7 days without a main claim (Art. 35(1)(a)). Limitation = accrual + 5 years (Art. 24). Pre-filing notice = at least 15 days before filing (Art. 19(1)). Each computed date must carry `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the formula and a separate flag that day-counting follows draft Regs Art. 4 (day of service excluded; electronic deadline 23:59 court time), tagged `[authority — MoJ]` (draft).
81: - **Forum routing.** Before stating that a claim belongs in the commercial court, a skill must cite the Art. 16 row and flag the SAR 100,000 / 500,000 threshold conflict for any claim by a non-merchant against a merchant. Foreign-judgment and award enforcement questions are routed to `enforcement-law.md` and `arbitration-law.md`, never answered from this file.
82: - **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule not in this table, or the row it needs is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, or depends on a draft-Regulations row the approved regulation may have changed, it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory or upgrade a tag.

FILE enforcement-law.md
1: # Enforcement Law (1447H) — نظام التنفيذ
2: 
3: <!-- Copied from references/jurisdictions/_TEMPLATE-instrument.md. This is the NEW Enforcement Law of 1447H/2026, not yet in force on the date of reading. -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Enforcement Law / نظام التنفيذ (1447H) |
11: | Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/237 of 3/11/1447H (2026-04-20), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 746 of 26/10/1447H |
12: | Issuing authority | Ministry of Justice (وزارة العدل); enforcement courts (محاكم التنفيذ) under the Supreme Judicial Council; Implementing Regulations to be issued by the Minister of Justice within 180 days of the Law's issue (Art. 64) |
13: | Effective date | 2026-10-28 (Art. 65(2): 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette; portal shows publication 14/11/1447H = 2026-05-01; portal status line "ساري بعد مدة 180 يوم من تاريخ النشر"). Regulations: effective from the date the Law takes effect (Art. 64). **Until 2026-10-28 the Enforcement Law of 1433H (Royal Decree M/53 of 13/8/1433H) remains in force**; its text is not in the source set for this folder and no row below is drawn from it. |
14: | Amendment history | none (new law; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 0 -->`). Replaces the Enforcement Law issued by Royal Decree M/53 of 13/8/1433H and its amendments (Art. 65(1)). Decree ¶8 amends item Third of Royal Decree M/15 of 27/1/1443H (Enforcement before the Board of Grievances). Transitional rules in Decree ¶2–¶7 (see rows). |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/67dd54bc-c33a-48c8-8356-b44700a9ab55/1 |
16: | Official translation | none read (the portal offers a "Translated document" link; no `boe-en` file was provided) |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Arabic, Arts. 1–65 plus Royal Decree and Council of Ministers Decision text) |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page read in full. Issue date 1447/11/03H (2026-04-20); publication 1447/11/14H (2026-05-01); status "ساري بعد مدة 180 يوم من تاريخ النشر"; issuing instruments Royal Decree M/237 and Council of Ministers Decision 746; 0 articles marked amended; no amending instrument named. This instrument post-dates the model's training data; every row was taken from the Arabic text read today and nothing was assumed from the 1433H law. The Implementing Regulations had not been located on the portal or in the source set on the reading date. |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law governs compulsory enforcement (التنفيذ الجبري) of enforcement instruments (السندات التنفيذية) by the enforcement courts: the court's jurisdiction and its limits, what counts as an enforcement instrument, the conditions for enforcing foreign judgments, awards, settlement agreements and notarised documents, the enforcement procedure (registration, order, service, disclosure of assets, compulsory measures, asset tracing, attachment, sale, distribution), direct enforcement of non-monetary obligations, personal-status enforcement, enforcement against private legal persons, enforcement disputes and grievances, criminal penalties for obstructing enforcement, and the licensing of private enforcement-service providers. It excludes execution of criminal penalties and matters within the jurisdiction of the Board of Grievances courts (Art. 4(1)), which have their own enforcement law (Royal Decree M/15 of 1443H). Precautionary attachment (الحجز التحفظي) and civil insolvency (الإعسار) are not in this Law: the corresponding chapters of the 1433H law continue to apply under Decree ¶6–¶7 until moved into the Law of Civil Procedure or replaced by a civil-insolvency statute. The Law is not yet in force on the date of reading; the 1433H law governs enforcement files until 2026-10-28.
23: 
24: ## Rules
25: 
26: Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.
27: 
28:  |  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Practical effect  |  Tag  | 
29:  | --- | --- | --- | --- | 
30:  |  Art. 65; Decree ¶2–¶3  |  The Law replaces the Enforcement Law of 1433H (Royal Decree M/53) and its amendments and takes effect 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette. All judgments, orders and decisions issued before its entry into force are valid under the rules in force before it; the Law does not apply to enforcement-dispute cases in which final judgments were issued before it took effect.  |  Effective 2026-10-28. Enforcement files opened before that date continue under the 1433H law as regards orders already made; the Decree does not expressly say which law governs *pending* files after 2026-10-28 beyond preserving prior orders and excluding finally decided enforcement disputes, so the Regulations or a Council circular must be checked for the handover rule. `[model knowledge — verify]` The 1433H text is not in this folder; skills must not describe it from memory.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
31:  |  Decree ¶5  |  By exception, bills of exchange and promissory notes issued before the Law's entry into force that satisfy the statutory conditions, other than registration on the national electronic platforms, remain enforcement instruments for one year after entry into force.  |  Paper promissory notes and bills held as security expire as enforcement instruments on 2027-10-28 unless registered on the national platform; lenders and landlords holding unregistered notes must re-paper or register within the year.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
32:  |  Decree ¶6–¶7  |  The precautionary-attachment provisions of Part 2 of the 1433H Enforcement Law continue to apply until transferred to the Law of Civil Procedure; the civil-insolvency (إعسار) provisions of Part 5 Chapter 1 of the 1433H law continue until a civil-insolvency statute is issued, with enforcement courts hearing insolvency claims, subject to appeal and without cassation; the Minister of Justice adjusts the 1433H Regulations accordingly.  |  Pre-judgment freezing (precautionary attachment) and individual insolvency stay on the old text even after 2026-10-28; this file cannot state those rules because the 1433H text was not provided.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
33:  |  Decree ¶4, ¶8; Art. 4(1)  |  Enforcement courts enforce decisions and orders of quasi-judicial committees; the enforcement circuits formed under the Board of Grievances enforcement law enforce the excepted committee decisions to which an administrative body is party; the enforcement court's jurisdiction excludes execution of criminal penalties and matters for the Board of Grievances courts.  |  Decisions of committees such as banking, insurance, securities or labor committees go to the enforcement court; decisions against government bodies go to the Board of Grievances route.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
34:  |  Arts. 2–3  |  The enforcement court is composed of first-instance circuits and appeal circuits, or first-instance circuits only (appeals then going to the appeal circuits of the court the Council designates). All competent bodies, including those supervising or registering assets, must respond to the court's enforcement orders and decisions within three working days of notification, absent a special text.  |  Banks, the CMA/Tadawul, the real-estate registry, the commercial register and vehicle registries are on a three-working-day clock to freeze or report.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
35:  |  Art. 4(2)–(3)  |  Subject to Art. 9, the court verifies that the enforcement instrument meets its statutory conditions without examining the underlying right; enforcement requests are allocated under the Regulations.  |  The enforcement judge does not re-open the merits; defences going to the underlying right belong in the court that issued the instrument or in an enforcement dispute limited to the instrument's validity (Art. 45).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
36:  |  Arts. 5–6  |  Where several bodies attach the same asset, the garnishee must inform the later attaching body of the earlier attachment. Where executory attachments from two judicial bodies, or a judicial and an administrative body, coincide on the debtor's assets, all the assets are treated as attached for the benefit of all enforcement requests and the judicial body that first ordered attachment supervises the attachments, sale and distribution; the Minister issues rules with the Council and the Administrative Judicial Council.  |  First-to-attach controls the collective process; a creditor's priority in distribution is a separate question (Art. 35).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
37:  |  Art. 7  |  Compulsory enforcement requires an enforcement instrument for a specified right that is due. The instruments are: (a) final judgments, orders and decisions, and judgments covered by immediate enforcement, issued by the Kingdom's courts; (b) arbitral awards under the relevant statutory texts; (c) settlement agreements and conciliation documents notarised under the Notarisation Law or issued by bodies authorised by law; (d) **bills of exchange and promissory notes, provided they are registered on the national electronic platforms** (registration conditions and procedures in the Regulations); (e) cheques; (f) contracts and acknowledgments notarised under the Notarisation Law; (g) foreign judgments, judicial orders, arbitral awards, settlement agreements and notarised documents, as provided in Art. 9; (h) contracts and documents given the status of enforcement instruments by a law or Council of Ministers decision.  |  Confirmed: commercial paper is limited to bills of exchange and promissory notes **registered on the national e-platforms**, plus cheques as a separate item. A notarised contract (e.g. a lease or loan notarised through the Ministry's notarisation platform) is directly enforceable without a judgment. Ordinary signed contracts and unregistered notes are not enforcement instruments; the creditor must sue (or seek a payment order under the Commercial Courts Law, `commercial-courts-law.md` Arts. 67–71). The platform name (e.g. "Nafith") is not in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
38:  |  Art. 8  |  Where the right in a settlement, notarised contract or statutory-status instrument (items (c), (f), (h)) is conditional on performance of an obligation whose performance cannot be seen from the instrument itself, the right-holder may apply for enforcement accompanied by a report from an expert body accredited by the Ministry confirming that performance.  |  Milestone-based notarised contracts (payment on delivery, completion certificates) can still be enforced directly if an accredited expert certifies the condition was met.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
39:  |  Art. 9(1)  |  Subject to the Kingdom's treaty obligations, a foreign judgment or order may be enforced only on the basis of reciprocity and after verifying: (a) the case was not one within the exclusive jurisdiction of a Saudi judicial body; (b) no similar case was pending in the Kingdom before the foreign case was registered; (c) the parties were summoned, properly represented and enabled to defend themselves; (d) the judgment or order is final under the rules of the issuing body; (e) it does not conflict with a prior judgment or order of a competent Saudi body on the same subject; (f) it contains nothing contrary to public order in the Kingdom.  |  A foreign court judgment (e.g. English or Delaware) is enforceable in the Kingdom only if the foreign state would enforce Saudi judgments (reciprocity) and the six conditions are met; interest components and matters contrary to Sharia are treated as public-order issues in practice. `[model knowledge — verify]` The Riyadh and GCC conventions supply reciprocity for member states. `[model knowledge — verify]` For a contract with a foreign counterparty, an arbitration clause seated in a New York Convention state is the safer enforcement route (next row).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
40:  |  Art. 9(2)–(3)  |  Foreign arbitral awards and foreign settlement agreements are enforced as required by the Kingdom's obligations under treaties and conventions, and after verifying the conditions in Art. 9(1). Foreign notarised documents are enforced after verifying the conditions required by the law of the issuing state, and on the basis of reciprocity regarding the conditions for enforcing such documents.  |  Foreign awards are enforced under the New York Convention (the Kingdom is a party, with a reciprocity reservation) `[model knowledge — verify]`, but the Law layers the Art. 9(1) conditions on top, including public order and the absence of a prior Saudi case; the Singapore Convention on mediated settlements would operate through the same clause if ratified `[model knowledge — verify]`. Domestic awards go through the Arbitration Law's enforcement order first (`arbitration-law.md`, Arts. 52–55).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
41:  |  Art. 10  |  A debtor may apply to the court where the creditor refuses to accept performance of a specified, due right established by an enforcement instrument; the court orders the creditor to accept, records any impossibility, and takes the steps the Regulations set so the debtor can discharge the debt; the costs are enforcement costs.  |  A debtor can force a creditor to take payment (or deposit it with the court) to stop penalties or fines accruing.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
42:  |  Art. 11  |  Without prejudice to the relevant statutory provisions, an enforcement request is not accepted on an instrument whose due date is more than ten years old.  |  Ten-year enforcement window from the due date of the right in the instrument; diligence should flag judgments or notes approaching ten years. Whether the limit applies to instruments already older than ten years on 2026-10-28 is not addressed in the text.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
43:  |  Arts. 12–14  |  The Regulations specify when the creditor must notify the debtor to perform before registering an enforcement request. The competent administration registers the request after checking the requirements; deficiencies must be cured within ten working days or the request is deemed refused, with a ten-working-day grievance under Art. 46. If the instrument meets its conditions, the court orders the debtor to comply; the enforcement order is served immediately, or, if service fails, announced through an appropriate medium, service taking effect from the announcement.  |  Enforcement starts with a court order to pay (أمر التنفيذ); the five-working-day clock in Art. 18 runs from service or announcement, so a debtor cannot stall by evading service.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
44:  |  Arts. 15–17  |  From service or announcement of the enforcement order the debtor must disclose its assets. On the creditor's request, where there are indications of concealment or removal of assets, the court orders disclosure by the debtor's agents and employees, its financial counterparties, debtors and suspected favoured persons, and any garnishee, within what serves the purpose; they must answer within ten working days. Bodies supervising or registering assets must supply data within three working days of the court's order, maintain a unit to process enforcement orders, build ownership databases, and integrate electronically with the Ministry's systems. Cross-border exchange of asset disclosure is on a reciprocal basis, subject to treaties and national security.  |  Third parties dealing with a judgment debtor (banks, customers, group companies, employees) can be ordered to disclose; a company's finance function should expect and be able to answer such orders within ten working days.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
45:  |  Art. 18  |  If five working days pass from service or announcement of the enforcement order without performance, compulsory enforcement against assets begins at once, unless the debtor provides a sufficient bank guarantee, in which case ten further working days are allowed. The measures are: (a) notifying the licensed credit bureaus of the non-performance; (b) attaching the debtor's present and future assets, including entitlements from public bodies, and enforcing against them. In addition the court may impose a fine of up to SAR 5,000 per day of non-performance, up to a cap set by the Regulations, which it may cancel in whole or part on performance; the fine is collected only after distribution of the proceeds and goes to the Treasury.  |  Five working days from service to pay, post a bank guarantee, or face credit-bureau (SIMAH) listing, account freezes and attachment of receivables including government payments; a daily fine is an additional lever. The credit bureau name is not in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
46:  |  Art. 19  |  After the Art. 18(1) period, on the creditor's request the court orders a ban on the debtor's travel outside the Kingdom for up to three years, extendable on a new request, the total not to exceed six years; the ban ends with the enforcement request. The ban does not limit the authorities' power to end a non-Saudi's residence or deport. The court lifts the ban where a certified medical report shows need for treatment abroad, where the total debt is small as defined in the Regulations, where the debtor's profession or job requires foreign travel, where the ban would harm the debtor, or where the debtor discloses sufficient unencumbered assets.  |  Travel bans are now time-capped (3 + 3 years) and require a creditor request; a foreign executive who is a personal debtor (e.g. as guarantor) is exposed to a ban but also to deportation, which the ban does not prevent. Whether managers of a debtor company can be banned is left to Art. 41 and the Regulations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
47:  |  Arts. 20–22  |  If the debtor still does not comply after compulsory measures begin, the court, on the creditor's request and where there are indications of concealment or removal, orders asset tracing (costs are enforcement costs), may let the creditor and enforcement agents see the asset data it holds, and may itself, or through the competent administration or a licensed private tracing provider, interrogate the debtor and suspected recipients of removed assets (relatives, agents, employees, financial counterparties, debtors, favoured persons), who must respond within three working days and may be brought by force; competent and supervisory bodies may be ordered to trace and report.  |  Intra-group transfers and related-party payments by a judgment debtor are within reach; group entities and their officers can be summoned.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
48:  |  Arts. 23–24  |  All the debtor's assets secure its debts from the date of the pre-filing notice (Art. 12) or of service/announcement of the enforcement order (Art. 14); executory attachment may be laid on assets already under precautionary attachment, the garnishee informing the precautionary attacher. The creditor may sue to annul a disposal by the debtor made after that date and before the attachment order, in whole or part up to the instrument's amount and against the transferee, where the disposal was a gift or donation, payment of a debt before maturity, or a contract or financial commitment unusual for a person in the debtor's position as the court assesses; a disposal of attached assets after the attachment order is void.  |  A statutory claw-back against transferees for gifts, early repayments and unusual contracts made after the debtor is on notice; counterparties acquiring assets from a known judgment debtor take title risk. Diligence should check the seller's enforcement record before an asset purchase.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
49:  |  Arts. 25–26  |  Attachment is limited to the amount of the debt unless the asset is indivisible; the court selects the assets to enforce against for speed of recovery, and the debtor with surplus assets may nominate the asset to be sold first. Exempt from attachment and enforcement: public funds; the residence and vehicle of the debtor or dependants within their needs, tools of the debtor's own trade and personal effects (unless mortgaged to the creditor or the creditor's own property); wages and salaries beyond one half (maintenance debts) or one third (other debts) of the gross amount, with a combined rule on competing claims; civil and military pensions beyond one half (maintenance) or one quarter (other); government aid; and whatever the Council of Ministers decides.  |  Employers served with a salary attachment for a non-maintenance debt withhold one third of gross salary; for maintenance one half. Relevant to `employment-legal` payroll queries.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
50:  |  Arts. 27–28  |  Bodies supervising or registering assets must execute the court's attachment immediately, with police support where force is needed; the Regulations govern seizure, deposit, management, custody and attachment of future entitlements. Where the court has evidence or indications of criminal concealment, removal, dissipation or false statements by the debtor or others, it refers the matter to the investigating authority while enforcement continues.  |  Concealment behaviour triggers a criminal referral (Public Prosecution) in parallel with enforcement; see Arts. 50–56 for penalties.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
51:  |  Arts. 29–31  |  The court may allow the debtor to sell assets voluntarily where that serves enforcement; otherwise attached assets are sold by public auction; the sale contract is concluded on the award decision (قرار الترسية), which clears the asset of any claim against the buyer; listed securities are sold under rules agreed with the capital-market regulator to secure a fair price. The price is released to the creditor only after title transfer is completed; an occupier of transferred real estate must vacate unless holding an enforcement instrument conferring a right to use it, failing which Art. 36 direct-enforcement measures apply on the transferee's request.  |  Buyers at enforcement auctions take clean title by statute; a tenant under a notarised (enforceable) lease survives the sale, an unregistered occupier does not.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
52:  |  Art. 32  |  Enforcement costs are borne by the debtor unless caused by another person, who then bears them by appealable decision; the creditor bears them where it ends the request by agreement with the debtor or by releasing the debt, unless agreed otherwise; if costs are not advanced (by the debtor, else by the creditor or an interested party) the enforcement steps are suspended.  |  Settlement agreements that end an enforcement file should allocate the enforcement costs expressly; the default puts them on the creditor who withdraws.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
53:  |  Arts. 33–35  |  The Ministry maintains enforcement bank accounts and deposit funds; bank participation in enforcement is set with the Saudi Central Bank. Sufficient proceeds are distributed among attaching creditors and parties to the enforcement as the Regulations provide; insufficient proceeds are distributed by agreement recorded by the court or, failing agreement, by court order giving priority to preferred debts (الديون الممتازة) under the applicable laws, the distribution order being appealable.  |  Ranking among competing creditors outside bankruptcy follows statutory preferences (e.g. secured creditors, wages, maintenance) `[model knowledge — verify]`; the enforcement court does not create a pari passu pool of its own.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
54:  |  Arts. 36–37  |  Direct enforcement (obligation to do or abstain): if not performed within five working days of service/announcement, the court immediately orders the police to carry out the necessary steps (unless the act must be done by the debtor personally) and, where relevant, writes to the competent body; where that is impossible or the act is personal, the court orders a travel ban on the creditor's request (Art. 19) and imposes a fine of up to SAR 10,000 per day up to a Regulations cap, cancellable on performance; the Regulations cover eviction and disposal of movables. If thirty working days pass after any such measure without performance, the court on the creditor's request orders imprisonment of the debtor to compel performance for up to 180 days, extendable on a further request; no imprisonment of minors under 18, of persons medically unfit, of a pregnant woman or a mother of a child under two, or of an ascendant or descendant of the creditor; imprisonment is separate from criminal prisoners and does not extinguish the right.  |  Specific-performance style obligations (deliver source code, hand over premises, cease use of a mark) are enforced by police action, daily fines and, ultimately, coercive imprisonment; the imprisonment route is confined in this text to direct (non-monetary) enforcement and to the personal-status and legal-person contexts of Arts. 39 and 41.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
55:  |  Arts. 38–40  |  Personal-status instruments are enforced by the Law's methods; periodic payments per the Regulations; custody and visitation orders are enforced by force, including entry into homes, and re-enforced as often as needed; direct-enforcement rules apply to a person refusing to comply, with regard to the nature of the matter and the child's interest.  |  Outside the commercial skills' scope; included for completeness of the instrument.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
56:  |  Art. 41  |  In enforcement against a private legal person, the direct-enforcement provisions apply to those of its personnel who cause the obstruction of enforcement, under the controls and procedures set by the Regulations; the Regulations set the rules for enforcement against private legal persons consistent with the Law and related laws and the nature of such enforcement.  |  Managers, directors or finance officers who obstruct enforcement against their company face the direct-enforcement measures personally (fines, travel ban, potentially imprisonment) once the Regulations define "obstruction"; a governance and escalation point for any Saudi subsidiary with a judgment against it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
57:  |  Arts. 42–43  |  The creditor must close the request within the Regulations' period after satisfaction. A request ends on satisfaction; on a court finding that the debtor has no assets (appealable decision); when all measures have been taken and the request's period has run since the last step; when enforcement proves impossible (appealable); when the creditor ends it by agreement or otherwise; or when the creditor releases the debtor. Ending on the second to fifth grounds does not bar the creditor from resuming under the Law when justified.  |  A "no assets" closure is not a discharge; the creditor can reopen when assets appear, subject to the ten-year rule in Art. 11.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
58:  |  Arts. 44–45  |  Except as the Law provides, all the court's orders and decisions are final. Any interested person, party or not, may raise an enforcement dispute (منازعة تنفيذ) concerning the validity of the enforcement instrument, the conditions for its enforcement, or the court's jurisdiction to enforce it; the court decides it under the rules for urgent cases and the Regulations' procedure; the Council determines which enforcement-dispute judgments are not appealable; appeal judgments in enforcement disputes are not subject to cassation, except where the Council permits.  |  Enforcement disputes are narrow (instrument validity, conditions, jurisdiction) and summary; a third party whose asset is attached uses this route. Two tiers at most, cassation only by Council designation.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
59:  |  Art. 46  |  Any interested person may lodge a grievance (تظلم) against orders and decisions on enforcement procedures, as specified in the Regulations, issued by the court, the competent administration or the private sector; the grievance is heard by the court president (or the Council's designee) within ten working days from the day after the decision was made available, with power to suspend the challenged order pending decision (suspension stops all related steps and periods); the grievance decision is final.  |  Procedural complaints (wrong asset frozen, excessive attachment, a private provider's conduct) have a ten-working-day window and one level of review.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
60:  |  Art. 47  |  An enforcement dispute, a dispute over the underlying right, or a grievance does not stop the enforcement steps unless the body seised of the dispute or grievance decides otherwise; a stay stops all related steps and periods.  |  No automatic stay: a debtor must obtain an express suspension. Combine with Arbitration Law Art. 54 (annulment action does not stay enforcement absent an order) and Commercial Courts Law Art. 74(5).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
61:  |  Arts. 48–50  |  The competent (criminal) court tries the offences in the penalties chapter on the Public Prosecution's case; Ministry and other public employees named by the Minister have judicial-police status. Imprisonment up to three years and/or a fine up to SAR 1,000,000 for: (a) concealing or removing assets to prevent satisfaction of the right; (b) deliberately obstructing enforcement by refusing to comply with the enforcement order, refusing to disclose assets, bringing a case to obstruct enforcement, disposing of attached assets after the attachment order, or not responding to interrogation; (c) resisting enforcement by threatening or assaulting enforcement officers, licensees or the creditor; (d) deliberately giving misleading or false data before the court or during enforcement. Accomplices (agreement, incitement, assistance with knowledge) receive the same penalty.  |  Non-disclosure and litigation brought to delay enforcement are now offences; advisers who assist in structuring asset transfers post-notice risk accomplice liability. Escalate any instruction to "move assets" from a judgment debtor.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
62:  |  Arts. 51–58  |  A public official who prevents or obstructs enforcement: up to five years (a breach-of-trust offence). A debtor who dissipates substantial assets: up to fifteen years, even if insolvent (a major offence requiring detention), with the Public Prosecutor setting the thresholds with the Ministries of Justice and Finance. Up to three years and/or SAR 100,000 for: a creditor who seeks enforcement steps to harm the debtor or delays closing the file after satisfaction; anyone disclosing asset data without legal cause, or accessing it without a court order; a licensed enforcement-service provider who misappropriates or evades handing over assets; valuers, sale agents or auction participants who manipulate prices. Up to 90 days and/or SAR 30,000 for refusing to comply with custody, guardianship or visitation orders. The heavier penalty applies where another law also covers the act; recidivism within three years of a final judgment may double the penalty; licensed providers face warning, fines up to SAR 300,000, licence suspension up to a year, or revocation, decided by a Ministry committee subject to challenge before the administrative court; publication of the judgment at the offender's expense and compulsory rehabilitation programmes may be ordered.  |  Creditors are also exposed: abusive enforcement and failing to close a satisfied file are offences. Asset-data confidentiality now carries criminal sanction for anyone who receives it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
63:  |  Arts. 59–63  |  The Law of Civil Procedure applies residually. Except imprisonment orders, travel bans, deciding disputes and grievances, and issuing judgments, the Minister may assign enforcement steps to the competent administration, to another public body by agreement, or to the private sector (licensed in the Kingdom, technically and financially capable, free of even potential conflicts, bound to confidentiality beyond the assignment). The Ministry licenses and supervises enforcement-service providers (judicial sale agents, judicial custodians, asset-recovery and asset-tracing providers, visitation-enforcement providers and others), under a Minister's regulation covering licensing, financial guarantees, qualification, duties, discipline and fee policy. All enforcement decisions and steps are taken through approved electronic means, falling back to ordinary means only where electronic execution is impossible; the Ministry issues procedural guides, decisions and forms.  |  Expect private tracing and asset-recovery firms acting under court authority; their data-handling obligations feed into PDPL analysis. Enforcement is platform-based (Najiz in practice). `[model knowledge — verify]`  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
64:  |  Art. 64  |  The Minister issues the Implementing Regulations within 180 days of the Law's issue, effective from the Law's effective date.  |  Regulations due by about 2026-10-17 (180 days from 2026-04-20); many operative details (pre-filing notice cases, fine caps, "small amount" for lifting travel bans, note-registration platform rules, legal-person obstruction rules, grievance list) depend on them. None were available on the reading date.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`  | 
65:  |  1433H law (Royal Decree M/53 of 13/8/1433H), applicable until 2026-10-28  |  Not read: the 1433H Enforcement Law and its Implementing Regulations are not in the source set. From model knowledge only: it created the enforcement judge (قاضي التنفيذ), listed enforcement instruments including commercial papers generally (bills of exchange, promissory notes and cheques) without a registration requirement, provided a five-day compliance period after service of the enforcement order, and authorised travel bans, account freezes, credit-bureau listing, asset disclosure orders and imprisonment for procrastination, with foreign judgments enforced on reciprocity under conditions similar to Art. 9 above.  |  Any advice on an enforcement file before 2026-10-28, or on a file opened before that date, must be given by Saudi counsel from the 1433H text. This row exists so skills know the gap is deliberate.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]`  | 
66: 
67: ## Open questions for a local practitioner
68: 
69: - **Pending files at 2026-10-28.** Decree ¶2–¶3 preserve prior orders and finally decided disputes; they do not say whether steps in a pending enforcement file after the effective date follow the new procedure (e.g. the new Art. 19 travel-ban caps, Art. 11 ten-year limit, Art. 18 fine). Confirm the Regulations' transitional article and any Supreme Judicial Council circular.
70: - **Implementing Regulations.** Not available on the reading date. Items that cannot be advised on without them: which creditors must give a pre-filing notice (Art. 12); the daily-fine caps (Arts. 18(3), 36(3)(b)); the "small amount" threshold that lifts a travel ban (Art. 19(3)(b)); the conditions for registering bills and notes on the national platforms (Art. 7(1)(d)); the definition of "obstruction" by a legal person's personnel (Art. 41); the list of grievable orders (Art. 46); the period to close a satisfied file (Art. 42); the bank-participation rules with SAMA (Art. 33(2)).
71: - **Precautionary attachment and insolvency** remain under the 1433H text (Decree ¶6–¶7); the text was not provided, so this folder cannot support a pre-judgment freezing analysis.
72: - **Foreign judgments.** Art. 9(1)(b) bars enforcement where a "similar case" was pending in the Kingdom before the foreign case was registered; how the enforcement court treats a Saudi case filed *after* the foreign case but before enforcement, and how reciprocity is proven for common-law states, is practice-dependent. `[model knowledge — verify]`
73: - **Foreign awards.** Art. 9(2) applies the Art. 9(1) conditions "in addition" to treaty obligations; whether the court reads this as adding grounds beyond New York Convention Art. V (e.g. the prior-Saudi-case ground) needs confirmation once the Law is applied. `[model knowledge — verify]`
74: - **Interest and Sharia public order.** The Law does not address the severance of interest components in foreign judgments or awards; practice under the 1433H law was to enforce the principal and refuse interest. `[model knowledge — verify]`
75: - **Art. 41 personal exposure of officers** of a debtor company (travel ban, imprisonment) depends entirely on the Regulations; until issued, advise only that exposure exists in principle.
76: - **Corporate guarantees and cheques.** Art. 7(1)(e) keeps cheques as enforcement instruments without a registration condition, unlike bills and notes; confirm whether the Regulations add conditions (e.g. bank certification of dishonour) before advising on cheque-based security.
77: 
78: ## How skills should use this file
79: 
80: - **Skills that load this file.** `commercial-legal:vendor-agreement-review` and `commercial-legal:saas-msa-review` load it at the dispute-resolution, security/guarantee and payment-terms steps (Arts. 7, 8, 9, 11, 18, 24; Decree ¶5 for promissory-note security). `commercial-legal:nda-review` loads Arts. 36–37 when assessing whether injunctive-type obligations are enforceable in the Kingdom. `commercial-legal:escalation-flagger` loads Arts. 18–19, 24, 41 and 50–52 whenever a client or counterparty is a judgment debtor or is asked to move assets. `commercial-legal:stakeholder-summary` cites only the Practical-effect column and carries the row tag. `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads Arts. 7, 11, 23–24, 41, 43 and 50–52 when reviewing litigation, enforcement and related-party-transfer schedules. `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` loads Art. 7 and Art. 9 when a closing deliverable is an enforceable instrument (notarised agreement, registered note) or when a condition depends on enforcement of a foreign judgment or award.
81: - **Effective-date gate.** Every citation of this file must state: "Enforcement Law 1447H (Royal Decree M/237), in force 2026-10-28; until then the 1433H law applies and is not in this folder." A skill asked about an enforcement step dated before 2026-10-28 must stop and route to Saudi counsel; it must not answer from the last row's model-knowledge summary.
82: - **Computed dates.** Effective date = publication 2026-05-01 + 180 days = 2026-10-28 (Art. 65(2)). Compulsory measures start = service/announcement of enforcement order + 5 working days (Art. 18(1)); + 10 further working days if a bank guarantee is lodged. Imprisonment for direct enforcement = 30 working days after any Art. 36 measure (Art. 37(1)). Travel ban ≤ 3 years, aggregate ≤ 6 years (Art. 19(1)). Enforcement window = due date + 10 years (Art. 11). Grievance = 10 working days from the day after the decision is made available (Art. 46(2)). Grace period for unregistered bills/notes = 2026-10-28 + 1 year (Decree ¶5). Each computed date carries `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the formula; "working days" are not defined in the Law and the Regulations must be checked, so add `[model knowledge — verify]` to any calendar conversion.
83: - **Foreign-judgment and award questions.** Answer only from the Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9 rows plus `arbitration-law.md` Arts. 52–55; state reciprocity and public order as conditions and flag the interest point as `[model knowledge — verify]`.
84: - **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule not in this table, or a row it needs is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, or the answer depends on the unissued Implementing Regulations or on the 1433H text, it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory or upgrade a tag.

