FILE arbitration-law.md
1: # Law of Arbitration — نظام التحكيم
2: 
3: <!-- Copied from references/jurisdictions/_TEMPLATE-instrument.md. Covers the Law, its Implementing Regulations, and the SCCA Arbitration Rules 2023 as the institutional layer. -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Law of Arbitration / نظام التحكيم; Implementing Regulations of the Law of Arbitration / اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام التحكيم; SCCA Arbitration Rules (second edition, effective 1 May 2023) with Appendix I (costs and fees), Appendix II (Expedited Procedure Rules), Appendix III (Emergency Arbitrator Procedure Rules) and Appendix IV (Online Dispute Resolution Procedure Rules) / قواعد التحكيم للمركز السعودي للتحكيم التجاري |
11: | Issuing instrument | Law: Royal Decree No. M/34 of 24/5/1433H (2012-04-16), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 156 of 17/5/1433H. Regulations: Council of Ministers Decision No. 541 of 26/8/1438H (2017-05-22). SCCA Rules: adopted by the Saudi Center for Commercial Arbitration (المركز السعودي للتحكيم التجاري), a licensed arbitral institution, not a statutory instrument. |
12: | Issuing authority | Law and Regulations: Council of Ministers (the Regulations were prepared by the Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of Commerce and Industry in coordination with the Supreme Judicial Council, Decision 156 ¶2). SCCA Rules: SCCA (sadr.org). |
13: | Effective date | Law: 2012-07-08 (Art. 58: 30 days after publication; portal shows publication 18/7/1433H = 2012-06-08). Regulations: 2018-11-29 (Regs Art. 19: effective on publication; portal shows publication 21/3/1440H). SCCA Rules: 2023-05-01, applying to arbitrations commenced on or after that date (Rules Art. 2(2)). |
14: | Amendment history | Law: (i) Royal Decree No. M/8 of 18/1/1443H deleted Art. 24(1) (the separate arbitrator-fee contract and its deposit); (ii) Royal Decree No. M/21 of 26/1/1447H amended Art. 10(1) (capacity to agree to arbitrate) and Art. 50(1)(b) (annulment ground of incapacity, wording change). Regulations: Council of Ministers Decision No. 249 of 7/4/1444H deleted Regs Art. 7 (deposit of the arbitrator contract with the SCCA). SCCA Rules: the 2023 edition replaced the first edition of 1 May 2016 (Rules introduction). |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | Law: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/5535039e-13da-43f6-8f53-a9a700f26485/1 ; Regulations: https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/12752a60-2a4a-4cab-a05d-a9a700f274a5/1 ; SCCA Rules (English official text): https://www.sadr.org/public/upload/pdf-files/2023-Arbitration-Rules-En.pdf |
16: | Official translation | Law and Regulations: the portal lists an English language page ("الإنجليزية (English)") and a "Translated document" attachment; neither was read today (no `boe-en` file was provided). SCCA Rules: the English text is an official SCCA text and was read; the Arabic SCCA text was not. |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Law, Arabic, Arts. 1–58 including the portal's in-force amendment blocks for Arts. 10, 24 and 50; Regulations, Arabic, Arts. 1–19 including the deletion note for Art. 7; SCCA Rules, English, Arts. 1–46 and Appendices I–IV, read for the articles cited) |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: Law page read; status "ساري"; issue 1433/05/24H; publication 1433/07/18H; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 3 -->` (Arts. 10, 24, 50); the amendment blocks name Royal Decree M/8 of 18/1/1443H and Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H; the in-force wording of each amended article was read and is what the rows cite. Regulations page read; status "ساري"; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 1 -->` (Art. 7, deleted by Council of Ministers Decision 249 of 7/4/1444H). SCCA Rules: the 2023 PDF (printed 27 February 2024) was read; whether the SCCA has since amended its Rules or fee schedule was not checked. A draft new Arbitration Law was reported to have been published for public consultation in September 2025; it was not in the source set and nothing from it is used: `[model knowledge — verify]`. |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law applies to every arbitration conducted in the Kingdom, whatever the nature of the legal relationship, and to international commercial arbitration conducted abroad where the parties agree to subject it to the Law, without prejudice to Islamic Sharia and the international conventions the Kingdom is party to (Art. 2). It does not apply to personal-status disputes or to matters not open to compromise. It covers the arbitration agreement (form, capacity, effect on court proceedings, separability), the tribunal (composition, qualifications, appointment, challenge, removal), interim measures by the court and the tribunal, the procedure, the applicable law, the award (time limit, form, deposit, interpretation and correction), the single remedy of annulment, and the enforcement order. The Regulations add the identity of the competent court, notification mechanics, appointment and challenge details, and the Supreme Court's role in appeals. The SCCA Rules are the institutional rules most often chosen for Saudi-seated arbitration; they bind only by agreement and yield to any mandatory provision of the applicable arbitration law (Rules Art. 2(4)). Enforcement of awards after the enforcement order, and enforcement of foreign awards, is governed by the Enforcement Law: see `enforcement-law.md` (Arts. 7(1)(b), 7(1)(g), 9(2)), cross-referenced here but not restated.
23: 
24: ## Rules
28:  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Tag 
29: --- | --- | ---
30:  Law Art. 2  |  Without prejudice to Islamic Sharia and international conventions to which the Kingdom is party, the Law applies to every arbitration, whatever the legal relationship, conducted in the Kingdom, or to international commercial arbitration conducted abroad whose parties agree to subject it to the Law. It does not apply to personal-status disputes or to matters in which compromise (صلح) is not permitted.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
31:  Law Art. 3  |  Arbitration is international if the dispute concerns international trade and: the parties' principal places of business were in different states when the agreement was made (a party with several places: the one most connected; none: habitual residence); or both were in the same state but the seat, the place of performance of a substantial part of the obligations, or the place most connected with the dispute is outside it; or the parties agreed to a permanent arbitral organisation or centre seated outside the Kingdom; or the subject-matter is connected with more than one state.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
32:  Law Arts. 4–5  |  Where the Law lets the parties choose a procedure, they may delegate the choice to a third party, including any individual, body, organisation or arbitration centre inside or outside the Kingdom. Where the parties subject their relationship to a document (model contract, international convention or other), its arbitration provisions apply insofar as not contrary to Sharia.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
33:  Law Art. 7  |  A party that continues with the arbitration knowing of a breach of a derogable provision of the Law or of the arbitration agreement, without objecting within the agreed period or, absent agreement, within 30 days of learning of it, is deemed to have waived its right to object.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
34:  Law Art. 8; Regs Arts. 2, 17  |  The competent court for annulment actions and the matters the Law refers to it is the Court of Appeal originally competent to hear the dispute; for international commercial arbitration, in or outside the Kingdom, the Court of Appeal originally competent in Riyadh unless the parties agree another Court of Appeal in the Kingdom. The Regulations confirm "competent court" means that Court of Appeal except in Art. 9(1), Art. 12 and the end of Art. 40(3) of the Law (court proceedings on the merits), and designate the Supreme Court as the appeal body against an annulment judgment (Art. 51(2)) and as the body hearing the grievance against refusal of enforcement (Art. 55(3)).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
35:  Law Art. 9  |  An arbitration agreement may precede the dispute, as a clause or a separate agreement, or follow it even after a court case is filed, in which case it must define the matters submitted or is void. It must be in writing or is void. It is written if contained in a document issued by the parties, or in exchanged authenticated correspondence, telegrams or other electronic or written means of communication; a reference in a contract to a document containing an arbitration clause is an arbitration agreement, and a reference to a model contract, international convention or other document containing an arbitration clause counts as a written agreement if the reference clearly makes the clause part of the contract.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
36:  Law Art. 10 (para. 1 as amended by Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H)  |  An arbitration agreement is valid only if made by a person with capacity to dispose (أهلاً للتصرف), whether a natural or a legal person (in-force text). Government bodies may not agree to arbitrate except with the approval of the Prime Minister, unless a specific statutory text permits it (para. 2, unamended).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text) 
37:  Law Arts. 11–12  |  A court seised of a dispute covered by an arbitration agreement must rule the case inadmissible if the defendant so pleads before any request or defence; filing the court case does not prevent commencing or continuing the arbitration or issuing the award. If the parties agree to arbitrate while the case is pending, the court must refer the dispute to arbitration.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
38:  Law Arts. 13–14  |  The tribunal consists of one or more arbitrators in an odd number, or the arbitration is void. An arbitrator must have full capacity, be of good conduct, and hold at least a university degree in Sharia or law (العلوم الشرعية أو النظامية); in a multi-member tribunal the degree requirement need be met only by its president.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
39:  Law Art. 15; Regs Arts. 4, 10  |  Parties may agree how to choose arbitrators; failing agreement, the competent court appoints a sole arbitrator (Regs: within 15 days of the request); for three, each party appoints one within 15 days of the other's request and the two agree the third within 15 days of the last appointment, failing which the court appoints on application within 15 days, the third arbitrator presiding (same rule for larger tribunals); the court also steps in where the agreed procedure breaks down or a third party fails to act; it applies the parties' and the Law's conditions and decides within 30 days; its appointment decision is not separately appealable. The applicant attaches the request for arbitration and the arbitration agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
40:  Law Arts. 16–19; Regs Arts. 5–6  |  An arbitrator must have no interest in the dispute and must disclose in writing, on appointment and throughout, any circumstances raising justifiable doubts about impartiality or independence; is barred in the same cases as a judge; may be challenged only for serious doubts about impartiality/independence or lack of agreed qualifications (without prejudice to Art. 14); a party may not challenge its own appointee except for later-discovered reasons. Absent agreed procedure: written challenge to the tribunal within five days of learning of the constitution or the ground; if the arbitrator does not withdraw and the other party does not agree within five days, the tribunal decides within 15 days; the challenger may go to the competent court within 30 days, whose judgment is final; no repeat challenge on the same grounds; a challenge before the tribunal suspends the arbitration (a court application does not); a successful challenge voids the proceedings including any award. Regs: an arbitrator may withdraw without giving reasons; no challenge after closing of pleadings. Removal by the court for inability or unjustified delay on either party's request (final); otherwise only by joint agreement, with compensation for a removed arbitrator not at fault; a replacement is appointed by the original method and proceedings are suspended meanwhile (except after a successful challenge).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
41:  Law Arts. 20–21  |  The tribunal rules on pleas to its jurisdiction, including that no arbitration agreement exists, has lapsed, is void or does not cover the dispute; such pleas must be raised by the time of the statement of defence (Art. 30(2)) (appointing an arbitrator is no waiver), and pleas that a matter raised during the proceedings falls outside the agreement must be raised at once; late pleas may be admitted for acceptable reasons; the tribunal decides jurisdiction before or with the merits, and rejection of the plea is reviewable only through an annulment action against the final award. An arbitration clause is an agreement independent of the contract's other terms; nullity, rescission or termination of the contract does not affect a clause valid in itself.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
42:  Law Arts. 22–23  |  The competent court may order interim or precautionary measures on a party's request before the arbitration begins, or on the tribunal's request during it, and may reverse them likewise, unless otherwise agreed; the court may order letters rogatory on the tribunal's request; the tribunal may ask any body for assistance (summoning witnesses or experts, producing documents) without prejudice to its own powers. If the parties so agree, the tribunal may on a party's request order either party to take interim or precautionary measures required by the nature of the dispute, may require security from the applicant, and, on non-compliance, may authorise the other party to take the steps needed to enforce it, without prejudice to asking the competent authority to compel compliance.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
43:  Law Art. 24 (para. 1 deleted by Royal Decree M/8 of 18/1/1443H); Regs Art. 7 (deleted by Council of Ministers Decision 249 of 7/4/1444H)  |  The former obligation to sign a separate fee contract with each arbitrator and deposit a copy (Law Art. 24(1); Regs Art. 7 naming the SCCA or the institution as depository) no longer applies. In force: if the parties and the arbitrators do not agree the arbitrators' fees, the competent court fixes them by a decision not subject to any appeal, and must fix them whenever it appointed the arbitrators.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text) 
44:  Law Arts. 25–27; Regs Arts. 8–9, 11  |  The parties may agree the procedure, including adopting the rules of any arbitration organisation or centre in or outside the Kingdom, provided they do not conflict with Sharia; absent agreement the tribunal chooses, observing Sharia and the Law (Regs: notifying the parties at least ten days before applying its chosen procedure). Proceedings begin on the day a party receives the request for arbitration from the other (with multiple parties, the last to receive it), unless agreed otherwise; the Regulations list the request's contents (parties, representatives, contract summary, arbitration agreement, subject, facts, claims, proposal or notice of arbitrator appointment) and provide that disputes over its adequacy do not stop constitution. Parties are treated equally with a full and equal opportunity to present their case.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
45:  Law Art. 28  |  The parties may agree the place of arbitration in or outside the Kingdom; absent agreement the tribunal fixes it considering the circumstances and convenience; the tribunal may meet anywhere to deliberate, hear witnesses, experts or parties, inspect or examine documents.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
46:  Law Art. 29  |  Arbitration is conducted in Arabic unless the tribunal decides or the parties agree on another language or languages; the choice governs written statements, oral pleadings, and every decision, communication and award unless otherwise provided; the tribunal may require translation of some or all documents.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
47:  Law Arts. 30–37; Regs Arts. 12–14  |  Written statement of claim and defence within the agreed or tribunal-set periods (counterclaims and set-off allowed, even later if justified), with copies of every submission and expert report to the other party; amendments allowed unless refused to avoid delay; hearings are held unless the tribunal decides documents-only (subject to contrary agreement), with sufficient notice and signed minutes; a claimant's failure to file its statement ends the proceedings, a respondent's failure does not; absence from a hearing or failure to produce documents lets the tribunal proceed on the evidence before it; the tribunal may appoint experts (Regs: the appointment decision fixes mission, deadline, fees and advance, and non-payment of the advance may forfeit the party's reliance on the expert), parties must cooperate, the report is circulated for comment and the expert may be examined at a hearing; joinder or intervention of a non-party requires the consent of all parties and the joined party (Regs Art. 13); matters outside the tribunal's authority, forgery pleas or criminal proceedings suspend the arbitration and the award deadline only if their determination is necessary; pleadings are closed and may be reopened before the award (Regs Art. 14).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
48:  Law Art. 38  |  Subject to Sharia and public order in the Kingdom, the tribunal applies the rules the parties chose to the merits (a chosen national law means its substantive rules, not its conflict rules, unless agreed otherwise); absent choice, the substantive rules of the law it considers most connected; it must observe the contract terms and take account of trade usages, customs and the parties' course of dealing; it may decide ex aequo et bono only if the parties expressly authorised amiable composition (تفويض بالصلح).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
49:  Law Arts. 39–40  |  Awards of a multi-member tribunal are by majority after secret deliberation; if no majority can form, the tribunal chooses an umpire (محكم مرجح) within 15 days or the court appoints one; procedural decisions may be taken by the president if the parties so agree in writing or all members authorise it; an amiable-composition award must be unanimous; interim and partial awards are permitted unless agreed otherwise. The final award must be issued within the agreed period or, absent agreement, within 12 months from commencement; the tribunal may extend by up to six months unless the parties agree a longer extension; if the award is not issued in time either party may ask the competent court to fix a further period or to terminate the arbitration, after which either party may sue in court; replacement of an arbitrator extends the period by 30 days.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
50:  Law Art. 41; Regs Art. 15  |  Proceedings end with the final award or with a tribunal decision terminating them: on the parties' agreement; on the claimant's withdrawal unless the respondent shows a serious interest in continuing; where the tribunal finds continuation pointless or impossible; or under Art. 34(1). Death or incapacity of a party does not end the proceedings but extends the period by 30 days. The arbitration agreement survives a termination decision unless the parties agree otherwise.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
51:  Law Arts. 42–44  |  The award is in writing and reasoned, signed by the arbitrators (a majority suffices if the minority's reasons for not signing are recorded); it must state the date and place of issue, the parties' and arbitrators' names, addresses, nationalities and capacities, a summary of the arbitration agreement, of the parties' statements, claims, pleadings and documents, and of any expert report, the operative part, and the arbitrators' fees and costs and their allocation. A true copy is delivered to each party within 15 days of issue; no publication without both parties' written consent. The tribunal deposits the original or a signed copy, in its language, with the competent court within the same 15 days, with a certified Arabic translation if in a foreign language.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
52:  Law Arts. 45–48; Regs Art. 16  |  A settlement reached during the arbitration may be recorded as a consent award with the force of an award for enforcement. Within 30 days of receipt a party may request interpretation of ambiguity in the operative part (issued in writing within 30 days, forming part of the award); the tribunal corrects clerical or arithmetical errors within 15 days of the award or the request (a correction exceeding its power is challengeable by annulment); within 30 days of receipt a party may request an additional award on claims omitted, issued within 60 days extendable by 30, the award-period rule not applying to it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
53:  Law Arts. 49–50 (para. 1(b) as amended by Royal Decree M/21 of 26/1/1447H)  |  Awards under the Law are not subject to any appeal other than an annulment action. Annulment lies only where: (a) there is no arbitration agreement, or it is void, voidable or lapsed by expiry of its period; (b) a party to the agreement lacked capacity or had limited capacity when it was made, under the law governing its capacity (in-force wording: عديم الأهلية); (c) a party could not present its defence for want of proper notice of an arbitrator's appointment or of the proceedings, or for another reason beyond its control; (d) the award set aside the legal rules the parties agreed to apply to the merits; (e) the tribunal was constituted or the arbitrators appointed contrary to the Law or the parties' agreement; (f) the award decided matters outside the agreement (severable parts survive); (g) the award failed to meet the required conditions in a way affecting its content, or rests on void procedures that affected it. The court annuls of its own motion an award contrary to Sharia or public order in the Kingdom, or to the parties' agreement, or on a non-arbitrable subject. The arbitration agreement survives annulment unless agreed otherwise or the agreement itself is annulled; the court may not review the facts or merits.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text) 
54:  Law Art. 51; Regs Arts. 17(1), 18  |  The annulment action is brought by either party within 60 days following notification of the award; a waiver of the right to bring it made before the award does not bar the action (Regs: a waiver after the award does). If the court upholds the award it must order its enforcement, by a judgment not subject to any appeal; if it annuls the award, its judgment may be appealed within 30 days from the day after notification (Regs: to the Supreme Court). The applicant files the original or certified copy of the award, a true copy of the arbitration agreement and a certified Arabic translation if needed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
55:  Law Arts. 52–55; Regs Art. 17(2)  |  An award under the Law has res judicata effect and is enforceable. The competent court (or its delegate) issues the enforcement order on an application accompanied by the original or certified copy of the award, a true copy of the arbitration agreement, a certified Arabic translation if in another language, and proof of deposit under Art. 44. The application is not admissible until the annulment period has expired; the order is issued only after verifying that the award does not conflict with a judgment or decision of a Saudi court, committee or body with jurisdiction over the subject, contains nothing contrary to Sharia or public order (severable parts may be enforced), and was properly notified to the losing party. An annulment action does not stay enforcement, but the court may order a stay if requested in the annulment petition on serious grounds, deciding the request within 15 days, possibly against security, and must then decide the annulment within 180 days of the stay. The enforcement order is not subject to grievance; refusal may be challenged within 30 days before the Supreme Court.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
56:  Law Arts. 56–58; Regs Arts. 3, 19  |  The Council of Ministers issues the Regulations; the Law replaces the Arbitration Law of 1403H (Royal Decree M/46 of 12/7/1403H) and takes effect 30 days after publication. Regulations: notifications may be made electronically; periods run from the day after notification unless agreed otherwise, with an official-holiday last day extended to the next working day; the Regulations took effect on publication.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
57:  SCCA Rules Arts. 2–3  |  The Rules apply where the parties agreed to SCCA arbitration or to arbitration "by the SCCA" without naming rules, and thereby authorise the SCCA to administer; they apply to arbitrations commenced on or after 1 May 2023 with the appendices as integral parts; they yield to non-derogable provisions of the applicable arbitration law; unless agreed otherwise, the Expedited Procedure Rules (Appendix II) apply automatically within their scope and, alternatively, the ODR Rules (Appendix IV) within theirs, prevailing over the main Rules in case of conflict. The SCCA Court is an independent body whose decisions under the Rules (appointment and removal of arbitrators, challenges, consolidation, costs, award scrutiny) are final and binding; the parties waive any appeal or review of them.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
58:  SCCA Rules Arts. 5–6, 15–16  |  The arbitration commences when the Administrator determines that the Request for Arbitration (statement referring to SCCA arbitration, party details, contract and arbitration agreement, claim description, relief and amount, proposals on number of arbitrators, language and seat) is complete and the registration fee paid; the Answer is due within 30 days of commencement, with any jurisdictional plea and counterclaims, and its absence does not stop the arbitration. One arbitrator is appointed unless the parties agreed otherwise or the SCCA Court, after consulting them, decides three are appropriate for size or complexity; the parties may agree any nomination procedure, failing which the SCCA Court appoints on request after 30 days; joint nomination of a sole arbitrator within 30 days of commencement, otherwise SCCA Court appointment; for three-member tribunals each side nominates within 30 days (multiple claimants or respondents jointly; a joined party jointly within 30 days of joinder).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
59:  SCCA Rules Art. 7; Appendix III  |  Before the tribunal is constituted a party may apply for emergency measures by e-mail to the Administrator and the other parties, with the SCCA administrative fee and emergency-arbitrator fee (Appendix III Art. 9: advance deposit SAR 75,000, being SAR 15,000 administrative fee and SAR 60,000 emergency-arbitrator fees and expenses, increasable by the SCCA Court); the SCCA Court appoints a single emergency arbitrator within one business day (challenge within one business day of notification); a schedule is set within two business days; the emergency arbitrator may order or award any interim, provisional or precautionary measure, with reasons (summary form allowed), possibly against security, and must issue the interim award or order within 14 days of transmission of the file (extendable by the Administrator); the emergency arbitration is terminated if no Request for Arbitration is filed within 10 days of the application; the measure binds once issued, ceases to bind if claims are withdrawn or the arbitration is not registered, and the constituted tribunal may reconsider, modify or vacate it; the emergency arbitrator cannot sit on the tribunal unless the parties agree; court applications for interim measures remain compatible with the agreement; the procedure does not apply after constitution, where the parties opted out, or where they agreed another pre-arbitral emergency mechanism.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
60:  SCCA Rules Arts. 17–18  |  Arbitrators must be impartial and independent, sign a statement of acceptance, availability, independence and impartiality, disclose circumstances giving rise to justifiable doubts at acceptance and throughout, and follow the SCCA Code of Ethics; a party's failure to disclose known circumstances within a reasonable period waives the challenge; each party must promptly disclose any non-party with an economic interest in the outcome, including third-party funders; unilateral communications with arbitrators are prohibited save as the Code allows. Challenge grounds: justifiable doubts as to impartiality or independence, failure to perform duties, or manifest lack of the agreed qualifications; notice of challenge within 14 days of notification of the appointment or of learning the circumstances; the challenge does not suspend the arbitration unless the tribunal decides; the SCCA Court decides admissibility and merits if the parties do not agree and the arbitrator does not withdraw (reasons on request, possibly for a fee); the Administrator may itself refer a non-performing arbitrator for removal.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
61:  SCCA Rules Arts. 22–23; Appendix III Art. 5; Appendix IV Arts. 7–8  |  Place of arbitration: if the parties do not agree by the date the Administrator sets, the SCCA Court initially determines the seat, subject to the tribunal's final determination within 30 days of its constitution, having regard to the circumstances and convenience; hearings, meetings and deliberations may be held anywhere (and remotely); the award is deemed issued at the seat wherever signed, including electronically. Language: if not agreed, the Administrator initially determines it, the tribunal finally within 30 days, guided by the language of the arbitration agreement and contract, the circumstances and convenience; translations may be ordered. The emergency arbitrator's seat follows Art. 22. **Only in the ODR track** (Appendix IV) are Riyadh and Arabic the defaults absent agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
62:  SCCA Rules Arts. 24, 28  |  The tribunal rules on its own jurisdiction (existence, scope, validity of the arbitration agreement) and on admissibility and arbitrability; invalidity of the contract does not by itself invalidate the clause; with multiple parties the arbitration proceeds among those bound; objections must be raised no later than the Answer (extendable); they may be decided as a preliminary matter or in the award; objections before constitution do not stop the Administrator from constituting the tribunal. Interim measures: on application the tribunal may order measures to maintain or restore the status quo, prevent imminent harm or prejudice to the process, preserve assets for satisfaction of an award, or preserve evidence; the applicant must show likely harm not adequately reparable by damages that substantially outweighs the harm to the respondent, and a reasonable possibility of success (relaxed for evidence preservation); the tribunal may modify, suspend or terminate measures, require security, require disclosure of changed circumstances, and award costs and damages for measures later found unjustified; court applications are compatible with the agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
63:  SCCA Rules Arts. 33, 36–37, 44  |  The tribunal declares the proceedings closed after the last hearing or authorised submission (reopening in exceptional circumstances); unless agreed otherwise it issues the final award within 75 days of closing, extendable by the Administrator. Awards are reasoned, in writing, signed (electronic signature permitted unless the parties or applicable law require otherwise), dated, and state the seat; they are final and binding; the SCCA may publish awards, orders and decisions unless a party objects before the arbitration concludes (anonymised as needed), and the parties must otherwise keep them confidential; before signing, the draft award goes to the Administrator for SCCA Court review as to form and no award issues until approved; filing or registration required by applicable law is the tribunal's responsibility on the parties' notice. Applicable law: the rules of law designated by the parties, else the law the tribunal finds appropriate; equity only on express authorisation; contract terms and trade usages always; **the law of the arbitration agreement is the law of the seat unless the parties agreed otherwise in writing.** Confidentiality binds arbitrators, the SCCA, its Court, Board, committees and staff, and the tribunal may make confidentiality orders and protect trade secrets.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
64:  SCCA Appendix II (Expedited Procedure Rules)  |  Apply automatically, as part of agreeing to the Rules, where the aggregate amount in dispute does not exceed SAR 4,000,000 (or equivalent) exclusive of costs, or where the parties opt in; the Administrator may decide they no longer apply (the tribunal then stays unless the SCCA Court reconstitutes it); full evidence goes in with the Request and Answer, the Answer period may be shortened, objections to applicability are decided finally by the Administrator, and a later increase above SAR 4m does not by itself remove the case from the track; a sole arbitrator is nominated within the Administrator's time limit or appointed; the award is made within 30 days of closing and in any event within 180 days of constitution unless the Administrator extends in exceptional circumstances; fees follow the expedited fee schedule.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
65:  SCCA Appendix IV (Online Dispute Resolution Procedure Rules)  |  Apply where the parties agree in writing and the aggregate sum in dispute does not exceed SAR 200,000 exclusive of costs (later increases do not remove the case unless decided otherwise); prevail over the Rules in conflict; the Administrator may move the case to the ordinary Rules; the seat is Riyadh and the language Arabic unless agreed otherwise; the final award is issued within 30 days of the tribunal's constitution (or re-affirmation after a challenge), reasoned and allocating costs, after SCCA Court form review, and served through the ODR platform.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SCCA]` 
66:  Cross-reference (Enforcement Law)  |  Enforcement of a domestic award after the Art. 53 enforcement order, and of a foreign award, is governed by the Enforcement Law (in force 2026-10-28; the 1433H law until then): domestic awards are enforcement instruments under Art. 7(1)(b) "in accordance with the relevant statutory texts" (i.e. after the Arbitration Law's order); foreign awards under Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9(2) are enforced per the Kingdom's treaty obligations (New York Convention `[model knowledge — verify]`) after verifying the Art. 9(1) conditions, including public order and the absence of a prior Saudi case; enforcement disputes and stays follow Enforcement Law Arts. 45–47.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the cross-referenced text in `enforcement-law.md`; routing statement `[model knowledge — verify]` as to practice 
76: - **Regs Art. 7 deletion.** With the deposit requirement gone, whether any registration of arbitrator engagements or awards with the SCCA is required for ad hoc arbitrations should be confirmed; only the Art. 44 court deposit remains in the text.
77: - **Emergency-arbitrator orders.** No provision of the Law addresses court enforcement of an emergency arbitrator's order; whether the competent court will support it under Art. 22–23 or treat it as an interim award for Art. 39(5) purposes is untested in the text. `[model knowledge — verify]`
78: - **SCCA Rules currency.** The 2023 edition and fee schedule were read; confirm no later amendment (the SCCA reserves the right to revise fees).
79: 
80: ## How skills should use this file
81: 
82: - **Skills that load this file.** `commercial-legal:vendor-agreement-review`, `commercial-legal:saas-msa-review` and `commercial-legal:nda-review` load it at the dispute-resolution clause step and must check, in order: form of agreement (Law Art. 9), capacity and government-entity approval (Art. 10), arbitrability (Art. 2), seat and language (Arts. 28–29; SCCA Arts. 22–23), tribunal qualifications (Art. 14), interim-measure powers (Arts. 22–23; SCCA Arts. 7, 28), award time limit (Art. 40; SCCA Art. 33), annulment window (Art. 51), and the expedited-track threshold (SCCA Appendix II) when the clause names the SCCA. `commercial-legal:escalation-flagger` loads Arts. 10(2), 14, 40 and 51 when a live arbitration or a government counterparty is involved. `commercial-legal:stakeholder-summary` cites only the Practical-effect column and carries the row tag. `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads Arts. 9–10, 49–51 and 55 when reviewing pending arbitrations and awards on a target. `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` loads Arts. 51–55 when a closing condition depends on an award becoming enforceable (annulment period expiry + enforcement order).
83: - **Cited rows.** Quote the Article cell and reproduce the Tag cell verbatim; SCCA rows carry `[authority — SCCA]` and must be described as institutional rules that apply only by agreement, never as statute.
84: - **Computed dates.** Award due = commencement (receipt of the request for arbitration, Art. 26; or the later SCCA commencement date) + 12 months, + up to 6 months by tribunal extension (Art. 40). Annulment deadline = notification of the award + 60 days, counted from the day after notification with holiday roll-forward (Art. 51(1); Regs Art. 3(2)). Earliest enforcement-order application = day after the annulment period expires (Art. 55(1)). Appeal against an annulment judgment = day after notification + 30 days (Art. 51(2)). SCCA: Answer = commencement + 30 days (Art. 6); final award = closing + 75 days (Art. 33); expedited award = constitution + 180 days (Appendix II Art. 10); emergency order = file transmission + 14 days (Art. 7(8)). Each computed date carries `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the formula; any statement about how long enforcement then takes is `[model knowledge — verify]`.
85: - **Enforcement routing.** Questions about executing an award, or enforcing a foreign award, go to `enforcement-law.md` (Arts. 7, 9, 45–47) with the effective-date gate stated there; this file answers only up to the Art. 53 enforcement order.
86: - **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule not in this table, or the row it needs is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, or the answer turns on the reported draft Arbitration Law, on court practice regarding Sharia/public-order review, or on an SCCA fee figure, it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory or upgrade a tag.

FILE commercial-courts-law.md
1: # Law of Commercial Courts — نظام المحاكم التجارية
2: 
3: <!-- Copied from references/jurisdictions/_TEMPLATE-instrument.md. Covers the Law and, as a secondary source, the Ministry of Justice implementing-regulations text. -->
4: 
5: ## Instrument
6: 
7: | Field | Value |
8: |---|---|
9: | Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
10: | Instrument | Law of Commercial Courts / نظام المحاكم التجارية, with its Implementing Regulations / اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام المحاكم التجارية |
11: | Issuing instrument | Law: Royal Decree No. M/93 of 15/8/1441H (2020-04-08), approving Council of Ministers Decision No. 511 of 14/8/1441H. Regulations: issued by the Minister of Justice in coordination with the Supreme Judicial Council under Art. 95 of the Law; the file read today is the Ministry of Justice PDF labelled **مشروع اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام المحاكم التجارية** (draft, dated 1441/9H – 2020/5) and is treated as the published project text. The approved regulation (reported as Minister of Justice Decision No. 8344 of 21/10/1441H `[model knowledge — verify]`) must be confirmed article by article before any regulation row below is relied on. |
12: | Issuing authority | Ministry of Justice (وزارة العدل); court organisation by the Supreme Judicial Council (المجلس الأعلى للقضاء) |
13: | Effective date | 2020-06-16 (Art. 96: 60 days after publication in the Official Gazette; portal shows publication 24/8/1441H = 2020-04-17). Regulations: "published in the Official Gazette and effective after publication" (Art. 95); date of the approved regulation not confirmed from the file. |
14: | Amendment history | Royal Decree No. M/191 of 29/11/1444H amended Art. 16(3): "disputes between partners in a mudaraba" (منازعات الشركاء في شركة المضاربة) became "disputes arising from the partnership contracts provided for in the Civil Transactions Law" (المنازعات التي تنشأ عن عقود المشاركة المنصوص عليها في نظام المعاملات المدنية). The portal marks only Art. 16 as amended; the in-force wording (portal amendment block) was read today and the Art. 16 row reflects it. Art. 16(2) and its SAR 100,000 threshold were not amended. Transitional rule (Decree ¶4): the Law applies to commercial cases not yet decided and procedures not completed at entry into force, except jurisdiction changes for cases already filed, time limits already running, and objection routes for final judgments already issued. |
15: | Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/38334008-3b70-4c6c-b3af-aba3016a8061/1 ; Regulations (MoJ, file labelled draft): https://www.moj.gov.sa/Documents/ProjectExecution.pdf |
16: | Official translation | The portal offers a "Translated document" attachment; it was not read today (no `boe-en` file for this instrument). Regulations: none read. |
17: | Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 (Law, Arabic, 96 articles plus Decree and Decision text; Regulations draft, Arabic, Arts. 1–351 read for Arts. 1–340) |
18: | Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page read; status line "ساري" (in force); issue date 1441/08/15H (2020-04-08); publication date 1441/08/24H (2020-04-17); issuing instruments listed: Royal Decree M/93 of 15/8/1441H and Council of Ministers Decision 511 of 14/8/1441H; `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 1 -->` (Art. 16; the portal's amendment block names Royal Decree M/191 of 29/11/1444H as amending paragraph (3) only). No other amending instrument appears in the text. The regulations file carries the heading "مشروع" and the date 1441/9H; whether the approved regulation differs from this text was not checked and cannot be confirmed from the files given. |
19: 
20: ## Scope
21: 
22: The Law governs the commercial courts (المحاكم التجارية) and the commercial circuits established in general courts where no commercial court exists (Decree ¶2), and applies, together with its Regulations, to the court and the cases within its jurisdiction, without prejudice to the commercial laws and the international conventions the Kingdom is party to (Art. 2). It covers subject-matter and territorial jurisdiction, pre-filing notice and conciliation, electronic filing and service, written pleadings and hearing management, urgent (interim) requests, evidence in commercial cases, judgments and immediate enforcement, payment orders, appeal, reconsideration and cassation, and (in the Regulations) small claims and class actions. It does not itself govern the compulsory enforcement of judgments or the recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments and arbitral awards: those are matters for the Enforcement Law (`enforcement-law.md`) and the Arbitration Law (`arbitration-law.md`); this file only cross-references them. Where the Law is silent, the Law of Civil Procedure (نظام المرافعات الشرعية) applies insofar as compatible with the commercial nature of the case (Art. 93). Labor, administrative and criminal matters are outside the commercial courts.
23: 
24: ## Rules
28:  Article  |  Rule (English)  |  Tag 
29: --- | --- | ---
30:  Art. 2; Regs Art. 2  |  The Law and Regulations apply to the commercial court and the cases within its jurisdiction, without prejudice to the commercial laws and international conventions to which the Kingdom is party. The Regulations set the order of application: (a) the international convention, (b) the special procedural text in a commercial law, (c) the Law and Regulations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
31:  Art. 6; Regs Arts. 10–13  |  By exception to the Law and the Law of Civil Procedure, and without prejudice to public order and the rules of justice, parties to a commercial dealing who are all merchants may agree on specific pleading procedures. The Regulations require any agreement the Law or Regulations refer to be in writing; the agreement may cover the number of memoranda, notice procedures, document deadlines, a named expert or expert procedure (including agreeing to accept an expert's opinion), and shortening (never lengthening) procedural periods other than the court's own; it must be invoked at the first hearing or the right lapses; parties may also agree a case plan for the court's approval.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
32:  Art. 7; Regs Arts. 38–47  |  Any procedure under the Law may be electronic, including filing and registering claims and requests, hearing them, remote pleading, exchange of memoranda, judgment and objection. The Regulations give an electronic act the same effects as a physical one, permit AI tools, bar a party from denying an opponent's submission merely because it was electronic, route e-litigation through the Ministry's approved systems, allow electronic verification in place of signature, and permit remote hearings in all cases subject to the Ministry's approved means and publicity (a non-merchant party may ask for an in-court hearing within one day of being notified).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
33:  Art. 8(1); Regs Arts. 100–107, 327  |  The Regulations determine conciliation and mediation procedures, including the cases in which conciliation/mediation must precede the hearing, not to exceed 30 days from commencement unless the parties agree a longer period. The draft Regulations require prior conciliation/mediation before registering: (a) single-judge cases (Regs Art. 17), (b) Art. 16(3) claims (drafted as mudaraba partner disputes; since Royal Decree M/191 of 1444H the paragraph covers partnership-contract disputes under the Civil Transactions Law), (c) cases between spouses or relatives to the fourth degree, (d) cases on contracts containing a written agreement to attempt conciliation, mediation or amicable settlement before litigation, (e) other categories the court president designates. Compliance is shown by a certificate that conciliation ended without (or with partial) settlement, or by proof of applying and the period having elapsed. Small claims: prior conciliation not exceeding 15 days. A settlement reached after registration is recorded and takes the force of an enforcement instrument.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 8) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
34:  Arts. 9–12; Regs Arts. 71–83  |  Valid service addresses include: the verified or chosen electronic address; residence; the address registered with the postal authority; the commercial-register address for that activity; the address stated in the case papers; **the address stated in the contract, for disputes arising from that contract**; and an address chosen by a foreigner in the Kingdom. A party may designate a lawyer's address. Change of the case-papers address must be notified within 48 hours or service there remains effective. Electronic service is by SMS to the verified mobile, verified/chosen e-mail, or notification through a government e-account; other addresses are served through a licensed postal provider. A person resident abroad may be served under these rules where the parties have so agreed, without prejudice to international conventions; the Regulations require that address to be recorded in a document between the parties, provide diplomatic-channel service where that fails, and treat the contract address as valid for five years from the contract unless agreed otherwise. Legal persons: service on the legal representative, a general partner, the manager, the legal department, or a branch manager for branch disputes; foreign companies with a branch or commercial representative: on that branch manager or representative.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
35:  Art. 13; Regs Arts. 89–93  |  The court may fine a party up to SAR 10,000 for breach of case procedure or hearing order (final). Case data, papers and documents of commercial cases are open to the public for a fee equal to the cost of the service; on application by an interested person the court may declare certain papers or documents confidential vis-à-vis persons other than the court, without prejudice to the publicity of hearings. The Regulations treat a document as confidential where a law or competent-authority decision so provides, where the parties' agreement makes it confidential, or where access would infringe a trade secret; confidentiality cannot be claimed if the parties previously agreed there is none; the court's decision on confidentiality is not subject to any objection.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
36:  Art. 15  |  Absent a special text in the commercial laws or in treaties and conventions to which the Kingdom is party, the international-jurisdiction rules of the Law of Civil Procedure apply to cases within the commercial court's jurisdiction.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
37:  Art. 16 (marked amended on the portal); Regs Arts. 48–50  |  The commercial court has jurisdiction over: (1) disputes between merchants arising from their original or ancillary commercial activities; (2) claims against a merchant in commercial-contract disputes where the principal claim exceeds SAR 100,000 in the text read today (the Supreme Judicial Council may raise this figure); (3) as amended by Royal Decree M/191 of 29/11/1444H, disputes arising from the partnership contracts (عقود المشاركة) provided for in the Civil Transactions Law (original text: disputes between partners in a mudaraba); (4) claims and violations under the Companies Law; (5) under the Bankruptcy Law; (6) under the intellectual-property laws; (7) under the other commercial laws; (8) claims and requests concerning judicial custodians, trustees, liquidators and appointed experts where linked to a case within its jurisdiction; (9) compensation claims for harm arising from a case previously heard by the court. The draft Regulations: item (2) requires a contractual relationship (Regs Art. 48) and, in Regs Art. 49, states the threshold as SAR 500,000; item (3) requires a written contract (Regs Art. 50, drafted against the original mudaraba wording).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (in-force text including the M/191 amendment to paragraph (3)); threshold conflict flagged; `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
38:  Art. 17; Regs Arts. 51–55  |  Unless the parties agree otherwise in writing, territorial jurisdiction lies with the court of the defendant's place of residence; if none in the Kingdom, the claimant's; the claim may also be brought where the contract was concluded, performed or was to be performed. Company cases: the court of the company's head office (claims against the company, by the company against a partner, between partners, or against managers/board members); branch-contract disputes may be brought where the branch is. The plea of lack of territorial jurisdiction is lost once the party makes any request or defence, including at the case-preparation stage.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
39:  Art. 18; Regs Arts. 54, 56–57  |  The court may rule separately on a plea of lack of subject-matter jurisdiction within 20 days of the plea. The Regulations add an administrative jurisdiction report within two working days of registration and a circuit decision within three working days, referral of a no-jurisdiction judgment to appeal the day after filing, and extend Art. 18 to pleas of lack of judicial (ولائي) and territorial jurisdiction.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
40:  Art. 19; Regs Arts. 116–121  |  In the cases the Regulations specify, the claimant must notify the defendant in writing to perform the claimed right at least 15 days before filing. Before registration, and under court supervision, the parties may undertake conciliation/mediation, exchange of documents and memoranda, or a meeting to define claims, defences and evidence, documented as the Regulations provide. Draft Regulations: the notice is required in all cases within the court's jurisdiction except penalty cases under commercial laws, cases with an administrative body as party, small claims and urgent requests; it must state the parties, subject, claims and supporting document, with proof attached to the statement of claim (failing which the cure procedure of Art. 21 applies); proof of a prior attempt at amicable settlement, conciliation or mediation counts as the notice once the 15-day period has elapsed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
41:  Arts. 20–23; Regs Arts. 94–99, 131–136  |  Claims are brought by a statement of claim filed with the court; the Regulations determine which claims must be filed by a lawyer. The statement must give the parties' details and addresses, enumerate the claims and all grounds; unrelated claims may not be joined. Registration is by the competent administration; deficiencies must be cured within 15 days (registration then dates back to the filing), with a 15-day grievance to the court president (final). The defendant must lodge a defence memorandum with the answer, all pleas, claims and grounds at least one day before the hearing (urgent requests excepted). Draft Regulations: all claims must be filed by a lawyer except Art. 16(2), (8), (9) claims and small claims; pleading by a lawyer is mandatory above SAR 10m (items 1, 2, 8, 9) or SAR 2m (items 3, 4, 6, 7) and in financial-restructuring bankruptcy applications; licensed in-house legal departments of private legal persons and representatives of administrative bodies may act; the first hearing is set within 20 days of registration and at least four days after service (24 hours in urgent cases).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
42:  Art. 24; Regs Arts. 58–67  |  Absent a special text, claims within the court's jurisdiction are not heard after five years from the date the claimed right arose, unless the defendant acknowledges the right or the claimant shows an excuse the court accepts. Draft Regulations: rights arising before the Law run from its entry into force; the period cannot be varied by agreement; waiver of the plea is valid only after the period has run; the period starts when the debt falls due; "excuse" includes a material or moral impediment for its duration; a judicial claim (even before an incompetent court) or an Art. 19(2) pre-filing step interrupts the period; the plea belongs to the debtor and is not raised by the court of its own motion; it may be raised at any stage including on appeal; abandoned or annulled claims count towards the period; non-hearing of the principal claim extends to dependent claims such as against a guarantor.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
43:  Arts. 25–28; Regs Arts. 68–70, 145–151  |  Proceedings and pleading are in writing, though the circuit may hear the parties orally and must record an oral summary on request; the court may fine or refuse counter/incidental claims from a party who fails to file on time without excuse; adjournment only for a recorded reason and never twice for the same reason; a circuit may delegate preparation of the case to one of its judges. Draft Regulations: statutory periods may be exceeded only for necessity by reasoned decision; maximum duration 180 days at first instance (small claims excepted), 20 days for appeal without hearing, 90 days for appeal with hearing; a pre-trial meeting checks jurisdiction, offers settlement, fixes the issues, evidence, witness list and timetable; at most two pleading hearings after service of the defendant; adjournment after the first hearing not more than 30 days.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
44:  Art. 29(2); Regs Arts. 104–107  |  If the parties settle before the circuit, the settlement is recorded in the hearing minutes, signed by the parties and approved by the presiding judge; the approved minutes are an enforcement instrument (سند تنفيذي), a copy is delivered as for judgments and the case ends. The Regulations extend this to settlements at any stage, even after closing of pleadings or before the appeal court, and to a settlement recorded before the competent administration, and require a copy where the settlement is embodied in an enforcement instrument under the Enforcement Law.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
45:  Arts. 30–32; Regs Arts. 154–160  |  A defendant served personally (or through a representative), or who attends any hearing or files a defence, is treated as present even if absent later; if served other than personally and absent, the case is adjourned once and re-served, after which judgment is treated as adversarial; service on a legal person under the Law is always personal service. A claimant absent without accepted excuse: judgment on the defendant's request if the case is ripe, otherwise striking out, with 30 days to apply to resume, failing which the case is deemed never brought. Draft Regulations: a party is absent if not present within 30 minutes of the scheduled time; a defendant served who fails to appear loses the right to raise temporary (procedural) pleas.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
46:  Arts. 33–37; Regs Arts. 161–177  |  Urgent requests (الطلبات المستعجلة) are heard by a single-judge circuit established by the Council, referred immediately and decided within three working days of referral, if necessary in the applicant's presence without the other party. They include: inspection to establish a state of affairs, travel ban, stopping new works, judicial custody, precautionary attachment, obtaining a product sample, preserving specified documents, prohibiting or permitting a disposal, and requests given urgent character by the commercial laws. The order lapses if the main claim is not filed within seven days, if the claim is abandoned or deemed not brought, or if the claimant fails on the merits; any interested person may seek cancellation where urgency has ceased, the case is stayed, interrupted or struck out. The court may require security to compensate the respondent if the applicant proves not entitled, with compensation decided in the merits judgment. Draft Regulations: an urgent request is a matter where delay is feared, without touching the merits; it may be filed before or after the main action; ex parte determination where surprise is needed, with immediate service of the order; a travel-ban request must be accompanied by security of 5% of the principal claim and not less than SAR 50,000; the person banned may lodge security for the claimed amount and is then allowed to travel; urgent decisions are always appealable.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
47:  Art. 38  |  Absent a special text in the commercial laws, the evidence chapter applies to cases within the court's jurisdiction; no particular form is required to prove an obligation unless the parties agree otherwise; the court may rely on evidentiary procedures conducted in a foreign state unless contrary to public order; where the parties agree specific evidence rules the court applies them, subject to public order; absent a special text, the burden of proof may be shifted by agreement.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
48:  Arts. 42–47; Regs Arts. 232–238  |  Statements in official documents bind their maker unless disproved; a private document is deemed to emanate from the person it is attributed to unless that person expressly denies the handwriting, signature, seal or fingerprint (the denial must specify its basis; a party who discussed the document's content cannot later deny it); a copy is deemed to match the original unless disputed, and disputed copies may still serve as presumptions in listed cases; signed or attributable correspondence has the force of a private document. A party may request specific or categorised documents held by the other party that relate to the commercial dealing and are not confidential; refusal to produce what the court orders is a presumption against the refusing party, who may not rely on the withheld document; the court may join a third party or order a government body to produce relevant documents; a document-recovery request is available to an interested person.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
49:  Art. 55; Regs Arts. 260–264  |  Electronic evidence may be treated as proof; it includes electronic records/documents, electronic media, means of communication, e-mail, electronic registers and any other electronic evidence the Regulations specify. Draft Regulations: electronic evidence embodying an official or private document has the same legal effect; evidence from electronic media is valid only if created with the other party's consent or knowledge; it is probative where issued under the Electronic Transactions Law, through a means verified with a government or licensed body or licensed notary, where the contract specified the electronic means, where the opponent discussed its content before denying it, or where drawn from the opponent's public communication channel; the court may appoint an expert to verify it.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
50:  Art. 56; Regs Arts. 265–284  |  The Minister, in coordination with the Council, issues the rules on expert evidence before the court (engagement and selection, fees and expenses, duties and powers, supervision, court expert departments). Draft Regulations: a party may seek leave to use an expert (stating the field, question, cost estimate and proposed name); where parties agree, a single joint expert may be ordered, the court considering value, complexity and speed; a five-day fast opinion is available; the competent administration manages appointment and deposits, and non-deposit can forfeit the right to expert evidence; the expert must disclose relationships and interests, act impartially, keep confidentiality and flag questions outside competence; the report is due within 60 days of the start date, extendable once by 30 days, otherwise replacement with refund; parties may put written questions once within 15 days; the expert's opinion does not bind the court, which must give reasons for departing from it; a party who relies on an expert report allows the other party to rely on it too.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
51:  Art. 57; Regs Art. 285  |  Absent a special text or contrary agreement, commercial custom or the course of dealing between the parties may be relied on; the party invoking custom or usage must prove it. The court relies on settled commercial custom and may seek assistance to establish it when disputed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
52:  Arts. 58–66; Regs Arts. 178–209  |  Pleadings close when the parties have presented their case; reopening only by reasoned decision; supplementary memoranda may be allowed (draft Regulations: within 10 days, one per party, no new claims, evidence or attachments). Deliberation is secret; judgments issue at least by majority with dissent reasons recorded; the reasoned draft is deposited at pronouncement; pronouncement in public session, adjournable once; a date for delivery of the judgment copy is set within 20 days of pronouncement (draft Regulations: 10 days absent special text). The judgment must state the court, date, circuit and judges, the parties, a summary of facts, claims and material pleas, and the reasons and operative part (insufficient factual reasoning voids the judgment, Regs Art. 193); the enforceable copy bears the enforcement formula and is delivered only to the party with an interest in enforcement. Draft Regulations require the merits judgment to decide the claim for material and moral damages including litigation costs, weighing the harm, the amount awarded, the debtor's procrastination, custom and any expert opinion. The issuing court handles correction and interpretation.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
53:  Art. 65; Regs Arts. 202–205  |  Judgments and orders are immediately enforceable (التنفيذ المعجل) when issued in an urgent case or as payment orders; on the judgment creditor's request the court may order immediate enforcement where delay would cause serious harm, or where the judgment rests on an acknowledgment or on a private document the debtor did not deny. Draft Regulations: statutory immediate enforcement applies even if the judgment is silent; the court may require security; the appeal court may stay immediate enforcement where the grounds of appeal may lead to reversal and serious harm is feared.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
54:  Arts. 67–71; Regs Arts. 210–228  |  By exception to ordinary filing, a creditor may apply for a payment order (أمر أداء) where the right is established in writing, the debt is due, and it is a determinate sum of money or fungible/specific movable; the creditor must first demand payment in writing at least five days before applying, stating the intended application date; the circuit decides within 10 days of registration, refusal being final without prejudice to an ordinary action; the debtor may lodge a grievance (تظلم) before the competent court within 15 days of service, by ordinary claim procedure and stating reasons; the court may stay immediate enforcement on reasoned decision where serious harm is feared. Draft Regulations: the sum is "determinate" if fixed on established bases leaving no discretion to the court; not available where the debtor resides outside the Kingdom, nor for reciprocal obligations unless the creditor attaches written proof of its own performance; venue is the debtor's residence; the application does not bar precautionary measures; the demand is proved through a postal or licensed electronic service provider; the debtor may also appeal for formal defects; the grievance reopens the merits within its scope.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
55:  Art. 74; Regs Arts. 288–290  |  Only the losing party, or a party not awarded all its claims, may object; the parties may agree, even before the claim is filed, that the first-instance judgment is final; objections to interlocutory judgments go with the objection to the merits judgment, except that judgments on staying the case, jurisdiction, hearing of the claim, **the plea of an arbitration clause**, and temporary and urgent judgments may be objected to before the merits judgment; objection to an immediately enforceable judgment does not stay it; the objector is not prejudiced by its own objection. Draft Regulations: a finality agreement cannot bind one party only; it must be invoked before any request or defence on appeal; an objector may waive the objection.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
56:  Art. 77; Regs Art. 206  |  Final judgments that have acquired res judicata are conclusive as to what they decided, but only in a dispute between the same parties in the same capacities concerning the same right, subject and cause; the court applies this of its own motion. Reasons acquire res judicata only where inseparably linked to the operative part.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
57:  Arts. 78–79; Regs Arts. 294, 303–312  |  All first-instance judgments and decisions are appealable except small claims not exceeding SAR 50,000 as determined by the Council (draft Regulations: unless the judgment was on jurisdiction, inadmissibility, non-hearing for lapse of time, formal non-acceptance, or a preliminary plea barring the case); the Council determines which appeals are decided without a hearing (draft Regulations: judgments in Art. 16(1)–(3) claims not exceeding SAR 2m, Art. 16(8)–(9) claims, and matters within the single-judge appeal circuit). **The appeal period is 30 days from the date fixed for delivery of the judgment copy; 10 days for judgments on jurisdiction and in urgent cases**, the latter being referred the next day and decided within five working days; a final judgment on subject-matter or territorial jurisdiction is not open to any objection.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
58:  Arts. 80–85; Regs Arts. 296–302  |  A single-judge appeal circuit hears objections to single-judge urgent decisions, payment orders, judgments ending or abandoning the case, and other matters the Council designates. Appeal is by petition stating the judgment, grounds and requests (missing elements mean non-acceptance under the draft Regulations); it transfers the case as it stood, limited to what is appealed, and the court may admit new evidence, pleas and defences but **not new claims** (except increased compensation, profits or rent accruing after the final submissions); the respondent may cross-appeal until closing of pleadings; an absent appellant risks the appeal being deemed not brought after one adjournment of up to 30 days; on reversal the case is remitted for alternative claims or, where dismissed on a preliminary ground, for the merits.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
59:  Arts. 86–92; Regs Arts. 313–321  |  Reconsideration (التماس إعادة النظر) of final judgments is available in the cases set by the Law of Civil Procedure, by petition to the court that issued the final judgment (draft Regulations: the appeal circuit that affirmed it; no stay unless ordered for irreparable harm, possibly against security). Cassation before the Commercial Chamber of the Supreme Court lies against appeal-circuit judgments for: violation or misapplication of Sharia or the laws, or of a Supreme Court judicial principle; lack of jurisdiction or improper constitution; error in characterising the facts; or conflict with a prior judgment between the same parties (draft Regulations: only if that prior judgment is final). Cassation does not stay enforcement unless the court so orders on request for irreparable effects (security may be required); a reporting judge screens the petition; on cassation the case is remitted to a differently constituted panel bound by the Supreme Court's principle, or decided by the Supreme Court if ripe, and must be decided by it on a second cassation.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
60:  Art. 8(3); Regs Arts. 322–340  |  The Regulations define small claims (الدعاوى اليسيرة), their procedure and judgment. Draft Regulations: small claims are the single-judge cases under Regs Arts. 17–18 (Art. 16(1) claims up to SAR 500,000, Art. 16(2) claims up to SAR 1m, related Art. 16(8)–(9) claims) except construction-contracting claims; decided within 90 days of registration (30 days where pre-filing exchange took place), entirely electronically and remotely unless the circuit decides otherwise, after conciliation not exceeding 15 days; the defendant is served the day of registration with a pre-trial meeting within 15 days; the defence is due one day before that meeting; no claims, evidence or pleas after the pre-trial meeting; expert reports within 20 days (+10); a single hearing (adjournment ≤ 15 days); judgment pronounced within five days of closing and delivered at once or within five days; appeal decided within 15 days of referral unless heard. Draft Regs Art. 17 also sets the single-judge first-instance circuit: Art. 16(1) claims up to SAR 500,000, Art. 16(2) claims up to SAR 1m, and a "requests and orders" circuit for urgent requests and payment orders; Regs Art. 18 lists the single-judge appeal matters.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 8) / `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text) 
61:  Art. 8(2); Regs Arts. 341–351  |  The Regulations set the class-action (الدعاوى الجماعية) procedure. Draft Regulations: unity of subject and cause, same defendant, a common objective criterion, at least ten consenting claimants, and a lawyer as representative; leave from the court president within ten days (final), with up to 60 days to reach the headcount; one statement of claim within ten days of leave with a schedule of claims; a public register; the court may appoint an adviser, classify the claimants and allow joinder until closing of pleadings.  |  `[authority — MoJ]` (Regs, draft text; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for Art. 8(2) `[BOE — Arabic]`) 
62:  Art. 93; Art. 96; Decree ¶2–¶4  |  Where the Law is silent, the Law of Civil Procedure applies to cases within the court's jurisdiction insofar as compatible with the commercial nature of the case. The Law entered into force 60 days after publication and repeals conflicting provisions; commercial circuits in general courts serve regions without a commercial court, with appeal to the nearest commercial court; Art. 35 of the Law of Civil Procedure (the former commercial-jurisdiction rule) is repealed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
63:  Cross-reference (no article of this Law)  |  Recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments and of arbitral awards is not governed by this Law. Foreign judgments, orders, arbitral awards, settlement agreements and notarised documents are enforcement instruments only under the Enforcement Law's Art. 7(1)(g) and Art. 9 (reciprocity, exclusive Saudi jurisdiction, no prior Saudi case, due process, finality, no conflict with a Saudi judgment, public order; foreign awards and settlements per treaty obligations), before the enforcement court. Domestic arbitral awards are enforced through the enforcement order under Arbitration Law Arts. 52–55 and then the Enforcement Law. The commercial court's role is limited to the arbitration-clause plea (Art. 74(4); Arbitration Law Art. 11), to the interlocutory support the Arbitration Law assigns to the "competent court" (the Court of Appeal originally competent, i.e. the commercial appeal circuits for commercial disputes) and, under draft Regs Art. 23(b), to specialised circuits for applications arising under the Arbitration Law.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the cross-referenced texts in the two named files; the routing statement itself is a reading of the texts, `[model knowledge — verify]` as to court practice 
76: ## How skills should use this file
77: 
78: - **Skills that load this file.** `commercial-legal:vendor-agreement-review`, `commercial-legal:saas-msa-review` and `commercial-legal:nda-review` load it at the dispute-resolution / governing-law / notices clause step (Arts. 6, 9–12, 13, 17, 19, 24, 38, 74; Regs Art. 101 for multi-tier clauses). `commercial-legal:escalation-flagger` loads it when a matter involves an existing or threatened Saudi commercial claim (Arts. 16, 33–37, 65, 67–71, 78–79). `commercial-legal:stakeholder-summary` cites only the Practical-effect column and must carry the row's tag. `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads it when reviewing litigation schedules and receivables (Arts. 16, 24, 65, 77–79; Regs Arts. 341–351 for group-claim exposure). `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` loads Arts. 29(2), 64–65 and 78–79 when a condition precedent depends on a judgment becoming final or a settlement being recorded.
79: - **Cited rows.** A skill citing a rule must quote the Article cell and reproduce the Tag cell verbatim. Where a row draws on the Regulations, the citation must say "draft Regulations text (MoJ file labelled مشروع)" and carry the `[authority — MoJ]` tag; it must not present a regulation rule as settled law.
80: - **Computed dates.** Appeal deadline = date fixed for delivery of the judgment copy + 30 days (Art. 79(1)); + 10 days for jurisdiction and urgent judgments (Art. 79(2)). Payment-order grievance = service + 15 days (Art. 71). Urgent-order lapse = order date + 7 days without a main claim (Art. 35(1)(a)). Limitation = accrual + 5 years (Art. 24). Pre-filing notice = at least 15 days before filing (Art. 19(1)). Each computed date must carry `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` for the formula and a separate flag that day-counting follows draft Regs Art. 4 (day of service excluded; electronic deadline 23:59 court time), tagged `[authority — MoJ]` (draft).
81: - **Forum routing.** Before stating that a claim belongs in the commercial court, a skill must cite the Art. 16 row and flag the SAR 100,000 / 500,000 threshold conflict for any claim by a non-merchant against a merchant. Foreign-judgment and award enforcement questions are routed to `enforcement-law.md` and `arbitration-law.md`, never answered from this file.
82: - **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a rule not in this table, or the row it needs is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`, or depends on a draft-Regulations row the approved regulation may have changed, it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory or upgrade a tag.

