35:| Regulation Art. 17(2)-(5) | The controller must issue clear instructions to the processor; the processor must notify the controller in writing without undue delay of any breach of those instructions or of Saudi law. The controller must periodically assess the processor's compliance (it may appoint an independent third party) covering processing by the processor or third parties acting for it. A processor that breaches the controller's instructions or the agreement is treated as a controller and is directly accountable. Before engaging a sub-processor the processor must take sufficient guarantees that protection is not reduced, choose only sub-processors giving sufficient guarantees, and obtain the controller's prior acceptance, notifying the controller before contracting and allowing it to object within a period agreed between controller and processor. | — (SDAIA English text; Arabic original of the Regulation not in the source files) | Contract terms to check: instruction-only processing; written notice of deviation; audit or assessment right (or independent assessor); sub-processor prior acceptance with an agreed objection window (general pre-authorisation with a list and a notice period fits the text, silent auto-approval does not `[model knowledge — verify]`); liability allocation reflecting that a deviating processor becomes a controller. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
38:| Art. 18 (paragraph 1 as amended) | The controller must destroy personal data without delay once the purpose of collection ends; it may retain the data if everything identifying the data subject is removed per the Regulations. It must retain the data after the purpose ends where a legal requirement mandates retention for a set period (destroy at the later of that period or the purpose's end) or where the data are closely connected to a case before a judicial body (destroy after proceedings end). | على جهة التحكم إتلاف البيانات الشخصية بعد انتهاء الغرض من جمعها دون تأخير... على جهة التحكم الاحتفاظ بالبيانات الشخصية حتى بعد انتهاء الغرض من جمعها في الحالتين الآتيتين: إذا توافر مسوغ نظامي يوجب الاحتفاظ بها مدة مُحددة... إذا كانت البيانات الشخصية متصلة اتصالاً وثيقاً بقضية منظورة أمام جهة قضائية. | Termination and exit clauses: require deletion or anonymisation on termination, with a carve-out only for legal-retention and litigation-hold data; open-ended vendor retention "for backup" is inconsistent with "without delay". | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
40:| Art. 20 (as amended); Regulation Art. 24 | The controller must notify the Competent Authority on becoming aware of a leak, damage or unlawful access to personal data, per the Regulations; and must notify the data subject if the incident may cause harm to his data or conflict with his rights or interests, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 24: notify the Competent Authority within no more than 72 hours of becoming aware, if the incident potentially harms the data or the data subject or conflicts with his rights or interests; the notification must describe the incident (time, date, circumstances, time of awareness), data categories and numbers of affected data subjects, risks and mitigation measures, whether data subjects were notified, and contact details of the controller or DPO; missing information is supplied as soon as possible with reasons; the controller keeps a copy of reports and corrective measures; NCA reporting duties are unaffected; data subjects are notified without undue delay in clear language with a description, risks, contact details and recommendations. | تشعر جهة التحكم الجهة المختصة عند علمها بحدوث تسرب لبيانات شخصية أو تلفها أو وصول غير مشروع إليها؛ وفقاً لما تحدده اللوائح. تشعر جهة التحكم صاحب البيانات الشخصية إذا كان من شأن حدوث تسرب... أن يرتب ضرراً على بياناته أو يتعارض مع حقوقه أو مصالحه. | The 72-hour clock runs from the controller's awareness; the processor's statutory duty is only "without undue delay" (Regulation Art. 17(1)(d)). A vendor agreement should fix a processor-to-controller notice period short enough for the controller to file within 72 hours (24 to 48 hours is the usual ask `[model knowledge — verify]`) and require the Regulation Art. 24(1) content. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` |
43:| Art. 29 (as amended by M/148) | (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a controller may transfer personal data outside the Kingdom or disclose it to a party outside the Kingdom for any of these purposes: (a) performance of an obligation under an agreement to which the Kingdom is party; (b) serving the Kingdom's interests; (c) performance of an obligation to which the data subject is party; (d) other purposes set by the Regulations. (2) Conditions: (a) the transfer or disclosure must not prejudice national security or the Kingdom's vital interests; (b) an appropriate level of protection for personal data must exist outside the Kingdom, not lower than that of the Law and Regulations, according to an assessment by the Competent Authority in coordination with the relevant bodies; (c) the transfer or disclosure is limited to the minimum data needed. (3) Paragraph (2) does not apply in cases of extreme necessity to protect the data subject's life or vital interests, or to prevent, examine or treat infection. (4) The Regulations set the rules, standards and procedures, including the cases in which controllers are exempted from conditions (2)(b) and (2)(c), and the controls and procedures for exemption and its withdrawal. | يجوز لجهة التحكم نقل البيانات الشخصية إلى خارج المملكة أو الإفصاح عنها لجهة خارج المملكة، وذلك لتحقيق أيٍّ من الأغراض الآتية: ... ج- إذا كان ذلك تنفيذاً لالتزام يكون صاحب البيانات الشخصية طرفاً فيه. د- إذا كان ذلك تنفيذاً لأغراض أخرى وفق ما تحدده اللوائح. تكون الشروط الواجب توافرها... أ- ألا يترتب على النقل أو الإفصاح مساس بالأمن الوطني أو بمصالح المملكة الحيوية. ب- أن يتوافر مستوى مناسب لحماية البيانات الشخصية في خارج المملكة؛ بما لا يقل عن مستوى الحماية المقرر في النظام واللوائح، وفقاً لنتائج تقويم تجريه الجهة المختصة. ج- أن يقتصر النقل أو الإفصاح على الحد الأدنى من البيانات الشخصية الذي تدعو الحاجة إليه. | The statutory structure since M/148: a permitted purpose (the "other purposes" list is in the Transfer Regulation) plus three conditions, of which (b) is an adequacy test run by SDAIA and can be waived under the Transfer Regulation. The original 2021 requirement for case-by-case Competent Authority approval of each transfer is gone. For contract review: identify where the vendor and its sub-processors process data, and require the transfer mechanism to be stated in the agreement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
