68:  Art. 178  |  The parties may fix the amount of compensation in advance in the contract or a later agreement, unless the object of the obligation is a sum of money; notice is not a condition of entitlement to such compensation.  |  [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
69:  Art. 179  |  Agreed compensation is not due if the debtor proves the creditor suffered no harm. The court may, on the debtor's application, reduce agreed compensation if the debtor proves it was exaggerated or the principal obligation was partly performed. The court may, on the creditor's application, increase agreed compensation to the amount of the harm if the creditor proves the harm exceeded the agreed amount as a result of the debtor's fraud or gross fault. Any agreement contrary to this article is void.  |  [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
70:  Art. 180  |  If compensation is not fixed in the contract or by statute the court assesses it under Arts. 136-139. However, where the obligation arises from contract, a debtor who committed no fraud or gross fault is liable only for harm that could ordinarily be foreseen at the time of contracting.  |  [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
personal-data-protection-law.md
34:  Art. 8 (as amended); Regulation Art. 17(1)  |  When choosing a processor, the controller must choose one that provides the guarantees necessary to implement the Law and Regulations, and must verify the processor's compliance with the Law and Regulations, without prejudice to the controller's own responsibility to the data subject or the Competent Authority; the Regulations set the rules, including for any subsequent contracts made by the processor. Regulation Art. 17(1): the controller must ensure the processor gives sufficient guarantees and that the agreement with the processor includes: (a) purpose of processing; (b) categories of personal data; (c) duration of processing; (d) the processor's commitment to notify the controller without undue delay of any personal data breach; (e) whether the processor is subject to regulations of other countries and the effect on its compliance with the Law; (f) that the data subject's prior consent is not required for disclosures mandatory under Saudi law, provided the processor notifies the controller of such disclosure; (g) identification of any subcontractors or other party to whom personal data will be disclosed.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
35:  Regulation Art. 17(2)-(5)  |  The controller must issue clear instructions to the processor; the processor must notify the controller in writing without undue delay of any breach of those instructions or of Saudi law. The controller must periodically assess the processor's compliance (it may appoint an independent third party) covering processing by the processor or third parties acting for it. A processor that breaches the controller's instructions or the agreement is treated as a controller and is directly accountable. Before engaging a sub-processor the processor must take sufficient guarantees that protection is not reduced, choose only sub-processors giving sufficient guarantees, and obtain the controller's prior acceptance, notifying the controller before contracting and allowing it to object within a period agreed between controller and processor.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
36:  Art. 11 (paragraph 4 as amended), 14; Regulation Art. 19  |  The purpose of collection must relate directly to the controller's purposes and not conflict with any legal provision; collection methods must be appropriate, direct, clear, secure and free of deception; content must be adequate and limited to the minimum necessary for the purpose; once data are no longer necessary, collection must stop and what was collected must be destroyed without delay. The controller may not process data without adequate steps to verify accuracy, completeness, currency and relevance.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
37:  Art. 12 (as amended), 13 (as amended)  |  The controller must adopt a privacy policy and make it available to data subjects when collecting their data, covering purpose, content of data collected, method of collection, means of storage, manner of processing and destruction, the data subject's rights and how to exercise them. When collecting directly from the data subject the controller must inform him, at collection, of: the legal basis for collection; the purpose, whether collection is mandatory or optional, and that the data will not be processed inconsistently with the purpose except under Art. 10; the identity and address of the collector; the entities to which the data will be disclosed and whether the data will be transferred, disclosed or processed outside the Kingdom; the consequences of not completing collection; the Art. 4 rights; other elements set by the Regulations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
38:  Art. 18 (paragraph 1 as amended)  |  The controller must destroy personal data without delay once the purpose of collection ends; it may retain the data if everything identifying the data subject is removed per the Regulations. It must retain the data after the purpose ends where a legal requirement mandates retention for a set period (destroy at the later of that period or the purpose's end) or where the data are closely connected to a case before a judicial body (destroy after proceedings end).  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
39:  Art. 19; Regulation Art. 23  |  The controller must take the organisational, administrative and technical measures necessary to protect personal data, including during transfer, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 23: implement security measures against breach; adopt the National Cybersecurity Authority's controls, standards and rules, or recognised best practice and cybersecurity standards if not bound by the NCA's.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
40:  Art. 20 (as amended); Regulation Art. 24  |  The controller must notify the Competent Authority on becoming aware of a leak, damage or unlawful access to personal data, per the Regulations; and must notify the data subject if the incident may cause harm to his data or conflict with his rights or interests, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 24: notify the Competent Authority within no more than 72 hours of becoming aware, if the incident potentially harms the data or the data subject or conflicts with his rights or interests; the notification must describe the incident (time, date, circumstances, time of awareness), data categories and numbers of affected data subjects, risks and mitigation measures, whether data subjects were notified, and contact details of the controller or DPO; missing information is supplied as soon as possible with reasons; the controller keeps a copy of reports and corrective measures; NCA reporting duties are unaffected; data subjects are notified without undue delay in clear language with a description, risks, contact details and recommendations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
41:  Art. 22; Regulation Art. 25  |  The controller must assess the effects of processing personal data for any product or service offered to the public, according to its activity, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 25: a written impact assessment is required for processing sensitive data; combining or linking two or more datasets from different sources; large-scale processing of persons lacking capacity, constant monitoring, new technologies, or automated decision-making; and products or services likely to cause serious harm to privacy; minimum content listed; a copy must be given to any processor acting for the controller; re-assessment if harm is indicated.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
42:  Art. 24 (paragraph 1 as amended), 25, 26  |  Credit data: the Regulations must require verification of the data subject's explicit consent (M/148 replaced "written") for collection, change of purpose, disclosure or publication, and notice to him when a disclosure request is received. Direct marketing: personal contact means may be used to send advertising or awareness material only with the recipient's consent and with a clear opt-out mechanism. Marketing processing is allowed for non-sensitive data collected directly from the data subject with his consent under the Law, per the Regulations.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
43:  Art. 29 (as amended by M/148)  |  (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a controller may transfer personal data outside the Kingdom or disclose it to a party outside the Kingdom for any of these purposes: (a) performance of an obligation under an agreement to which the Kingdom is party; (b) serving the Kingdom's interests; (c) performance of an obligation to which the data subject is party; (d) other purposes set by the Regulations. (2) Conditions: (a) the transfer or disclosure must not prejudice national security or the Kingdom's vital interests; (b) an appropriate level of protection for personal data must exist outside the Kingdom, not lower than that of the Law and Regulations, according to an assessment by the Competent Authority in coordination with the relevant bodies; (c) the transfer or disclosure is limited to the minimum data needed. (3) Paragraph (2) does not apply in cases of extreme necessity to protect the data subject's life or vital interests, or to prevent, examine or treat infection. (4) The Regulations set the rules, standards and procedures, including the cases in which controllers are exempted from conditions (2)(b) and (2)(c), and the controls and procedures for exemption and its withdrawal.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
44:  Transfer Regulation (not read) — mechanisms under Art. 29(4)  |  Under the Regulation on Personal Data Transfer Outside the Kingdom (as amended September 2024): "other purposes" include central processing within a group, provision of services or products to the data subject, scientific research, and other listed purposes; where the destination lacks a SDAIA adequacy decision, a controller may transfer under appropriate safeguards, namely SDAIA's Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Common Rules for intra-group transfers, or a certification of compliance, with a documented transfer risk assessment in prescribed cases; narrow exceptions exist for specific situations (e.g., performance of a contract with the data subject, vital interests).  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 
45:  Localisation (no PDPL article)  |  The PDPL contains no general data-localisation or in-Kingdom hosting requirement; localisation obligations, where they exist, come from sector regulators and the NCA (e.g., the NCA's cloud controls and government-data rules, SAMA's outsourcing rules, CST's cloud framework and their data-classification levels).  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 
electronic-transactions-law.md
43:  Art. 23, 24 (as amended by RD M/57 of 1436H), 25-27  |  Offences include operating as a certification service provider without licence; misuse or disclosure of certificate-applicant information; creating, publishing or using a certificate or signature for a fraudulent or unlawful purpose; forging an electronic record, signature or certificate or knowingly using one; knowingly giving false information to a provider or relying party; accessing, copying or seizing another's signature system without authority; impersonation in certificate applications; publishing forged, revoked or suspended certificates. Penalty: fine up to SAR 5 million and/or imprisonment up to 5 years, with possible confiscation of equipment; the court may order publication of the judgment summary at the offender's expense after the judgment becomes final. The Commission investigates and seizes; cases go to the Public Prosecution; an injured person keeps a civil claim for damages.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
44:  Art. 28, 29  |  The Law does not prejudice other laws, in particular intellectual property rights and international agreements to which the Kingdom is party. Ministry, Commission and Center staff must keep confidential the information of providers and their customers.  |  `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
45:  Practice note (no article)  |  Position of DocuSign, Adobe Sign and similar foreign platforms versus Saudi-certified signatures.  |  `[model knowledge — verify]` 
