47: ## Open questions for a local practitioner
48: 
49: - The Implementing Regulation (Art. 30) was not read. It sets the technical conditions under which a signature is "made in accordance with the Law" (Art. 14(2)), the retention and original-record conditions (Art. 6(3), 8), the dispatch and receipt rules (Art. 13), and the licensing and foreign-accreditation procedures (Art. 15, 17). Whether any additional instruments (powers of attorney, negotiable instruments, wills) are excluded by the Regulation must be checked there; the statute excludes only personal-status transactions and real-property deeds.
50: - Which foreign certificate issuers, if any, the NCDC has accredited under Art. 17, and whether a widely used foreign platform's certificates are among them.
51: - The current allocation of the NCDC's functions (Ministry, CST, or Digital Government Authority) and the current name of the Commission.
52: - How the Commercial Courts treat platform-signed contracts in practice: whether an audit-trail certificate is routinely accepted as meeting Art. 8, and whether courts require the platform provider to authenticate it.
53: - Interaction with the Evidence Law (1443H) chapter on digital evidence: which of the two instruments governs admissibility and weight when they differ.
54: - Whether the Ministry of Justice has issued permissions under Art. 3 for electronic real-property deeds (the Najiz practice suggests so) and the scope of those permissions.
