**4. Detailed findings and bilingual redlines**

The numbered edits below constitute the redline package. Quoted replacement text is proposed contractual language; `[review]` notes outside it identify decisions needed before use.

**F1 — Clause 7: Vendor’s own use of fleet data**

**Playbook:** “Any clause letting the vendor use our fleet data for its own purposes.”

**Contract:**
> “Vendor may use Customer's vehicle telemetry data to improve Vendor's products and services”

**Gap:** Unacceptable. **Legal risk 🔴; business friction 🔴.**

This is a commercial deal-breaker independently of whether the telemetry qualifies as personal data. If identifiable driver data are involved, determining an independent processing purpose also raises controller-role and lawful-processing issues. [PDPL reference, definitions and processor selection](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/personal-data-protection-law.md:30>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`; Art. 8 `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**Redline R1 — replace the quoted permission:**

> “Vendor may use Customer’s vehicle telemetry data solely to provide the Services to Customer in accordance with Customer’s documented instructions. Vendor shall not use that data, or any aggregation, anonymisation or derivative of it, for Vendor’s or any third party’s own purposes, including product improvement, benchmarking, commercialisation or model training.”

**العربية:**
> «يجوز للمورّد استخدام بيانات القياس عن بُعد لمركبات العميل حصراً لتقديم الخدمات إلى العميل وفق تعليماته الموثقة. ولا يجوز للمورّد استخدام تلك البيانات، أو أي بيانات مجمعة أو مخفاة الهوية أو مشتقة منها، لأغراض المورّد الخاصة أو أغراض أي طرف ثالث، بما في ذلك تطوير المنتجات أو المقارنات المعيارية أو الاستغلال التجاري أو تدريب النماذج.»

**If Vendor refuses:** No documented fallback. Legal Lead to decide whether to discontinue negotiations; the current playbook does not authorise acceptance.

**F2 — Clause 3: Liability exclusion and cap**

**Playbook:** “Vendor cap at 12 months fees paid or payable”; indirect/consequential damages “excluded both ways”; required above-cap carveouts include gross negligence, fraud, confidentiality, and data breach.

**Contract:**
> “Vendor shall not be liable to Customer for any loss whatsoever arising out of or in connection with this Agreement, including loss caused by Vendor's gross negligence, and in no event shall Vendor's aggregate liability exceed SAR 100,000.”

**Gap:** Unacceptable. **Legal risk 🔴; business friction 🔴.**

The required dimensions are:

| Dimension | Assessment |
|---|---|
| Direct damages | “Any loss whatsoever” first attempts to exclude all recovery. The subsequent cap does not affirmatively restore direct-damages recovery. |
| Indirect/consequential damages | Vendor receives a blanket exclusion; Customer receives no reciprocal exclusion. Customer’s recoverable indirect exposure is not contractually capped or excluded. |
| Exact cap base | **“SAR 100,000.”** This is a fixed amount, not a fees-based cap. |
| Above/below cap | No express carveouts. On its wording, ordinary breach, confidentiality, data breach, and delay compensation all face the blanket exclusion and cap. No indemnity exists. |
| Mandatory limits | Art. 173 prohibits exclusion of fraud/gross fault and harmful-act liability. Its exception includes “غش أو خطأ جسيم”. The clause’s contrary reach cannot stand. |
| Playbook conflict | The profile says both “paid or payable” and “fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim.” The latter can produce a very low early-term cap. The proposal below uses paid/payable with first-year protection. `[review]` |

The capped remedy is commercially illusory because the opening exclusion removes the very recovery the cap appears to preserve. Article 180 also distinguishes ordinarily foreseeable contractual loss from loss involving fraud or gross fault. [Civil Transactions reference, Arts. 173 and 180](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:66>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**Redline R2 — replace clause 3 because the exclusion and cap require coordinated restructuring:**

> “Neither party shall be liable to the other for indirect or consequential loss. Subject to the following exceptions, each party’s aggregate liability arising out of or in connection with this Agreement shall not exceed the fees paid or payable for the twelve months preceding the event giving rise to the claim; for an event occurring during the first twelve months, the cap shall equal the fees paid or payable for those first twelve months.
>
> The aggregate cap shall not apply to Vendor’s fraud, gross negligence or gross fault, breach of confidentiality, or breach of its data-protection obligations. Neither the cap nor any exclusion shall apply to liability arising from fraud or gross fault, harmful acts to the extent exclusion is prohibited, or any other liability that cannot lawfully be limited or excluded. Customer’s obligation to pay fees properly due is unaffected. Compensation under clause 2 counts towards the applicable aggregate cap, without displacing Article 179 of the Civil Transactions Law.”

**العربية:**
> «لا يكون أي من الطرفين مسؤولاً تجاه الآخر عن الخسائر غير المباشرة أو التبعية. ومع مراعاة الاستثناءات التالية، لا يتجاوز إجمالي مسؤولية كل طرف الناشئة عن هذه الاتفاقية أو المتعلقة بها الرسوم المدفوعة أو المستحقة الدفع عن الاثني عشر شهراً السابقة للواقعة المنشئة للمطالبة؛ وإذا وقعت الواقعة خلال الاثني عشر شهراً الأولى، كان الحد مساوياً للرسوم المدفوعة أو المستحقة الدفع عن تلك الأشهر الاثني عشر الأولى.
>
> لا يسري الحد الإجمالي على غش المورّد أو إهماله الجسيم أو خطئه الجسيم أو إخلاله بالسرية أو بالتزاماته المتعلقة بحماية البيانات. ولا يسري الحد أو أي استبعاد على المسؤولية الناشئة عن الغش أو الخطأ الجسيم، أو عن الفعل الضار بالقدر الذي يمنع النظام الإعفاء منه، أو أي مسؤولية لا يجوز نظاماً تحديدها أو استبعادها. ولا يتأثر التزام العميل بسداد الرسوم المستحقة على وجه صحيح. ويُحتسب التعويض بموجب البند 2 ضمن الحد الإجمالي المنطبق، دون إخلال بالمادة 179 من نظام المعاملات المدنية.»

The mutual customer cap and first-year basis are proposed protections, not separately documented house positions. The ordinary indirect-loss exclusion remains for confidentiality/data claims except where mandatory law prevents it; those claims are above the **monetary cap**. Legal Lead and counsel must settle that distinction. `[review]`

**If Vendor refuses:** Escalate to Legal Lead; no fallback permits exclusion of Vendor’s fraud or gross negligence.

**F3 — Clause 8: Lock-in, renewal and termination**

**Playbook:** “Termination for convenience on 30 days’ notice”; “auto-renewal only with a 30-day cancel window”; never accept “Multi-year lock-in with no termination rights.”

**Contract:**
> “Three (3) years from the Effective Date, renewing automatically for successive one-year terms unless either party gives ninety (90) days' written notice before the end of the then-current term.”

**Gap:** Unacceptable. **Legal risk 🔴; business friction 🔴.**

Statutory breach remedies exist, but they do not provide the requested convenience exit. Arts. 107–108 distinguish default termination remedies from an agreed right to terminate without a judgment; formal demand must be addressed expressly. Continuing-contract termination operates prospectively under Art. 111. [Civil Transactions reference](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:54>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`; Art. 108 `[BOE — Arabic]`.

Interpret the house “30-day cancel window” as permitting non-renewal notice until 30 days before expiry. `[review]`

**Redline R3 — replace “ninety (90)” with “thirty (30)” and append:**

> “Customer may terminate this Agreement for convenience at any time on thirty (30) days’ written notice, without an early-termination charge or liability for future fees. Customer may terminate for Vendor’s material breach if not cured within thirty (30) days after written notice specifying the breach, or immediately on written notice if the breach is incapable of remedy. These rights require no court judgment; the stated notices suffice, and any additional notice, demand or i’thar is expressly waived.
>
> Vendor shall refund prepaid fees attributable to the period after termination and provide reasonable transition assistance. The Effective Date is the date of the last authorised signature. Contractual years and months are Gregorian and days are calendar days unless expressly stated otherwise.”

**العربية:**
> يُستبدل «تسعين (90)» بـ«ثلاثين (30)»، ويُضاف:
>
> «يجوز للعميل إنهاء هذه الاتفاقية اختيارياً في أي وقت بإشعار كتابي قبل ثلاثين (30) يوماً، دون رسم إنهاء مبكر أو التزام بالرسوم المستقبلية. ويجوز للعميل فسخ الاتفاقية بسبب إخلال المورّد الجوهري إذا لم يعالجه خلال ثلاثين (30) يوماً من إشعار كتابي يحدد الإخلال، أو فوراً بإشعار كتابي إذا تعذرت معالجته. ولا تتطلب هذه الحقوق حكماً قضائياً؛ وتكفي الإشعارات المحددة، ويُعفى صراحةً من أي إشعار أو مطالبة أو إعذار إضافي.
>
> يرد المورّد الرسوم المدفوعة مقدماً عن الفترة التالية للإنهاء، ويقدم مساعدة معقولة للانتقال. ويكون تاريخ النفاذ تاريخ آخر توقيع صادر من مفوض بالتوقيع. وتُحسب السنوات والأشهر التعاقدية بالتقويم الميلادي، والأيام أياماً تقويمية، ما لم يُنص صراحةً على خلاف ذلك.»

The proposed Effective Date must reflect the actual commercial commencement arrangement. `[review]` No cancellation deadline is calculated from the undefined date in the existing draft.

**If Vendor refuses:** No documented fallback for the lock-in. Legal Lead escalation.

**F4 — Clause 2: Agreed compensation for delay**

**Accepted playbook default:** “Accept a fixed compensation clause only with the statutory reduction in view.”

**Contract:**
> “Vendor shall pay Customer a penalty of 10% of the total contract value per week of delay, payable irrespective of any loss suffered by Customer. The parties agree that this penalty is a genuine pre-estimate and is not subject to reduction by any court or tribunal.”

**Gap:** Unacceptable statutory-override wording; commercially excessive-looking formula. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🟠.**

**Enforceability `[ksa]`:** Art. 178 allows advance compensation for non-monetary obligations. Under Art. 179, no compensation is due if Vendor proves no harm; the court may reduce an exaggerated amount or account for partial performance. An increase requires Customer to prove greater harm resulting from fraud or gross fault. The article concludes:

> “يقع باطلًا كلُّ اتفاقٍ يُخالف أحكام هذه المادة.”

Accordingly, the “irrespective of loss” and “not subject to reduction” language is void to that extent. [Civil Transactions reference, Arts. 178–179](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:68>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**Exposure as written:** Assuming “total contract value” means the initial three-year fees and one delayed milestone, the formula is **SAR 540,000 per week**, or **SAR 1,080,000 after two weeks**, without an express clause 2 ceiling. `[computed — civil-transactions-law.md Arts. 178–179, settled 2026-09-04; inputs: agreement cls. 1, 2, 8; SAR 1,800,000 × 3 × 10% × 1 or 2 weeks]` `[review]`

Clause 3 nevertheless purports to eliminate or cap that recovery at SAR 100,000. The two clauses do not produce a reliable collectible amount.

**After the statutory rule:** The accrued agreed sum is ordinarily the maximum under Art. 179 absent the fraud/gross-fault condition for an increase; it remains reducible and may become zero on proof of no harm. No actual harm, delay duration, or performed share is supplied, so no judicially adjusted amount can be computed.

**Redline R4 — retain the milestone trigger, replace the remainder:**

> “Vendor shall pay Customer agreed compensation of 10% of the total fees for the initial three-year term per week of delay, subject to Article 179 of the Civil Transactions Law and the applicable aggregate cap in clause 3. No agreed compensation is due where Vendor proves that Customer suffered no harm; the court may reduce it for exaggeration or partial performance, and may increase it only on the grounds permitted by Article 179. This clause does not exclude Customer’s rights to require performance or terminate, and no loss shall be recovered twice.”

**العربية:**
> «يلتزم المورّد بأن يدفع للعميل تعويضاً اتفاقياً بنسبة 10% من إجمالي رسوم المدة الأولية البالغة ثلاث سنوات عن كل أسبوع تأخير، مع مراعاة المادة 179 من نظام المعاملات المدنية والحد الإجمالي المنطبق بموجب البند 3. ولا يستحق التعويض الاتفاقي إذا أثبت المورّد أن العميل لم يلحقه ضرر؛ وللمحكمة تخفيضه للمبالغة أو التنفيذ الجزئي، ولا تجوز زيادته إلا للأسباب التي تجيزها المادة 179. ولا يستبعد هذا البند حق العميل في طلب التنفيذ أو الفسخ، ولا يُعوض عن الضرر ذاته مرتين.»

Retaining 10% is a minimal legal correction, **not approval that this rate is supportable**. Procurement and Legal Lead should negotiate a rate and milestone-value base supported by anticipated delay harm once Schedule 2 is available. No percentage fallback appears in the profile. `[review]`

**F5 — Clause 5: Six-month claims waiver**

**Accepted playbook default:** Statutory limitation “cannot be shortened or lengthened by contract.”

**Contract:**
> “Any claim by Customer under this Agreement must be brought within six (6) months of the event giving rise to it, failing which it is waived.”

**Gap:** Unacceptable claims-bar structure. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🟠.**

Art. 305 states:

> “لا يجوز الاتفاق على تقصير مدة عدم سماع الدعوى، ولا على إطالتها.”

This is a general claims bar, not a narrowly drafted warranty notification procedure. The latter’s treatment is an unresolved practice point and does not save this wording. [Civil Transactions reference, Arts. 305–306](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:86>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; warranty-condition distinction `[model knowledge — verify]`.

Do **not** replace six months with a blanket ten years. Commercial Courts Law Art. 24 supplies a five-year non-hearing rule for claims within its jurisdiction, subject to special provisions and stated exceptions. The Civil Transactions Law’s general and special periods must be reconciled for the actual claim. [Commercial Courts reference, Art. 24](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/commercial-courts-law.md:42>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[review]`

**Redline R5 — replace clause 5:**

> “Claims are subject to the periods and exceptions prescribed by applicable law. Nothing in this Agreement shortens or extends a statutory period for hearing a claim or waives a right contrary to mandatory law.”

**العربية:**
> «تخضع المطالبات للمدد والاستثناءات المقررة نظاماً. ولا يترتب على هذه الاتفاقية تقصير أو إطالة أي مدة نظامية لسماع الدعوى، أو إسقاط أي حق على نحو يخالف الأحكام النظامية الآمرة.»

**If Vendor refuses:** Legal Lead and Saudi counsel; no approver can validate a prohibited alteration of the statutory period.

**F6 — Clause 6: Late-payment interest**

**Accepted playbook default:** “No contractual interest; pre-agreed compensation on money debts barred.”

**Contract:**
> “Overdue amounts bear interest at 8% per annum from the due date until payment.”

**Gap:** Outside the accepted position. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🟠.**

This fixes compensation on a monetary obligation by reference to time, contrary to Art. 178. Any claim for delay loss must instead satisfy the applicable requirements under Art. 171. Art. 385 separately addresses increases on loans; this services invoice is not shown to be a loan. No broader Sharia proposition is needed to identify the defect. [Civil Transactions reference](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:65>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`; Art. 178 `[BOE — Arabic]`. Interest treatment beyond those provisions remains `[model knowledge — verify]` `[review]`.

For illustration only, one equal quarterly invoice outstanding for a full year generates **SAR 36,000** under the drafted formula. `[computed — civil-transactions-law.md Arts. 171, 178, settled 2026-09-04; inputs: SAR 1,800,000 ÷ 4 × 8% × 1 year; simple interest assumed]` This is not an enforceable-exposure estimate.

**Redline R6 — replace clause 6:**

> “No interest or predetermined time-based charge accrues on overdue amounts. Any entitlement to compensation for delayed payment shall be determined under applicable law on proof of recoverable loss.”

**العربية:**
> «لا تستحق على المبالغ المتأخرة أي فائدة أو رسوم محددة مسبقاً مرتبطة بمدة التأخير. ويُحدد أي استحقاق للتعويض عن تأخر السداد وفق النظام، بناءً على إثبات الضرر القابل للتعويض.»

**If Vendor refuses:** Automatic jurisdiction-specific escalation to Legal Lead and Saudi counsel.

**F7 — Clause 4: Assignment and novation**

**Accepted playbook default:** “Assignment of a whole contract needs counterparty consent; advance consent valid on notice.”

**Contract:**
> “Vendor may assign or novate this Agreement in whole or in part to any third party without the consent of Customer. Customer may not assign this Agreement.”

**Gap:** Non-standard, one-sided transfer rights. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🟠.**

Do not call this automatically void: the clause can operate as the advance consent Art. 255 permits. It gives away Customer’s ability to assess the replacement vendor. Arts. 238–240 address rights, Arts. 248–254 debts, and Arts. 255–258 the contractual position. Express retained liability is needed rather than assuming consent never releases the transferor. [Civil Transactions reference, transfer rows](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:75>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`; Arts. 255–256 `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**Redline R7 — replace clause 4:**

> “Vendor may not assign rights, transfer obligations, novate this Agreement or transfer its contractual position, in whole or in part, without Customer’s prior written consent. Any consent does not release Vendor unless Customer expressly agrees in writing; otherwise Vendor remains jointly and severally liable with the transferee. Customer may assign or transfer this Agreement with Vendor’s prior written consent, not to be unreasonably withheld or delayed.”

**العربية:**
> «لا يجوز للمورّد حوالة حقوقه أو نقل التزاماته أو إحلال طرف آخر محله أو التنازل عن صفته كطرف في هذه الاتفاقية، كلياً أو جزئياً، دون موافقة العميل الكتابية المسبقة. ولا تبرئ الموافقة ذمة المورّد إلا إذا وافق العميل صراحةً على ذلك كتابةً؛ وإلا ظل المورّد مسؤولاً بالتضامن مع المتنازل له. ويجوز للعميل التنازل عن هذه الاتفاقية أو نقلها بموافقة المورّد الكتابية المسبقة، على ألا تُحجب أو تؤخر دون سبب معقول.»

The customer consent standard is a proposed negotiating position; no specific fallback is recorded. `[review]` A share sale does not itself transfer the contract under the reference’s Art. 98 treatment; any change-of-control protection would require separate drafting.

**F8 — Clause 7 and omissions: Processor, security, confidentiality and transfers**

**Playbook:** “Vendor signs our data-processing terms as processor”; “ISO 27001 or equivalent for any vendor touching customer data.”

**Contract:**
> “may transfer such data to Vendor's affiliates outside the Kingdom.”

**Gap:** Missing required safeguards and unrestricted transfer permission. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🔴.**

The seven processor-agreement items are inadequately addressed: purpose, data categories, duration, breach notice, foreign-law exposure, mandatory Saudi disclosure mechanics, and identified subprocessors/recipients. The draft also lacks instruction controls, assessment rights, subprocessor acceptance, return/deletion, and records assistance.

The regulator notification period is **no more than 72 hours from controller awareness where the regulatory harm/rights threshold is met**. It is not a 72-hour allowance for Vendor to notify Customer. The proposed 24-hour vendor deadline below is contractual. [PDPL reference, Arts. 8, 18, 20, 29 and 31](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/personal-data-protection-law.md:34>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; [SDAIA regulation, Arts. 17 and 24](https://dgp.sdaia.gov.sa/wps/portal/pdp/knowledgecenter/details/PDPL2/) `[authority — SDAIA]`.

Article 29 requires a permitted transfer purpose and the applicable protection, national-interest and minimisation conditions. Affiliate status alone does not establish compliance. Specific transfer mechanisms remain `[model knowledge — verify]` under the reference; none is approved here. `[review]`

Article 36 provides an administrative fine ceiling of SAR 5,000,000, with increased exposure for repetition; Article 40 preserves compensation claims. A private cap does not bind the regulator or data subjects. `[BOE — Arabic]`

**Redline R8a — replace the offshore permission:**

> “Vendor shall not transfer Customer Data outside the Kingdom, permit access from outside the Kingdom, or disclose it to an affiliate without Customer’s prior written approval identifying the recipient, destination, purpose and data categories, and documented satisfaction of applicable legal transfer requirements. Approval alone does not establish legal compliance.”

**العربية:**
> «لا يجوز للمورّد نقل بيانات العميل إلى خارج المملكة أو إتاحة الوصول إليها من خارج المملكة أو الإفصاح عنها لجهة تابعة، دون موافقة العميل الكتابية المسبقة التي تحدد المستلم والوجهة والغرض وفئات البيانات، ودون توثيق استيفاء المتطلبات النظامية المنطبقة على النقل. ولا تثبت الموافقة وحدها مشروعية النقل.»

**Redline R8b — append processor and confidentiality protections:**

> “Before processing Customer Data, the parties shall execute Customer’s data-processing agreement and a completed processing schedule identifying the purpose, data categories, processing duration, processing locations, recipients and subprocessors, and applicable foreign laws affecting Vendor’s compliance. Vendor shall act as processor solely on Customer’s documented instructions.
>
> Vendor shall notify Customer in writing without delay if an instruction conflicts with Saudi law. Vendor shall notify Customer of a personal data breach without undue delay and in any event within twenty-four hours of awareness, provide available incident details and continuing updates, and assist Customer with investigation, mitigation, regulatory reporting and data-subject communications.
>
> Vendor shall maintain ISO 27001 certification or equivalent security controls evidenced to Customer, bind authorised personnel to confidentiality, and permit Customer or its independent assessor to assess compliance. Vendor shall obtain prior written approval before engaging a subprocessor, provide at least thirty days’ advance notice and opportunity to object, impose equivalent obligations, and remain responsible for its performance.
>
> Disclosure mandatory under Saudi law does not require Customer’s prior approval, but Vendor shall notify Customer of that disclosure. Vendor shall provide information and assistance for Customer’s processing records and data-subject requests.
>
> On Customer’s instruction or termination, Vendor shall return Customer Data in a usable electronic format and destroy remaining copies without delay, except data required to be retained by law or for relevant judicial proceedings. Retained data shall remain protected, be used only for the retention purpose, and be destroyed when that requirement ends.
>
> Each party shall protect the other’s non-public information, use it only to perform this Agreement, and disclose it only to persons needing it for that purpose and bound to confidentiality, or as legally required. These obligations survive termination.”

**العربية:**
> «قبل معالجة بيانات العميل، يبرم الطرفان اتفاق معالجة البيانات الخاص بالعميل وملحقاً مكتملاً للمعالجة يحدد الغرض وفئات البيانات ومدة المعالجة ومواقعها والمستلمين وجهات المعالجة الفرعية، والأنظمة الأجنبية المنطبقة التي تؤثر على التزام المورّد. ويعمل المورّد بصفته جهة معالجة حصراً وفق تعليمات العميل الموثقة.
>
> يُشعر المورّد العميل كتابةً ودون تأخير إذا خالفت إحدى التعليمات الأنظمة السعودية. ويُشعر المورّد العميل بأي تسرب للبيانات الشخصية دون تأخر غير مبرر، وفي جميع الأحوال خلال أربع وعشرين ساعة من علمه، ويقدم تفاصيل الحادثة المتاحة وتحديثاتها المستمرة، ويساعد العميل في التحقيق والمعالجة والإبلاغ النظامي والتواصل مع أصحاب البيانات الشخصية.
>
> يحافظ المورّد على شهادة ISO 27001 أو ضوابط أمنية مكافئة يقدم ما يثبتها للعميل، ويلزم العاملين المصرح لهم بالسرية، ويمكّن العميل أو مقيّمه المستقل من تقييم الالتزام. ويحصل المورّد على موافقة كتابية مسبقة قبل التعاقد مع جهة معالجة فرعية، مع إشعار مسبق لا يقل عن ثلاثين يوماً وإتاحة الاعتراض، ويلزمها بالتزامات مكافئة ويظل مسؤولاً عن أدائها.
>
> لا يتطلب الإفصاح الوجوبي بموجب الأنظمة السعودية موافقة العميل المسبقة، على أن يُشعر المورّد العميل بذلك الإفصاح. ويقدم المورّد المعلومات والمساعدة اللازمة لسجلات أنشطة المعالجة لدى العميل وطلبات أصحاب البيانات الشخصية.
>
> بناءً على تعليمات العميل أو عند انتهاء الاتفاقية، يعيد المورّد بيانات العميل بصيغة إلكترونية قابلة للاستخدام، ويتلف النسخ المتبقية دون تأخير، باستثناء البيانات التي يلزم الاحتفاظ بها نظاماً أو لإجراءات قضائية ذات صلة. وتظل البيانات المحتفظ بها محمية، ولا تستخدم إلا لغرض الاحتفاظ، وتُتلف عند انتهاء موجبه.
>
> يحمي كل طرف المعلومات غير المتاحة للعموم الخاصة بالطرف الآخر، ولا يستخدمها إلا لتنفيذ هذه الاتفاقية، ولا يفصح عنها إلا لمن يحتاجها لهذا الغرض ويلتزم بالسرية، أو عند وجوب الإفصاح نظاماً. وتستمر هذه الالتزامات بعد انتهاء الاتفاقية.»

The processing schedule still requires real particulars. The proposed notice periods, security equivalence, and confidentiality scope need Legal Lead approval. `[review]`

**If Vendor refuses:** No processing authorisation; escalate to Legal Lead and the responsible security/privacy reviewer. Customer’s statutory accountability remains despite outsourcing.

**F9 — Missing indemnities**

**Playbook:** “Vendor indemnifies for IP infringement and data breach; we indemnify for our data.”

**Contract:** No indemnity provision appears.

**Gap:** Missing term. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🟠.**

General damages rights do not supply a negotiated defence obligation. PDPL Art. 40 provides a potential third-party compensation route; the agreement should allocate the resulting defence and loss between the parties. [PDPL reference, Art. 40](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/personal-data-protection-law.md:51>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**Redline R9 — insert:**

> “Vendor shall defend and indemnify Customer against third-party claims and resulting damages, settlements approved by Customer, and reasonable defence costs arising from infringement of intellectual property rights by the Services or deliverables, or Vendor’s breach of its data-protection obligations.
>
> Customer shall defend and indemnify Vendor against third-party claims arising from infringement of intellectual property rights by data supplied by Customer for authorised use, except to the extent caused by Vendor’s unauthorised use, modification or breach.
>
> The indemnified party shall give prompt notice and reasonable cooperation. The indemnifying party may control the defence with competent counsel, but shall not admit fault, impose a non-monetary obligation or settle without a full release of the indemnified party without its prior written consent. Delay in notice reduces protection only to the extent of demonstrated prejudice. Clause 3 governs the applicable caps and exclusions.”

**العربية:**
> «يتولى المورّد الدفاع عن العميل وتعويضه عن مطالبات الغير وما يترتب عليها من تعويضات وتسويات يوافق عليها العميل وتكاليف دفاع معقولة، الناشئة عن انتهاك حقوق الملكية الفكرية بواسطة الخدمات أو المخرجات، أو إخلال المورّد بالتزاماته المتعلقة بحماية البيانات.
>
> ويتولى العميل الدفاع عن المورّد وتعويضه عن مطالبات الغير الناشئة عن انتهاك حقوق الملكية الفكرية بواسطة البيانات التي يقدمها العميل للاستخدام المصرح به، باستثناء ما ينشأ بالقدر ذاته عن استخدام المورّد غير المصرح به أو تعديله للبيانات أو إخلاله.
>
> يُقدم الطرف المستفيد إشعاراً عاجلاً وتعاوناً معقولاً. ويجوز للطرف الملتزم بالتعويض إدارة الدفاع بواسطة محامٍ مختص، على ألا يقر بخطأ أو يفرض التزاماً غير نقدي أو يبرم تسوية دون إبراء كامل للطرف المستفيد إلا بموافقته الكتابية المسبقة. ولا يقل نطاق الحماية بسبب تأخر الإشعار إلا بقدر الضرر المثبت الناشئ عنه. ويحدد البند 3 الحدود والاستبعادات المنطبقة.»

The profile does not define “our data” indemnity scope; this proposal confines it to IP claims from authorised use. Ordinary IP indemnity remains capped; the vendor data-breach indemnity sits above the cap through R2. No enforceability assumption is made about indemnifying regulatory fines. `[review]`

**If Vendor refuses:** Legal Lead; no indemnity fallback is documented.

**F10 — Missing schedules, acceptance and service rights**

**Playbook:** No specific acceptance, service-level, IP-ownership or payment fallback is configured. “Standard terms” cannot be assumed where these essentials are missing. `[review]`

**Contract:**
> “the fleet-analytics platform described in Schedule 1”

and:

> “any milestone in Schedule 2”

**Gap:** Missing incorporated documents. **Legal risk 🟠; business friction 🔴.**

Arts. 45–46 recognise incorporated documents. Good-faith and implied obligations under Art. 95, the defect baseline under Art. 109, and applicable work-contract provisions do not establish the missing uptime targets, delivery dates, or acceptance tests. [Civil Transactions reference](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/civil-transactions-law.md:43>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`.

The sales-law 180-day defect regime in Arts. 338–344 should not automatically be applied to the entire implementation/hosting arrangement without characterising the relevant supply. `[review]`

**Redline R10 — append to clause 1:**

> “The parties shall complete and sign Schedules 1 and 2 before signature of this Agreement. The schedules shall identify deliverables, dependencies, delivery dates, objective acceptance tests, service availability and support commitments, applicable fees, and authorised users. No fees become payable before the schedules are signed.
>
> Vendor warrants that the Services will conform to the agreed specifications and be performed with reasonable skill and care. Customer’s silence, payment or operational testing does not by itself constitute acceptance. Vendor shall correct non-conformities at its cost.
>
> Vendor grants Customer the rights necessary to access and use the platform and deliverables for Customer’s business during the term, and a continuing right to retain and use Customer Data and reports delivered to Customer. Additional deliverable ownership and licence terms shall be stated in Schedule 1.”

**العربية:**
> «يستكمل الطرفان الملحقين 1 و2 ويوقعانهما قبل توقيع هذه الاتفاقية. ويحدد الملحقان المخرجات والمتطلبات السابقة ومواعيد التسليم واختبارات القبول الموضوعية والتزامات إتاحة الخدمة والدعم والرسوم المنطبقة والمستخدمين المصرح لهم. ولا تستحق رسوم قبل توقيع الملحقين.
>
> يضمن المورّد مطابقة الخدمات للمواصفات المتفق عليها وتنفيذها بمهارة وعناية معقولتين. ولا يُعد سكوت العميل أو سداده أو اختباره التشغيلي قبولاً بذاته. ويعالج المورّد أوجه عدم المطابقة على نفقته.
>
> يمنح المورّد العميل الحقوق اللازمة للوصول إلى المنصة والمخرجات واستخدامها لأعمال العميل خلال المدة، وحقاً مستمراً في الاحتفاظ ببيانات العميل والتقارير المسلمة إليه واستخدامها. وتُحدد في الملحق 1 أي أحكام إضافية لملكية المخرجات وترخيصها.»

These are proposed contractual protections; the loaded files do not resolve software-IP ownership formalities. `[no rule in ksa files — verify]` `[review]`

**If Vendor refuses:** Procurement lead and Legal Lead; the incomplete schedules remain a signing blocker.

**F11 — Clause 9: Riyadh courts versus SCCA**

**Playbook:** Preferred “SCCA, Riyadh”; accepted Commercial default: “Saudi Commercial Courts.”

**Contract:**
> “Disputes shall be referred to the competent courts in Riyadh.”

**Gap:** Within accepted fallback, below preference. **Legal risk 🟡; business friction 🟠.**

This is not a foreign-court escalation. Assuming both parties act as merchants in their commercial activities, Art. 16(1) supports commercial-court jurisdiction; Art. 17 addresses territorial venue. [Commercial Courts reference](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/commercial-courts-law.md:37>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`; Art. 16 `[BOE — Arabic]` `[review]`.

If Customer is not acting as a merchant, the reference flags a statutory/draft-regulation threshold discrepancy; no non-merchant routing conclusion is made.

**Redline R11 — retain Saudi governing law; replace the disputes sentence:**

> “Any dispute arising out of or relating to this Agreement, including its existence, validity, performance or termination, shall be finally resolved by arbitration administered by the Saudi Center for Commercial Arbitration under its Arbitration Rules. The seat shall be Riyadh, Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. The tribunal shall consist of one arbitrator meeting the qualifications required by Saudi law. The language of arbitration shall be English. Applications to a competent court for interim protection do not waive this agreement to arbitrate.”

**العربية:**
> «يُفصل نهائياً في أي نزاع ينشأ عن هذه الاتفاقية أو يتعلق بها، بما في ذلك وجودها أو صحتها أو تنفيذها أو انتهاؤها، عن طريق تحكيم يديره المركز السعودي للتحكيم التجاري وفق قواعد التحكيم لديه. ويكون مقر التحكيم الرياض، المملكة العربية السعودية. وتتكون هيئة التحكيم من محكم واحد مستوفٍ للمؤهلات المطلوبة نظاماً. وتكون لغة التحكيم الإنجليزية. ولا يُعد طلب الحماية الوقتية من المحكمة المختصة تنازلاً عن اتفاق التحكيم.»

Writing, capacity, seat and language are addressed by Arbitration Law Arts. 9–10, 14 and 28–29. [Arbitration reference](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/arbitration-law.md:35>) `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`.

**If Vendor refuses:** Retain competent Riyadh commercial courts as the accepted fallback. The overall agreement still needs escalation for other findings.

**F12 — Clause 10: English-only agreement**

**Playbook:** “English; Arabic prevails in a Saudi forum.”

**Contract:**
> “This Agreement is executed in English only.”

**Gap:** Weaker than the adopted language position. **Legal risk 🟡; business friction 🟡.**

English-only execution is not established to be invalid. The adopted Arabic-prevails convention is a practice position, not a statutory rule proved by the loaded files. [Playbook defaults, contract-language row](</Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant/references/jurisdictions/ksa/playbook-defaults.md>) `[model knowledge — verify]`. The loaded Commercial Courts file does not establish a general contract-language validity rule. `[no rule in ksa files — verify]`

**Redline R12 — replace clause 10:**

> “This Agreement, including its schedules, is executed in English and Arabic. In the event of inconsistency, the Arabic text prevails. The language of arbitration, if applicable, remains English as provided in clause 9.”

